window.WHS_COURSE = {"version":"1.0.0","title":"Promise & Practice","school":"Waukegan High School","brand":"Shadow Dog Studios","asOf":"October 21, 2026","lessons":[{"id":"L01","number":1,"title":"A nation at war","period":"1861–1865","section":"Reading","skill":"Locate explicit evidence","era":0,"stage":"Foundations","minutes":40,"story":["When the Civil War began in 1861, preserving the Union was the federal government's central stated goal. The conflict also forced Americans to confront the institution of slavery. Enslaved people escaped to Union lines, pressed for freedom, and helped change what Union victory would mean. Decisions made far from Washington could influence the choices of national leaders.","Lincoln issued the final Emancipation Proclamation on January 1, 1863. It declared freedom for enslaved people in designated areas in rebellion. It did not end slavery in the loyal border states. Its effect depended in part on the advance of Union forces. The proclamation also opened the way for Black men to serve in the Union Army and Navy.","These limits matter. A reader who says the document immediately ended slavery everywhere has moved beyond the evidence. A reader who calls it meaningless also misses evidence: it changed federal policy and strengthened the connection between Union victory and emancipation. Good historical reading requires noticing both a claim and its boundaries."],"steps":["Read the question and name the task: a detail, a main idea, or an inference.","Find the sentence that directly answers it.","Restate that sentence in your own words before checking the choices.","Reject a choice that adds a claim the text never makes."],"worked":{"prompt":"A question asks whether the proclamation freed people in every state immediately. What evidence should you use?","steps":["Locate paragraph 2: it specifies areas in rebellion and excludes loyal border states.","The word every makes the proposed claim broader than the passage.","Choose the answer that preserves the document’s geographic limits."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which phrase most directly limits the proclamation’s reach?","choices":["Designated areas in rebellion","All people within Union lines","All states at the same time","Every part of the nation"],"answer":0,"why":"Paragraph 2 specifies designated areas in rebellion. A limit tells you where a claim applies.","skill":"Locate explicit evidence","id":"L01-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"According to the passage, what did the proclamation make possible?","choices":["Immediate freedom in every loyal state","A peace agreement with the Confederacy","The immediate end of every wartime dispute","Black military service in Union forces"],"answer":3,"why":"Paragraph 2 identifies Army and Navy service. The other changes are not stated.","skill":"Locate explicit evidence","id":"L01-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which detail contradicts the claim that emancipation happened everywhere at once?","choices":["Loyal border states were excluded.","Union soldiers advanced during the war.","Washington made national decisions.","The war began during 1861."],"answer":0,"why":"An exclusion is direct evidence against everywhere. The date alone does not establish the geographic limit.","skill":"Locate explicit evidence","id":"L01-questions-2"},{"stem":"Why does the author discuss people escaping to Union lines?","choices":["To list every cause of the Civil War","To show action beyond Washington","To argue that leaders had no influence","To identify the war’s final battle"],"answer":1,"why":"Paragraph 1 connects the actions of enslaved people to changes in policy. It does not erase the role of leaders.","skill":"Locate explicit evidence","id":"L01-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which statement stays within the passage’s evidence?","choices":["The proclamation brought the war to an immediate end.","The proclamation applied identically in every state.","Federal policy connected victory and emancipation.","The proclamation ended all resistance to freedom."],"answer":2,"why":"The final paragraph describes a stronger connection between victory and emancipation, with limits still present.","skill":"Locate explicit evidence","id":"L01-questions-4"},{"stem":"In this passage, the word boundaries most nearly means:","choices":["Borders of a battlefield","Limits on a claim","Names on a military roster","Reasons to avoid all documents"],"answer":1,"why":"The final paragraph concerns what the evidence allows a reader to claim, not the physical edge of a battlefield.","skill":"Locate explicit evidence","id":"L01-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which question can this passage answer directly?","choices":["How many people read it on its first day?","Which soldier first carried a printed copy?","How much did each printed copy cost?","What limits did the proclamation have?"],"answer":3,"why":"The text supplies the document’s limits. It provides none of the quantities or identities in the other choices.","skill":"Locate explicit evidence","id":"L01-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new document applies to three named counties. Which conclusion is justified?","choices":["It cannot affect anyone in those counties.","It permanently settles every local conflict.","It must apply in every nearby state.","Its stated reach includes those counties."],"answer":3,"why":"A bounded claim respects the document’s actual scope. Do not enlarge or erase that scope.","skill":"Locate explicit evidence","id":"L01-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H01"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Busca la oración que responde directamente. Conserva los límites: algunas regiones no significa todas las regiones. No uses lo que recuerdas de historia para reemplazar la evidencia del texto."},{"id":"L02","number":2,"title":"Emancipation in complete sentences","period":"1863–1865","section":"English","skill":"Recognize complete sentences","era":0,"stage":"Foundations","minutes":40,"story":["A historical explanation can contain accurate facts and still be difficult to follow. A sentence needs a subject and a complete verb, and it must express a complete thought. A dependent clause begins a thought that must be connected to another clause.","Consider the statement: Although the proclamation had limits. A reader is left waiting. Although signals a relationship, but the statement does not complete it. The thought becomes complete when a second clause is added: Although the proclamation had limits, it changed the purpose of the war.","The Thirteenth Amendment abolished slavery in the United States, with the exception stated in its punishment clause. Explaining that change accurately requires clear sentences. Strong editing preserves historical meaning while repairing the structure. It does not replace a precise statement with a broader, inaccurate one."],"steps":["Find who or what the sentence is about.","Find the complete verb attached to that subject.","Check whether a word such as although, because, or when makes the clause dependent.","Connect a dependent clause to a complete thought; do not merely add a period."],"worked":{"prompt":"Repair: Because the document changed federal policy.","steps":["Because introduces a dependent clause.","Add a complete thought: Because the document changed federal policy, Union victory carried a new meaning.","Check that both parts fit together and that the sentence preserves the original meaning."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which is a complete sentence?","choices":["Because of the amendment to the Constitution.","The amendment changing the Constitution.","The amendment changed the Constitution.","Although the amendment changed the Constitution."],"answer":2,"why":"The subject amendment has the complete verb changed and expresses a complete thought.","skill":"Recognize complete sentences","id":"L02-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Which revision completes this fragment: Although the proclamation had limits.","choices":["Although the proclamation with its limits.","Although the proclamation had limits, it changed policy.","The proclamation although having limits.","Although the proclamation had limits and."],"answer":1,"why":"The independent clause it changed policy completes the relationship introduced by although.","skill":"Recognize complete sentences","id":"L02-questions-1"},{"stem":"In The soldiers carried the news, which word is the subject?","choices":["Carried","Soldiers","The","News"],"answer":1,"why":"Soldiers names who performed the action. Carried is the verb; news receives the action.","skill":"Recognize complete sentences","id":"L02-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which group of words is a dependent clause?","choices":["Congress proposed the amendment","The states considered the proposal","When the amendment was ratified","The amendment was ratified"],"answer":2,"why":"When makes the clause depend on another complete thought.","skill":"Recognize complete sentences","id":"L02-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which revision preserves meaning and repairs the fragment: The amendment, a major constitutional change.","choices":["The amendment was a major constitutional change.","The amendment being a major constitutional change.","The amendment, and a major constitutional change.","A major constitutional change of the amendment."],"answer":0,"why":"Adding was provides a complete verb for the subject amendment.","skill":"Recognize complete sentences","id":"L02-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which choice joins these ideas into one complete sentence: The war ended. The work of freedom continued.","choices":["The war ended, the work of freedom continued.","Although the war ended. The work of freedom continued.","The war ended, but the work of freedom continued.","The war ending but the work of freedom continued."],"answer":2,"why":"A comma and the coordinating conjunction but join the independent clauses while preserving contrast.","skill":"Recognize complete sentences","id":"L02-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which sentence is complete as written?","choices":["The proposal that Congress debated before the vote.","Congress, debating the proposal before the vote.","Congress debated the proposal before the vote.","Before Congress debated the proposal."],"answer":2,"why":"Congress debated is an independent clause. The others are incomplete constructions.","skill":"Recognize complete sentences","id":"L02-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"Choose a complete sentence about a later amendment.","choices":["The states ratifying the amendment.","The states ratified the amendment.","After the states ratified the amendment.","Because of the states and the amendment."],"answer":1,"why":"A subject, a finite verb, and a complete thought are present in the first option.","skill":"Recognize complete sentences","id":"L02-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H01","H02"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Una oración completa necesita sujeto, verbo completo y una idea completa. Palabras como aunque, porque o cuando pueden crear una cláusula dependiente que necesita otra idea."},{"id":"L03","number":3,"title":"Supplying an army","period":"1861–1865","section":"Mathematics","skill":"Fractions decimals and percentages","era":0,"stage":"Foundations","minutes":40,"story":["The Civil War required armies to move food, clothing, and equipment across long distances. A supply officer had to compare quantities, keep records, and recognize whether a delivery met a stated need. Arithmetic could connect a number in a ledger to a practical decision.","In this original practice scenario, a depot plans a shipment of 800 supply bundles. A record divides the shipment among several units. The quantities in the problems are invented for learning; they are not an actual Civil War supply ledger. Historical context gives the arithmetic a purpose without making the numbers historical evidence.","Fractions, decimals, and percentages are different representations of a part of a whole. Before calculating, identify the whole. One quarter of a shipment is not one quarter of every army's supplies. Units matter just as much as arithmetic."],"steps":["Name the whole and write its units.","Convert a percent to a decimal by dividing by 100.","Multiply the whole by the decimal to find a part.","Check whether the answer is a sensible part of the original whole."],"worked":{"prompt":"A practice depot has 600 bundles and sends 25% to one unit. How many bundles does it send?","steps":["25% = 0.25 = one quarter.","600 × 0.25 = 150 bundles.","One quarter must be smaller than the whole; 150 fits."]},"guided":{"stem":"What decimal is equal to 25%?","choices":["2.5","0.25","0.025","25"],"answer":1,"why":"Percent means per hundred. 25 ÷ 100 = 0.25.","skill":"Fractions decimals and percentages","id":"L03-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"A practice shipment contains 800 bundles. How many is 1/4 of the shipment?","choices":["100 bundles","600 bundles","200 bundles","400 bundles"],"answer":2,"why":"Divide 800 by 4. The result is 200 bundles.","skill":"Fractions decimals and percentages","id":"L03-questions-1"},{"stem":"What percent of 800 bundles is a delivery of 600 bundles?","choices":["60%","75%","25%","80%"],"answer":1,"why":"600 ÷ 800 = 0.75; multiply by 100 to write 75%.","skill":"Fractions decimals and percentages","id":"L03-questions-2"},{"stem":"A depot receives 150 of 500 planned blankets. What fraction of the plan arrived?","choices":["3/5","1/3","1/5","3/10"],"answer":3,"why":"150/500 simplifies to 3/10 after dividing numerator and denominator by 50.","skill":"Fractions decimals and percentages","id":"L03-questions-3"},{"stem":"A practice unit uses 30% of 900 meal packs. How many remain?","choices":["630 meal packs","870 meal packs","300 meal packs","270 meal packs"],"answer":0,"why":"The remaining percentage is 70%. 900 × 0.70 = 630.","skill":"Fractions decimals and percentages","id":"L03-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which amount equals 0.4 of a 250-item practice shipment?","choices":["100 items","150 items","40 items","10 items"],"answer":0,"why":"0.4 × 250 = 100. A decimal part can be multiplied directly by the whole.","skill":"Fractions decimals and percentages","id":"L03-questions-5"},{"stem":"An estimate is needed for 49% of 802 bundles. Which estimate is most reasonable?","choices":["1,600 bundles","40 bundles","400 bundles","800 bundles"],"answer":2,"why":"49% is close to one half and 802 is close to 800; one half of 800 is 400.","skill":"Fractions decimals and percentages","id":"L03-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"In a new practice ledger, 18 of 60 crates are marked urgent. What percent is urgent?","choices":["30%","18%","60%","3%"],"answer":0,"why":"18/60 = 0.3 = 30%. The whole is 60 crates.","skill":"Fractions decimals and percentages","id":"L03-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H01"],"visual":{"kind":"table","title":"Practice shipment plan","caption":"Invented classroom quantities; not a historical ledger.","headers":["Destination","Bundles"],"rows":[["Unit A",200],["Unit B",300],["Unit C",300]]},"writing":null,"es":"Identifica el total. Para hallar un porcentaje de una cantidad, convierte el porcentaje a decimal y multiplica. Revisa las unidades y estima si el resultado tiene sentido."},{"id":"L04","number":4,"title":"Signals across a battlefield","period":"1861–1865","section":"Science","skill":"Read a data table","era":0,"stage":"Foundations","minutes":40,"story":["Telegraph networks helped move messages during the Civil War. Electrical signals allowed information to travel faster than a messenger could ride, though a message still depended on equipment, power, and people who could interpret the code.","Imagine a classroom model of an electromagnet used to explore a related principle. Students wrap different numbers of wire turns around identical metal cores, use the same battery, and measure the number of small steel clips lifted. The model is a modern learning activity, not a report of a wartime experiment. Its measurements are invented practice data.","The table lets a reader compare measured results. It does not establish what happened on every battlefield. Reading scientific information begins with its labels, its units, and its limits."],"steps":["Read the title and both column headings.","Find the row for the condition named in the question.","Match a value to its unit or measured quantity.","Describe the pattern only across the tested conditions."],"worked":{"prompt":"Using the table, how many clips were lifted with 20 wire turns?","steps":["Find 20 in the wire-turns column.","Read across that row rather than down the other column.","The matching result is 8 clips."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which quantity did the students change between trials?","choices":["Type of metal core","Size of the steel clips","Identity of the battery","Number of wire turns"],"answer":3,"why":"The scenario varies the turns while describing the core and battery as unchanged.","skill":"Read a data table","id":"L04-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"At 30 wire turns, how many clips were lifted?","choices":["30 clips","8 clips","16 clips","12 clips"],"answer":3,"why":"Read across the row for 30 turns. Its measured result is 12 clips.","skill":"Read a data table","id":"L04-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which tested condition lifted the most clips?","choices":["30 wire turns","40 wire turns","10 wire turns","20 wire turns"],"answer":1,"why":"The largest measured value is 16 clips, paired with 40 turns.","skill":"Read a data table","id":"L04-questions-2"},{"stem":"How did the measured result change from 10 to 20 turns?","choices":["It increased by 4 clips.","It stayed at 4 clips.","It increased by 10 clips.","It decreased by 4 clips."],"answer":0,"why":"The measurements are 4 and 8 clips. Their difference is 8 − 4 = 4.","skill":"Read a data table","id":"L04-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which trend is supported by all four rows?","choices":["More turns accompanied more clips lifted.","The battery changed after each trial.","Every number of turns lifted the same count.","More turns accompanied fewer clips lifted."],"answer":0,"why":"Each increase in tested turns is paired with a larger measured clip count. Battery changes are not reported.","skill":"Read a data table","id":"L04-questions-4"},{"stem":"What is the measured outcome in this activity?","choices":["Date of the trial","Length of the war","Wire color","Clips lifted"],"answer":3,"why":"The second table column records the outcome: the count of clips lifted.","skill":"Read a data table","id":"L04-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which claim goes beyond the available evidence?","choices":["The table includes four tested settings.","The 40-turn setting lifted the most clips.","The 20-turn model lifted eight clips.","Any battery will produce the same results."],"answer":3,"why":"Only the same battery was used. The experiment does not compare batteries.","skill":"Read a data table","id":"L04-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new table lists 5, 10, and 15 turns with 2, 4, and 6 clips. How many clips match 10 turns?","choices":["6 clips","2 clips","4 clips","10 clips"],"answer":2,"why":"Match the condition in the first column to the result in the same row.","skill":"Read a data table","id":"L04-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H17"],"visual":{"kind":"line","title":"Electromagnet classroom model","caption":"Invented practice measurements. Same battery and metal core in every trial.","headers":["Wire turns","Clips lifted"],"rows":[[10,4],[20,8],[30,12],[40,16]]},"writing":null,"es":"Lee los encabezados antes de los números. Busca la condición en una columna y lee el resultado en la misma fila. Una tabla no demuestra cosas que no fueron investigadas."},{"id":"L05","number":5,"title":"Freedom citizenship and a central idea","period":"1865–1877","section":"Reading","skill":"Identify a main idea","era":0,"stage":"Foundations","minutes":40,"story":["Reconstruction raised a question that victory in battle could not settle by itself: what would freedom mean in everyday life? Formerly enslaved people built schools, reunited families, negotiated work, and sought political influence. Their actions made freedom a practical project as well as a legal status.","Constitutional changes created a new framework. The Thirteenth Amendment abolished slavery, subject to its punishment exception. The Fourteenth Amendment established national citizenship and protections involving due process and equal protection. The Fifteenth barred denial of voting rights on account of race, color, or previous servitude.","Yet a promise in law did not enforce itself. Organized violence and discriminatory practices restricted the rights the amendments described. Federal action could defend rights, but protection was uneven and contested. The result was neither a simple story of complete success nor a story in which nothing changed.","Reconstruction transformed the Constitution while leaving the struggle over enforcement unfinished. To understand the period, a reader must connect the broad legal changes with the people who tried to make those changes real."],"steps":["Summarize each paragraph in a few words.","Find the idea that connects those summaries.","Choose an answer broad enough for the whole passage but narrow enough to stay supported.","Reject a detail that is true but covers only one paragraph."],"worked":{"prompt":"Is schools opened a main idea for the entire passage?","steps":["Schools are one example in paragraph 1.","The passage also discusses constitutional change, resistance, and enforcement.","A whole-passage summary must connect those parts."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which summary best covers the whole passage?","choices":["Schools were the only important Reconstruction institution.","Legal change required a continuing fight for enforcement.","Federal authorities had no role in protecting any rights.","Constitutional amendments immediately ended racial violence."],"answer":1,"why":"All four paragraphs connect expanded legal rights with the work and conflict involved in making them effective.","skill":"Identify a main idea","id":"L05-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Why does paragraph 1 list family work education and politics?","choices":["To rank one group of people above another","To show constitutional law was irrelevant","To show freedom had everyday dimensions","To explain the military plan for a final battle"],"answer":2,"why":"The examples show what freedom involved in daily life, which supports the passage’s broader argument.","skill":"Identify a main idea","id":"L05-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which statement describes a supporting detail rather than the main idea?","choices":["Legal rights required practical protection.","Constitutional change and enforcement were connected.","Reconstruction changed law without ending the struggle.","The Fourteenth Amendment established national citizenship."],"answer":3,"why":"The citizenship provision supports the larger point; it does not summarize all the passage’s parts.","skill":"Identify a main idea","id":"L05-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which word best describes the relationship between paragraphs 2 and 3?","choices":["Unrelatedness","Chronological reversal","Repetition","Contrast"],"answer":3,"why":"Paragraph 2 describes legal promises, while paragraph 3 describes obstacles to their enforcement.","skill":"Identify a main idea","id":"L05-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which title best fits the author’s focus?","choices":["New Rights and Unfinished Enforcement","The Final Battlefield of the Civil War","The History of One Reconstruction School","A Complete End to All Political Conflict"],"answer":0,"why":"The title captures both constitutional change and the continued contest over real protection.","skill":"Identify a main idea","id":"L05-questions-4"},{"stem":"What does the last paragraph mainly do?","choices":["Connect the passage’s major ideas","Describe an event before the Civil War","Replace the earlier constitutional facts","Introduce an unrelated military topic"],"answer":0,"why":"It brings legal transformation and practical struggle together rather than introducing a new topic.","skill":"Identify a main idea","id":"L05-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which summary is too broad for this passage?","choices":["Reconstruction changed constitutional protections.","All conflicts in American history concern voting.","Resistance complicated the enforcement of rights.","People worked to give freedom practical meaning."],"answer":1,"why":"The passage concerns Reconstruction, not every conflict in all of American history.","skill":"Identify a main idea","id":"L05-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new passage describes a law, resistance, and a campaign to enforce it. Which summary covers all three?","choices":["All laws always enforce themselves.","The law had exactly three paragraphs.","The campaign changed nothing anywhere.","A legal promise faced obstacles in practice."],"answer":3,"why":"A useful main idea joins the law with both resistance and enforcement.","skill":"Identify a main idea","id":"L05-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H02","H16"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L06","number":6,"title":"An argument about Reconstruction","period":"1865–1877","section":"Writing","skill":"Build a claim and explain support","era":0,"stage":"Foundations","minutes":40,"story":["A claim tells the reader what you argue. Evidence or an example gives the reader something concrete to consider. Reasoning explains how that example supports the claim. Without reasoning, an essay can become a list of facts.","Reconstruction offers a way to practice this connection. New constitutional protections could change what governments were allowed to do. Schools and community organizations could change how people exercised opportunity. Protection against violence could affect whether rights were usable at all. These actions addressed related problems, but they were not interchangeable.","An argument can choose a priority while recognizing that other actions mattered. For example, a writer might argue that enforcement deserves priority because a right that cannot be used has limited practical value. The next task is to explain a specific example rather than simply repeat the word enforcement."],"steps":["Answer the prompt with a clear position.","Choose a specific example that supports that position.","Explain the connection using because or this matters because.","Address another reasonable perspective without pretending your own choice solves everything."],"worked":{"prompt":"Claim: Protecting the exercise of rights should be a government priority. How could you develop it?","steps":["Name an example: intimidation can prevent eligible people from voting.","Explain the link: protection allows a legal right to be exercised.","Recognize a limit: education and opportunity still require attention."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which sentence makes a claim?","choices":["Protection of rights should be the first priority.","The amendment was ratified during Reconstruction.","This paragraph will contain several sentences.","Many people discussed the future after the war."],"answer":0,"why":"The sentence takes a position that can be supported and debated.","skill":"Build a claim and explain support","id":"L06-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Which detail most directly supports a claim about enforcement?","choices":["A speech can use several paragraphs.","A historian can write about different decades.","A school building can have several classrooms.","Intimidation can prevent eligible voters from voting."],"answer":3,"why":"The example connects a barrier to using a right with the need for protection.","skill":"Build a claim and explain support","id":"L06-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which sentence supplies reasoning rather than just an example?","choices":["An eligible voter faced intimidation.","The government ratified an amendment.","A community organized a school.","Protection matters because a right must be usable."],"answer":3,"why":"Reasoning explains why the examples support the claim.","skill":"Build a claim and explain support","id":"L06-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which revision makes this claim more precise: Everything must get better?","choices":["The future should contain some improvements.","Government should prioritize protecting constitutional rights.","People should discuss everything more often.","All things should become different somehow."],"answer":1,"why":"The revision identifies an actor and a specific priority that can be argued.","skill":"Build a claim and explain support","id":"L06-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which response fairly addresses an education-first perspective?","choices":["Schools have never affected any community.","Education and voting are exactly the same action.","Education expands opportunity, but students also need protected rights.","Anyone favoring education must oppose freedom."],"answer":2,"why":"It acknowledges the value of the other view and explains how it relates to the writer’s position.","skill":"Build a claim and explain support","id":"L06-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which organization is clearest for a short argument?","choices":["Claim, example, explanation, other view","Example, unexplained claim, unrelated date","Question, repeated question, repeated question","Unrelated fact, new topic, new topic, ending"],"answer":0,"why":"This structure helps the reader follow a position and the reasons supporting it.","skill":"Build a claim and explain support","id":"L06-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which addition most improves an unexplained example?","choices":["Add a date unrelated to the example.","Explain why it supports the position.","Repeat the example in capital letters.","Remove the claim from the paragraph."],"answer":1,"why":"The missing connection is reasoning; repetition or unrelated detail cannot supply it.","skill":"Build a claim and explain support","id":"L06-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new argument favors local history museums. Which sentence supplies reasoning?","choices":["This essay is about a local museum.","They let residents examine evidence about shared changes.","The museum has a door at its entrance.","The building contains several rooms."],"answer":1,"why":"The sentence explains a benefit that could support a claim about museums.","skill":"Build a claim and explain support","id":"L06-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H02","H16"],"visual":null,"writing":{"issue":"After a major expansion of legal rights, which action should receive the greatest priority?","perspectives":["Protect the rights through consistent public enforcement.","Expand education so people can use new opportunities.","Strengthen local organizations that can respond to community needs."],"minWords":60,"directions":"Develop your own position and explain its relationship to at least one other perspective. Use a specific example and explain how it supports your reasoning. These are original practice prompts.","rubric":["Ideas and analysis","Development and support","Organization","Language use and conventions"]},"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L07","number":7,"title":"Railroads rates and proportion","period":"1869–1890","section":"Mathematics","skill":"Use ratios and unit rates","era":1,"stage":"Foundations","minutes":40,"story":["The transcontinental railroad linked existing rail networks across the continent in 1869. Travel and trade changed, but the railroad also depended on labor, federal support, and the taking of Indigenous lands. A transportation improvement could create opportunities while placing costs on different communities.","This lesson uses an invented freight scenario. A planner compares the number of crates moved with the time required. A rate combines two quantities with different units. A unit rate describes the amount per one unit, such as crates per hour. A proportional model assumes that the rate remains constant.","That assumption must be stated. Actual railroads could face weather, equipment failures, and changing demand. A classroom proportion is a model for reasoning, not a promise that every real journey followed a constant rate."],"steps":["Write the two quantities with their units.","Divide to find the amount per one unit.","Use that rate only when the problem states or assumes a constant relationship.","Check that the units in the answer match the question."],"worked":{"prompt":"A practice crew moves 84 crates in 7 hours at a constant rate. How many in 4 hours?","steps":["84 crates ÷ 7 hours = 12 crates per hour.","12 × 4 = 48 crates.","The answer is crates, not hours."]},"guided":{"stem":"A practice rail crew loads 72 crates in 6 hours. What is the unit rate?","choices":["18 crates per hour","432 crates per hour","12 crates per hour","6 crates per hour"],"answer":2,"why":"72 ÷ 6 = 12 crates per hour.","skill":"Use ratios and unit rates","id":"L07-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"At a constant rate of 15 crates per hour, how many crates are loaded in 4 hours?","choices":["60 crates","19 crates","11 crates","45 crates"],"answer":0,"why":"Multiply 15 crates/hour by 4 hours to get 60 crates.","skill":"Use ratios and unit rates","id":"L07-questions-1"},{"stem":"A model uses 3 freight cars for 90 crates. At the same ratio, how many cars carry 150 crates?","choices":["6 cars","9 cars","3 cars","5 cars"],"answer":3,"why":"Each car carries 30 crates. 150 ÷ 30 = 5 cars.","skill":"Use ratios and unit rates","id":"L07-questions-2"},{"stem":"A practice map scale is 1 inch to 40 miles. What distance does 3.5 inches represent?","choices":["120 miles","43.5 miles","11.4 miles","140 miles"],"answer":3,"why":"3.5 × 40 = 140 miles. The map distance is multiplied by the scale factor.","skill":"Use ratios and unit rates","id":"L07-questions-3"},{"stem":"A practice train travels 180 miles in 3 hours. At constant speed, how long for 300 miles?","choices":["3 hours","5 hours","6 hours","9 hours"],"answer":1,"why":"180/3 = 60 miles per hour. 300/60 = 5 hours.","skill":"Use ratios and unit rates","id":"L07-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which pair of quantities has the same ratio as 4 workers to 12 tools?","choices":["10 workers to 12 tools","6 workers to 12 tools","6 workers to 18 tools","8 workers to 20 tools"],"answer":2,"why":"Both ratios simplify to 1 worker to 3 tools.","skill":"Use ratios and unit rates","id":"L07-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which assumption is necessary to use the same unit rate for every hour?","choices":["The loading rate stays constant.","The crew changes size every hour.","The number of crates is always zero.","The schedule has no stated units."],"answer":0,"why":"A proportional model relies on a constant ratio. Changing the operating conditions may invalidate it.","skill":"Use ratios and unit rates","id":"L07-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new practice crew unloads 96 crates in 8 hours. At that rate, how many in 5 hours?","choices":["60 crates","120 crates","80 crates","40 crates"],"answer":0,"why":"96/8 = 12 crates per hour; 12 × 5 = 60 crates.","skill":"Use ratios and unit rates","id":"L07-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H18"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Una tasa unitaria expresa una cantidad por una unidad: cajas por hora. Divide primero. Después multiplica por el número de horas. Usa la proporción solo si la tasa se mantiene constante."},{"id":"L08","number":8,"title":"Industry and sentence boundaries","period":"1880–1900","section":"English","skill":"Punctuate independent clauses","era":1,"stage":"Foundations","minutes":40,"story":["Industrial growth brought large factories, new fortunes, and conflicts over power at work. At Pullman, near Chicago, workers faced wage cuts during a depression while rents in the company town remained a source of tension. A local strike in 1894 became part of a national railroad conflict.","Explaining a conflict requires showing relationships between ideas. Consider: Workers protested, rail service was disrupted. Both parts are complete sentences. A comma alone does not join them correctly. The writer can use a period, a semicolon, or a comma with a coordinating conjunction, depending on the intended relationship.","Punctuation is not decoration. It helps readers see where one complete thought ends and how another relates to it. The best revision fixes the boundary while keeping the account's meaning intact."],"steps":["Check whether each side can stand as a complete sentence.","If both can, a comma alone is insufficient.","Use a period, a semicolon, or a comma plus a coordinating conjunction.","Choose a connector that preserves the logical relationship."],"worked":{"prompt":"Repair: The workers organized, the conflict widened.","steps":["Both clauses are independent.","The workers organized; the conflict widened is correctly joined with a semicolon.","A comma plus and would also be grammatical."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which correctly joins two independent clauses?","choices":["Workers organized, the conflict spread.","Workers organized the conflict, spread.","Workers organized; and the conflict spread.","Workers organized; the conflict spread."],"answer":3,"why":"A semicolon can join closely related independent clauses. A comma alone creates a comma splice.","skill":"Punctuate independent clauses","id":"L08-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Choose the correct revision: Wages fell, rents remained a concern.","choices":["Wages fell but, rents remained a concern.","Wages fell, but rents remained a concern.","Wages fell, because, rents remained a concern.","Wages fell, rents, remained a concern."],"answer":1,"why":"A comma and but correctly join the complete clauses and express contrast.","skill":"Punctuate independent clauses","id":"L08-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which choice correctly uses a period?","choices":["The strike began the. Dispute widened.","The strike began. The dispute widened.","The strike. Began the dispute widened.","The strike began. And because the dispute."],"answer":1,"why":"Each side of the period is a complete sentence.","skill":"Punctuate independent clauses","id":"L08-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which choice is a comma splice?","choices":["The factory closed; the workers waited.","The factory closed. The workers waited.","The factory closed, the workers waited.","The factory closed, and the workers waited."],"answer":2,"why":"Two independent clauses are connected by only a comma.","skill":"Punctuate independent clauses","id":"L08-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which connector best shows contrast?","choices":["The town offered parks, so workers lacked control.","The town offered parks, or workers lacked control.","The town offered parks, but workers lacked control.","The town offered parks, for workers lacked control."],"answer":2,"why":"But signals contrast between an amenity and a limit on workers’ power.","skill":"Punctuate independent clauses","id":"L08-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which sentence uses a dependent clause correctly?","choices":["When the conflict spread, and officials responded.","When the conflict spread. Officials responded.","When the conflict spread, officials responded.","When the conflict spread; officials responded."],"answer":2,"why":"The dependent opening clause is followed by a comma and an independent clause.","skill":"Punctuate independent clauses","id":"L08-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which punctuation can join complete clauses without adding a word?","choices":["A semicolon","A pair of quotation marks","A comma by itself","An apostrophe"],"answer":0,"why":"A semicolon joins related independent clauses. A comma by itself cannot.","skill":"Punctuate independent clauses","id":"L08-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"Edit: Reformers gathered evidence, lawmakers debated it.","choices":["Reformers gathered evidence because. Lawmakers debated it.","Reformers gathered, evidence lawmakers debated it.","Reformers gathered evidence, and lawmakers debated it.","Reformers gathered evidence, lawmakers, debated it."],"answer":2,"why":"A comma and coordinating conjunction join the two complete thoughts.","skill":"Punctuate independent clauses","id":"L08-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H03"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L09","number":9,"title":"Neighbors on Halsted Street","period":"1889–1910","section":"Reading","skill":"Use vocabulary in context","era":1,"stage":"Foundations","minutes":40,"story":["In 1889 Jane Addams and Ellen Gates Starr established Hull House in Chicago. Settlement workers lived in the neighborhood rather than offering help only from a distance. Clubs, classes, and services brought people into regular contact with one another.","Addams came to see that individual assistance could not remove every obstacle facing her neighbors. Low wages, dangerous work, and poor urban conditions called for public investigation and reform. Gathering information about a neighborhood could make an argument more concrete than a general expression of sympathy.","Her approach was often conciliatory. She preferred bringing opposing groups into conversation to simply denouncing one side. That preference did not mean she refused to take positions. It meant she sought ways to pursue change while keeping a conversation possible.","The word conciliatory becomes clearer because of the sentences around it. A dictionary might supply several meanings or related words. The reader's task is to choose the meaning that fits this particular description of a method."],"steps":["Read the sentence containing the word and the sentences around it.","Look for a contrast, example, definition, or restatement.","Replace the word with a simple meaning that fits the context.","Test the replacement in the sentence rather than choosing a familiar dictionary meaning."],"worked":{"prompt":"What does conciliatory mean in paragraph 3?","steps":["The next sentence describes conversation rather than denunciation.","A useful replacement is seeking agreement or reducing conflict.","The word describes her method, not an absence of opinions."]},"guided":{"stem":"In paragraph 3, conciliatory most nearly means:","choices":["Acting in secrecy","Avoiding all action","Seeking dialogue","Refusing to listen"],"answer":2,"why":"The contrast with denouncing one side supports a method of dialogue.","skill":"Use vocabulary in context","id":"L09-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"In paragraph 2, concrete most nearly means:","choices":["Specific and supported","Made from building material","Impossible to change","Physically heavy to carry"],"answer":0,"why":"The sentence contrasts gathered information with general sympathy. It concerns specificity, not construction.","skill":"Use vocabulary in context","id":"L09-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which nearby detail best helps explain settlement in paragraph 1?","choices":["Addams had a first name.","Workers lived in the neighborhood.","Several dates appear in the account.","Chicago appears in the sentence."],"answer":1,"why":"Living among neighbors explains the settlement method described by the passage.","skill":"Use vocabulary in context","id":"L09-questions-2"},{"stem":"In paragraph 2, obstacles most nearly means:","choices":["Reasons to reject evidence","Objects in a classroom","Barriers to improvement","Streets in a city map"],"answer":2,"why":"The examples of low wages and unsafe work are barriers affecting neighbors’ lives.","skill":"Use vocabulary in context","id":"L09-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which interpretation of conciliatory conflicts with the passage?","choices":["Trying to keep conversation possible","Bringing opposing groups together","Seeking change through dialogue","Having no position on an issue"],"answer":3,"why":"Paragraph 3 explicitly says the method did not mean refusing to take positions.","skill":"Use vocabulary in context","id":"L09-questions-4"},{"stem":"Why is a familiar definition not always the best answer?","choices":["Every word has only one use.","A dictionary cannot contain real words.","Context never changes a word’s meaning.","Meaning depends on this use."],"answer":3,"why":"A word can have multiple uses. The surrounding sentences select the relevant meaning.","skill":"Use vocabulary in context","id":"L09-questions-5"},{"stem":"In the phrase pursue change, pursue most nearly means:","choices":["Refuse to consider","Work toward","Record a street address","Run physically behind"],"answer":1,"why":"The passage discusses methods of reform, so work toward fits the purpose.","skill":"Use vocabulary in context","id":"L09-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new passage says a reformer was reserved: she listened quietly before speaking. Reserved most nearly means:","choices":["Restrained in manner","Already booked for travel","Completely uninformed","Unable to hear others"],"answer":0,"why":"The explanation about listening quietly points to a restrained manner, not a reservation.","skill":"Use vocabulary in context","id":"L09-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H04"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L10","number":10,"title":"Cities water and a fair comparison","period":"1890–1910","section":"Science","skill":"Identify variables and controls","era":1,"stage":"Foundations","minutes":40,"story":["Rapidly growing cities had to confront the problem of providing water and removing waste. Chicago's early-twentieth-century engineering projects show how a local problem could require large public systems. Scientists and engineers needed evidence about how those systems worked.","In an original classroom model, students compare two filter materials. They pour the same volume of the same cloudy water through equal-size filters. They use the same container shape and measure the cloudiness of the water afterward. Material A produces a reading of 12 units; Material B produces 7 units. A lower reading means clearer water in this model.","The numbers are invented. The activity measures cloudiness only. It does not test whether water is safe to drink. A fair comparison changes the factor being studied while holding other relevant conditions constant. Its conclusion must match the outcome actually measured."],"steps":["Identify the factor deliberately changed: the independent variable.","Identify the measured result: the dependent variable.","List relevant conditions held the same.","Limit the conclusion to what the measurement can show."],"worked":{"prompt":"What is the independent variable in the filter comparison?","steps":["The students deliberately change the filter material.","They measure cloudiness afterward.","The material is the independent variable; cloudiness is the dependent variable."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which variable is deliberately changed?","choices":["Filter material","Initial source of water","Starting water volume","Shape of the container"],"answer":0,"why":"The scenario compares A with B while holding the other listed conditions constant.","skill":"Identify variables and controls","id":"L10-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"What is the dependent variable?","choices":["Cloudiness after filtering","Date of Chicago’s founding","Material label before testing","Name of the researcher"],"answer":0,"why":"The dependent variable is the measured outcome, which is cloudiness.","skill":"Identify variables and controls","id":"L10-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which condition helps make the comparison fair?","choices":["Different starting water sources","Different filter sizes in every trial","Different measures for each material","Equal starting water volumes"],"answer":3,"why":"Equal starting conditions help isolate the effect of the material.","skill":"Identify variables and controls","id":"L10-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which result is supported?","choices":["B removed every harmful organism.","B works best under every possible condition.","A made all water unsafe to drink.","B produced clearer water in this test."],"answer":3,"why":"B had a lower cloudiness reading, but the experiment did not test organisms or all conditions.","skill":"Identify variables and controls","id":"L10-questions-3"},{"stem":"Why repeat the same procedure several times?","choices":["To eliminate the need to record evidence","To ensure every trial has a different goal","To check consistency of the result","To turn a measurement into a historical date"],"answer":2,"why":"Repeated trials help show whether a measured pattern is consistent rather than a single unusual result.","skill":"Identify variables and controls","id":"L10-questions-4"},{"stem":"A student changes the material and the starting cloudiness together. What is the problem?","choices":["A result can never be measured.","Every comparison becomes equally fair.","Two changing factors obscure the cause.","The material becomes the outcome unit."],"answer":2,"why":"With both factors changed, the effect of material cannot be isolated as clearly.","skill":"Identify variables and controls","id":"L10-questions-5"},{"stem":"What additional evidence is needed to evaluate microbial safety?","choices":["A relevant test for microorganisms","The number of letters in each material name","A second label for the same cloudiness result","The researcher’s opinion about the water"],"answer":0,"why":"Cloudiness does not by itself establish the absence of microorganisms. A suitable additional measure is needed.","skill":"Identify variables and controls","id":"L10-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new test compares two wire materials using the same length and battery. Which factor is independent?","choices":["Battery identity","The label on the table","Wire material","Wire length"],"answer":2,"why":"The factor deliberately changed is the independent variable.","skill":"Identify variables and controls","id":"L10-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H19"],"visual":{"kind":"bar","title":"Classroom filter comparison","caption":"Invented practice data. Lower cloudiness means clearer water; safety was not measured.","headers":["Material","Cloudiness units"],"rows":[["A",12],["B",7]]},"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L11","number":11,"title":"The purpose of a paragraph","period":"1890–1917","section":"Reading","skill":"Analyze text structure","era":1,"stage":"Foundations","minutes":40,"story":["Investigative writers of the Progressive Era tried to make hidden conditions visible. Rather than repeat a company's assurances, they gathered documents, observed workplaces, and described the effects of power on everyday life. Their reporting could connect an individual experience to a broader public problem.","Writers did not all use the same form. Jacob Riis combined text with photographs of urban poverty. Ida Tarbell developed a detailed account of Standard Oil's business practices. Upton Sinclair used a novel to portray the world of immigrant workers in Chicago's meatpacking industry. These examples show a range of approaches to public persuasion.","The response to a work could differ from its author's purpose. Sinclair sought concern for workers, while public attention focused strongly on food conditions. A writer could influence public debate without fully controlling what readers took from the work.","The account therefore moves from a general method to examples and then to a limit on that method's power. Understanding its structure means explaining what each part contributes, not merely repeating the topic of each paragraph."],"steps":["Write a brief label for each paragraph’s job.","Distinguish what a paragraph says from why it appears here.","Look for a pattern: claim, example, contrast, cause, limit, or conclusion.","Test whether the proposed function connects the paragraph to the whole."],"worked":{"prompt":"What is paragraph 2 doing?","steps":["Paragraph 1 introduces investigative writing in general.","Paragraph 2 names different methods through examples.","Its job is to demonstrate the range of approaches, not to supply a full biography of each writer."]},"guided":{"stem":"Paragraph 2 primarily provides:","choices":["An unrelated change in time period","Examples of different methods","Instructions for operating a factory","A complete history of one writer"],"answer":1,"why":"Its three examples illustrate the variety introduced in the first paragraph.","skill":"Analyze text structure","id":"L11-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"What is the main function of paragraph 3?","choices":["Repeat the dates in paragraph 1","Qualify the power of a writer","Explain how to print a newspaper","Prove that readers always agree"],"answer":1,"why":"The author introduces a limit: public response may differ from the intended purpose.","skill":"Analyze text structure","id":"L11-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which outline best matches the passage?","choices":["Method, examples, limitation","Biography, battle, treaty","Problem, unrelated list, recipe","Ending, beginning, unrelated ending"],"answer":0,"why":"The final paragraph explicitly describes the movement from method to examples to limits.","skill":"Analyze text structure","id":"L11-questions-2"},{"stem":"Why is Sinclair a useful example in paragraph 3?","choices":["His book had no readers at all.","He never discussed working conditions.","His purpose and reception differed.","He wrote only government reports."],"answer":2,"why":"The difference between his worker-focused purpose and food-focused reception illustrates the limitation.","skill":"Analyze text structure","id":"L11-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which statement describes function rather than content alone?","choices":["The paragraph names Ida Tarbell.","The paragraph contains three names.","The paragraph illustrates a general claim.","The paragraph mentions Chicago."],"answer":2,"why":"Function explains what a part contributes to the larger argument.","skill":"Analyze text structure","id":"L11-questions-4"},{"stem":"Removing paragraph 2 would most weaken the passage’s:","choices":["List of constitutional amendments","Concrete support for the general method","Instructions for reading a thermometer","Description of a Civil War battle"],"answer":1,"why":"The examples make the general description of investigative writing specific.","skill":"Analyze text structure","id":"L11-questions-5"},{"stem":"The final paragraph mainly helps the reader:","choices":["Calculate the writers’ annual incomes","Recognize the organization of the account","Determine which writer was oldest","Identify the first inventor of photography"],"answer":1,"why":"The conclusion names how the account’s parts fit together.","skill":"Analyze text structure","id":"L11-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new paragraph follows a general claim with three detailed cases. Its likely function is to:","choices":["Switch to an unrelated subject","Withdraw every earlier statement","Provide a dictionary pronunciation","Illustrate the claim"],"answer":3,"why":"Specific cases commonly supply examples of a general claim. Always verify the relationship in context.","skill":"Analyze text structure","id":"L11-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H20"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L12","number":12,"title":"Suffrage and a developed example","period":"1913–1920","section":"Writing","skill":"Develop relevant evidence","era":1,"stage":"Foundations","minutes":40,"story":["A persuasive example does more than decorate an argument. It gives a reader a specific situation and explains why that situation supports a position. A familiar name alone is usually not enough.","The campaign for woman suffrage used several approaches, including state-level organizing, lobbying, and public protest. Illinois expanded women's voting rights in 1913, although the law did not give women identical access to every election. Ratification of the Nineteenth Amendment in 1920 prohibited denying the vote on account of sex. Other barriers still prevented many women from exercising that right.","A student arguing that local action can influence national change might use a state campaign as an example. The reasoning must connect the local result to the broader effort, such as its demonstration that political change was possible. It should also acknowledge what the example cannot establish. One successful campaign does not prove that every local effort succeeds."],"steps":["Choose an example directly related to your claim.","Explain the action and the result with enough detail to follow.","State why that result supports the claim.","Acknowledge a limit without abandoning a supported position."],"worked":{"prompt":"How can a state-level suffrage example support a claim about local action?","steps":["Describe the concrete action: organize and win a change in state voting law.","Explain its relevance: the change can demonstrate feasibility and increase political influence.","Keep the claim limited: this does not prove every local campaign succeeds."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which evidence best supports a claim about local campaigns influencing broader change?","choices":["A building had several windows.","A newspaper had a long title.","A politician owned a travel bag.","A state campaign won a change in voting access."],"answer":3,"why":"The example directly concerns an organized campaign and an institutional result.","skill":"Develop relevant evidence","id":"L12-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Which sentence explains rather than merely names evidence?","choices":["A state victory showed that reform could win political support.","Many leaders had different names.","The date in the example is 1913.","Illinois is the example in this paragraph."],"answer":0,"why":"It explains how the example relates to a broader claim about political change.","skill":"Develop relevant evidence","id":"L12-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which claim is appropriately limited?","choices":["Every local campaign always changes the nation.","One example proves all campaigns are identical.","Local campaigns can help build national change.","No national change has any local cause."],"answer":2,"why":"Can leaves room for varied outcomes while supporting a meaningful relationship.","skill":"Develop relevant evidence","id":"L12-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which statement about 1920 matches the passage?","choices":["The amendment ended all debate about citizenship.","Every woman voted immediately in every place.","Ratification removed every racial voting barrier.","Other barriers remained after ratification."],"answer":3,"why":"The passage distinguishes a constitutional prohibition from complete access in practice.","skill":"Develop relevant evidence","id":"L12-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which example needs more development?","choices":["A state victory demonstrated political feasibility.","A campaign linked a clear demand to a specific legal change.","Local organizing created a group with electoral influence.","People protested, and that proves my claim."],"answer":3,"why":"The vague sentence does not identify a situation or explain the connection.","skill":"Develop relevant evidence","id":"L12-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which question best tests whether evidence is relevant?","choices":["How many letters are in the name?","How does it support this claim?","Can I repeat it without explanation?","Does it come at the end of the page?"],"answer":1,"why":"Relevance depends on the relationship between the evidence and the position.","skill":"Develop relevant evidence","id":"L12-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which revision avoids an unsupported absolute?","choices":["Protest can help attract attention.","Protest always guarantees success.","All movements use identical tactics.","Attention never affects any campaign."],"answer":0,"why":"Can is consistent with varied outcomes; the absolute claims require evidence the passage does not provide.","skill":"Develop relevant evidence","id":"L12-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new essay argues that public libraries expand access to learning. Which example is most relevant?","choices":["A visitor owns a red notebook.","A library offers free research assistance.","The library’s roof has a particular color.","The street has an unrelated shop."],"answer":1,"why":"Free research assistance directly connects the institution with access to learning.","skill":"Develop relevant evidence","id":"L12-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H15"],"visual":null,"writing":{"issue":"Which approach can most effectively help a community expand political participation?","perspectives":["Organize locally and pursue changes in local or state policy.","Focus resources on national law and national institutions.","Use public education to change attitudes before pursuing new law."],"minWords":80,"directions":"Develop your own position and explain its relationship to at least one other perspective. Use a specific example and explain how it supports your reasoning. These are original practice prompts.","rubric":["Ideas and analysis","Development and support","Organization","Language use and conventions"]},"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L13","number":13,"title":"Words that agree","period":"1917–1918","section":"English","skill":"Use agreement tense and reference","era":2,"stage":"Apply","minutes":40,"story":["During World War I, posters, speeches, and printed appeals asked Americans to enlist, conserve resources, or support the war effort. Such messages reveal choices about audience and purpose. A historian asks not only what a message says, but also who produced it and what action it seeks.","An editor must make equally careful choices inside a sentence. In The collection of posters shows a repeated theme, the subject is collection, not posters. A phrase between the subject and verb can distract a reader from their grammatical relationship.","Clear reference also matters. A sentence that says They wanted support may leave readers uncertain whether they means officials, poster artists, or soldiers. Precise language helps a historical explanation make its claims without accidental confusion."],"steps":["Identify the actual subject and ignore interrupting phrases for a moment.","Match a singular subject with a singular verb and a plural subject with a plural verb.","Keep verb tense consistent unless the time genuinely changes.","Replace an unclear pronoun with the noun it refers to."],"worked":{"prompt":"Choose the verb: The collection of wartime posters show/shows a pattern.","steps":["The subject is the singular noun collection.","Of wartime posters is a modifying phrase.","Choose shows to agree with collection."]},"guided":{"stem":"Choose the correct verb: The collection of posters ___ an appeal to duty.","choices":["show","were showing","have shown","shows"],"answer":3,"why":"Collection is singular. The plural noun posters in the modifying phrase does not control the verb.","skill":"Use agreement tense and reference","id":"L13-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Choose the correct verb: The posters in the collection ___ several audiences.","choices":["addresses","was addressing","has addressed","address"],"answer":3,"why":"Posters is plural, so address agrees with it.","skill":"Use agreement tense and reference","id":"L13-questions-1"},{"stem":"Choose a consistent past-tense sentence.","choices":["The artist designed the poster and selected its colors.","The artist had designed the poster and select its colors.","The artist designed the poster and selects its colors.","The artist designs the poster and selected its colors."],"answer":0,"why":"Both actions are in the same past-tense account: designed and selected.","skill":"Use agreement tense and reference","id":"L13-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which revision clarifies reference in Officials showed artists the posters because they needed revision?","choices":["Officials showed artists the posters because they needed it.","Officials showed artists the posters, and they needed revision.","Officials showed artists the posters because the posters needed revision.","They showed them the posters because they needed revision."],"answer":2,"why":"Naming the posters removes ambiguity about what needed revision.","skill":"Use agreement tense and reference","id":"L13-questions-3"},{"stem":"Choose the correct verb: Each of the campaign messages ___ a purpose.","choices":["have","were having","has","are having"],"answer":2,"why":"Each is singular even though messages is plural inside the phrase.","skill":"Use agreement tense and reference","id":"L13-questions-4"},{"stem":"Choose the correct pronoun: The museum revised ___ exhibit.","choices":["its","their","they’re","it’s"],"answer":0,"why":"Its is possessive. It’s means it is; the plural alternatives do not agree with museum.","skill":"Use agreement tense and reference","id":"L13-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which sentence correctly shifts from past to present because the time changes?","choices":["The poster appears in 1917 and remaining today.","The poster appear in 1917 and remained today.","The poster appearing in 1917 and remain today.","The poster appeared in 1917 and remains in a collection today."],"answer":3,"why":"Appeared describes a completed past event; remains describes its present location.","skill":"Use agreement tense and reference","id":"L13-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"Choose the correct verb: A group of letters ___ the exhibit’s centerpiece.","choices":["forms","have formed","form","were forming"],"answer":0,"why":"The subject group is singular. Of letters does not make it plural.","skill":"Use agreement tense and reference","id":"L13-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H21"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L14","number":14,"title":"Migration and a linear model","period":"1916–1930","section":"Mathematics","skill":"Solve linear equations","era":2,"stage":"Apply","minutes":40,"story":["During the Great Migration, African Americans moved from the South toward jobs and communities in other regions. Work opportunities mattered, but so did the desire to escape racial violence and unequal treatment. Arrival in a northern city did not eliminate discrimination.","The following problems use invented planning situations to practice linear equations. In one model, a community group pays a fixed room fee plus a cost for each participant. A fixed amount does not depend on the number of participants; a per-person amount does. The model can be written as a total equal to a fixed cost plus a rate multiplied by a quantity.","An equation is a relationship, not merely a direction to calculate. Solving it means finding a value that makes both sides equal. Substitute your answer back into the original relationship to check it."],"steps":["Define the unknown with its unit.","Translate the fixed amount and changing amount into an equation.","Use inverse operations on both sides to isolate the unknown.","Substitute the result into the original equation."],"worked":{"prompt":"A practice meeting costs $30 plus $4 per participant. The total is $70. How many participants?","steps":["Let p be participants: 30 + 4p = 70.","Subtract 30: 4p = 40.","Divide by 4: p = 10. Check: 30 + 4(10) = 70."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which expression represents a $25 fee plus $3 per participant p?","choices":["3(p + 25)","25p + 3","25 + 3p","28p"],"answer":2,"why":"The fixed fee is added once; 3p represents the changing per-person amount.","skill":"Solve linear equations","id":"L14-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Solve 20 + 5p = 65.","choices":["p = 9","p = 13","p = 17","p = 45"],"answer":0,"why":"Subtract 20 to get 5p = 45, then divide by 5.","skill":"Solve linear equations","id":"L14-questions-1"},{"stem":"A practice trip costs $18 per traveler plus a $90 fixed fee. A total of $360 pays for how many travelers?","choices":["25 travelers","20 travelers","30 travelers","15 travelers"],"answer":3,"why":"90 + 18t = 360. Subtract 90 and divide 270 by 18 to get 15.","skill":"Solve linear equations","id":"L14-questions-2"},{"stem":"Solve 3x − 7 = 20.","choices":["x = 9","x = 13/3","x = 4","x = 27"],"answer":0,"why":"Add 7 to both sides to get 3x = 27; divide by 3.","skill":"Solve linear equations","id":"L14-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which value satisfies 4n + 12 = 40?","choices":["10","7","28","13"],"answer":1,"why":"Substitution gives 4(7) + 12 = 28 + 12 = 40.","skill":"Solve linear equations","id":"L14-questions-4"},{"stem":"Solve 2(x + 5) = 26.","choices":["x = 21","x = 3","x = 8","x = 16"],"answer":2,"why":"Divide both sides by 2: x + 5 = 13. Subtract 5.","skill":"Solve linear equations","id":"L14-questions-5"},{"stem":"In C = 40 + 6n, what does 40 represent?","choices":["The cost per participant","The cost of six participants","The number of participants","The fixed cost"],"answer":3,"why":"The value 40 is added regardless of n. The rate per participant is 6.","skill":"Solve linear equations","id":"L14-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new practice plan satisfies 15 + 4n = 47. Find n.","choices":["32","4","8","12"],"answer":2,"why":"Subtract 15 to get 4n = 32, then divide by 4.","skill":"Solve linear equations","id":"L14-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H05"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Define la incógnita y sus unidades. Separa el costo fijo del costo que cambia. Haz la misma operación en ambos lados y sustituye tu respuesta para comprobarla."},{"id":"L15","number":15,"title":"Public health and experimental design","period":"1918–1919","section":"Science","skill":"Evaluate an investigation","era":2,"stage":"Apply","minutes":40,"story":["The influenza pandemic of 1918 affected communities during the final year of World War I. Public officials faced difficult decisions with incomplete information. Historical study asks what people knew at the time and how institutions responded, rather than assuming that everyone had today's knowledge.","To practice scientific reasoning, students build a sealed classroom airflow model. They release the same number of harmless visible model particles into identical boxes. They change the speed of a fan and count particles remaining after five minutes. These invented measurements concern the model only. They are not evidence about infection, treatment, or the safety of an actual room.","A conclusion is strongest when the design isolates the variable being tested. Repeated trials can help reveal variability, and a comparison condition can show what happens without the intervention. An impressive pattern does not automatically justify a claim beyond the measurement."],"steps":["State the exact research question.","Identify which factor changes and which outcome is measured.","Check that starting conditions and measurement times match.","Separate a conclusion about the model from a claim about the wider world."],"worked":{"prompt":"What comparison helps isolate fan speed?","steps":["Use identical boxes with the same starting number of particles.","Change fan speed while keeping the observation time fixed.","Measure particles remaining with the same procedure."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which condition should be held constant?","choices":["Starting number of model particles","The result the students hope to find","Fan speed in every condition","Number of particles remaining"],"answer":0,"why":"Starting particle count must match so the comparison can isolate fan speed.","skill":"Evaluate an investigation","id":"L15-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Which condition acts as a comparison without fan action?","choices":["Fan at medium speed","Fan at low speed","Fan off","Fan at high speed"],"answer":2,"why":"A fan-off condition shows what happens in the model without fan action.","skill":"Evaluate an investigation","id":"L15-questions-1"},{"stem":"What was measured after five minutes at high speed?","choices":["80 particles","15 particles","40 particles","25 particles"],"answer":1,"why":"Read the row for high speed. The result is 15 particles.","skill":"Evaluate an investigation","id":"L15-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which conclusion is supported?","choices":["Higher tested fan settings left fewer model particles.","No particles remain in the high-fan condition.","The high fan prevents all real infections.","Every actual room behaves like this model."],"answer":0,"why":"The table supports a pattern in this model. It does not measure infection or every real room.","skill":"Evaluate an investigation","id":"L15-questions-3"},{"stem":"Why would different observation times weaken the comparison?","choices":["The table would no longer have any labels.","Time cannot be measured in science.","The fan speed would become a year.","Time could affect the result too."],"answer":3,"why":"If both time and fan speed change, their separate effects are harder to identify.","skill":"Evaluate an investigation","id":"L15-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which follow-up best tests consistency?","choices":["Change all materials for every trial.","Stop recording numbers after the first trial.","Erase trials that differ from expectations.","Repeat each condition using the same procedure."],"answer":3,"why":"Repeated trials under the same procedure help evaluate how stable the pattern is.","skill":"Evaluate an investigation","id":"L15-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which additional question requires evidence beyond this activity?","choices":["How does infection risk change in actual classrooms?","Was the observation interval five minutes?","Which tested fan setting left the fewest model particles?","How many model particles remained with the fan off?"],"answer":0,"why":"The experiment does not measure actual infection risk. The other questions are answered by the setup and table.","skill":"Evaluate an investigation","id":"L15-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new trial changes both starting particles and fan speed. Why is interpretation harder?","choices":["Repeating the trial would erase every difference.","All possible conclusions become certain.","The timer becomes the independent result.","The factors’ effects are confounded."],"answer":3,"why":"Two changing factors can both influence the outcome; the design no longer isolates one clearly.","skill":"Evaluate an investigation","id":"L15-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H22"],"visual":{"kind":"bar","title":"Airflow classroom model","caption":"Invented model data; no health or treatment conclusions. Same starting count and five-minute interval.","headers":["Fan setting","Particles remaining"],"rows":[["Off",80],["Low",40],["Medium",25],["High",15]]},"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L16","number":16,"title":"A new voice in Harlem","period":"1920–1935","section":"Reading","skill":"Make a supported inference","era":2,"stage":"Apply","minutes":40,"story":["Original historical fiction for reading practice. The narrator and events below are invented; this is not a recovered memoir or a primary-source quotation.","Maya carried her notebook past the print shop twice before she went inside. From the sidewalk, the window displayed poems in neat columns. At home, her own lines crowded the margins of grocery lists. She had told her brother that she was only going to buy paper.","The editor set a stack of pages beside the counter. \"We read new work on Fridays,\" he said. Maya looked down at the notebook. She had rehearsed an introduction on the train, but now the sentences seemed too grand. Instead she asked what kind of paper the shop sold.","Outside, a pianist practiced above an open window. A woman on the steps argued that a neighborhood needed more than outsiders' stories about it. Maya stopped at the corner, opened her notebook, and crossed out the title of a poem. Beneath it she wrote the name of the street. Then she turned back toward the shop.","The scene is set amid the artistic energy associated with the Harlem Renaissance. Its reading task concerns the narrator's details: what they suggest, and what they do not establish."],"steps":["Find two or more details that point toward the inference.","Distinguish what is implied from what is stated.","Choose the smallest reasonable conclusion supported by those details.","Reject a choice that needs an invented event or motive."],"worked":{"prompt":"What suggests Maya is nervous about sharing her work?","steps":["She passes the shop twice and gives a different reason for going.","At the counter she abandons her rehearsed introduction.","These actions support hesitation; they do not prove she dislikes writing."]},"guided":{"stem":"Maya’s actions at the beginning most strongly suggest that she is:","choices":["Trying to prevent the shop from opening","Hesitant to present her work","Uninterested in any form of writing","Certain the editor will publish her immediately"],"answer":1,"why":"Passing the shop twice and hiding her purpose support hesitation, not certainty or hostility.","skill":"Make a supported inference","id":"L16-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Why is her question about paper significant?","choices":["It proves the shop sells no poems.","It delays saying why she came.","It shows the editor refused to speak.","It establishes the notebook is empty."],"answer":1,"why":"The question replaces the introduction she had rehearsed. It allows her to postpone revealing her purpose.","skill":"Make a supported inference","id":"L16-questions-1"},{"stem":"Changing the title to the street’s name suggests a growing interest in:","choices":["Abandoning writing for all time","Leaving the neighborhood that moment","Writing from her immediate world","Copying the editor’s published lines"],"answer":2,"why":"The local conversation and title revision connect her writing to the neighborhood around her.","skill":"Make a supported inference","id":"L16-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which detail best supports the idea that Maya intends to try again?","choices":["The editor sets down a stack of pages.","She turns back toward the shop.","The pianist practices upstairs.","The poems appear in neat columns."],"answer":1,"why":"Turning back is an action indicating renewed effort. The other details describe the setting.","skill":"Make a supported inference","id":"L16-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which inference is unsupported?","choices":["She initially conceals her purpose.","The editor has already accepted her poem.","She cares about her writing.","She has practiced what to say."],"answer":1,"why":"No submission or acceptance occurs in the scene. The other ideas are stated or supported by her actions.","skill":"Make a supported inference","id":"L16-questions-4"},{"stem":"The contrast between printed columns and crowded margins mainly emphasizes:","choices":["The editor’s exact annual income","The gap she feels between private work and publication","The price of all available writing paper","The number of buildings on the street"],"answer":1,"why":"The contrast connects polished public work with her informal private writing.","skill":"Make a supported inference","id":"L16-questions-5"},{"stem":"Why does the historical-fiction label matter?","choices":["Every event in the scene is a documented fact.","The dialogue is a verified quotation from 1925.","The scene is an authored exercise.","The narrator was a named historical editor."],"answer":2,"why":"The label prevents invented characters and dialogue from being mistaken for primary evidence.","skill":"Make a supported inference","id":"L16-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new narrator rehearses a speech but says nothing when called. Which inference is best supported?","choices":["The narrator definitely dislikes every listener.","The narrator may be anxious about speaking.","The audience has already canceled the event.","The narrator has never learned any words."],"answer":1,"why":"The action supports anxiety; the stronger claims require facts not provided.","skill":"Make a supported inference","id":"L16-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H23"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L17","number":17,"title":"Measuring a New Deal project","period":"1933–1941","section":"Mathematics","skill":"Use geometry and measurement","era":2,"stage":"Apply","minutes":40,"story":["New Deal programs put people to work on public projects during the Great Depression. The Civilian Conservation Corps undertook conservation work and improvements in parks and other public lands. A completed project could involve both a social goal and a practical engineering task.","This lesson uses invented plans for a rectangular park shelter, a path, and a storage area. No dimensions are taken from an actual CCC plan. The problems ask you to decide which geometric measurement fits a task. A fence needs perimeter; a floor covering needs area; a storage box may require volume.","Draw and label before substituting into a formula. A correct calculation with the wrong measurement does not answer the question. Keep linear units, square units, and cubic units distinct."],"steps":["Draw a simple labeled diagram.","Decide whether the task asks for length, area, or volume.","Use a formula that matches the shape and measurement.","Keep the appropriate units and check the result."],"worked":{"prompt":"A practice shelter floor is 12 feet by 8 feet. What is its area?","steps":["A floor covering requires area.","For a rectangle, area = length × width.","12 × 8 = 96 square feet."]},"guided":{"stem":"A practice rectangle is 10 feet by 6 feet. What is its area?","choices":["60 square feet","120 square feet","32 square feet","16 square feet"],"answer":0,"why":"Area is length times width: 10 × 6 = 60 square feet.","skill":"Use geometry and measurement","id":"L17-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"How much fencing surrounds a 14-foot by 9-foot rectangle?","choices":["23 feet","252 feet","126 feet","46 feet"],"answer":3,"why":"Perimeter is 2(14 + 9) = 46 feet. Area would not measure the fence length.","skill":"Use geometry and measurement","id":"L17-questions-1"},{"stem":"A triangular display has a 12-foot base and 5-foot perpendicular height. What is its area?","choices":["24 square feet","60 square feet","30 square feet","17 square feet"],"answer":2,"why":"Triangle area = one half × base × height = 0.5 × 12 × 5 = 30.","skill":"Use geometry and measurement","id":"L17-questions-2"},{"stem":"A rectangular storage box measures 4 by 3 by 2 feet. What is its volume?","choices":["9 cubic feet","18 cubic feet","48 cubic feet","24 cubic feet"],"answer":3,"why":"Volume = length × width × height = 4 × 3 × 2 = 24 cubic feet.","skill":"Use geometry and measurement","id":"L17-questions-3"},{"stem":"A right triangle has legs 6 feet and 8 feet. How long is its hypotenuse?","choices":["7 feet","10 feet","14 feet","100 feet"],"answer":1,"why":"By the Pythagorean theorem, c² = 6² + 8² = 100, so c = 10.","skill":"Use geometry and measurement","id":"L17-questions-4"},{"stem":"A practice path is 3 yards long. How many feet is that?","choices":["6 feet","9 feet","1 foot","12 feet"],"answer":1,"why":"There are 3 feet per yard. Multiply 3 yards by 3 feet per yard.","skill":"Use geometry and measurement","id":"L17-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which unit correctly reports the area of a park map?","choices":["Centimeters per second","Centimeters only","Square centimeters","Cubic centimeters"],"answer":2,"why":"Area measures a two-dimensional region and uses square units.","skill":"Use geometry and measurement","id":"L17-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new rectangular garden is 11 meters by 4 meters. Find its perimeter.","choices":["15 meters","30 meters","88 meters","44 meters"],"answer":1,"why":"The perimeter is 2(11 + 4) = 30 meters.","skill":"Use geometry and measurement","id":"L17-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H24"],"visual":{"kind":"diagram","title":"Practice shelter floor plan","caption":"Invented dimensions for geometry practice. Diagram is not to scale.","widthLabel":"12 ft","heightLabel":"8 ft"},"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L18","number":18,"title":"Relief recovery and another perspective","period":"1933–1939","section":"Writing","skill":"Compare perspectives fairly","era":2,"stage":"Apply","minutes":40,"story":["During the Great Depression, the federal government tried to respond to unemployment, unstable banks, and damaged confidence. The New Deal included different kinds of action. Direct assistance, work programs, and institutional reforms did not address exactly the same needs.","An argument about public priorities becomes stronger when it describes other positions accurately. A writer can favor work programs while acknowledging that someone without food may need immediate relief. A writer can favor institutional reform while recognizing that a repaired system may not meet an urgent need today.","Fair comparison requires identifying both a shared goal and a real difference. Two views can agree that public action is needed while disagreeing about which action deserves priority. Reducing the other view to a weak caricature makes comparison easier but reasoning worse."],"steps":["State each perspective in terms its supporter would recognize.","Identify a shared goal before discussing a disagreement.","Explain the difference in priority, method, or assumption.","Develop your own position with a specific reason and example."],"worked":{"prompt":"How can a work-program argument engage an immediate-relief perspective?","steps":["Acknowledge the shared goal of helping people in need.","Recognize that an urgent need may arise before a job is available.","Explain when work programs are valuable without claiming they solve every case."]},"guided":{"stem":"What shared goal could unite relief and work-program perspectives?","choices":["Avoiding any response to unemployment","Reducing hardship","Eliminating all public discussion","Rejecting every form of assistance"],"answer":1,"why":"Both can seek to reduce hardship while favoring different methods.","skill":"Compare perspectives fairly","id":"L18-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Which sentence represents an opposing view fairly?","choices":["Reform supporters reject every public institution.","Work supporters believe hunger is imaginary.","Relief supporters want nobody ever to work.","Immediate relief can meet needs before work is available."],"answer":3,"why":"The sentence describes a defensible reason for relief instead of inventing an extreme motive.","skill":"Compare perspectives fairly","id":"L18-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which difference is most relevant to the debate?","choices":["The number of letters in the program names","The order of paragraphs in an unrelated book","Immediate assistance versus longer-term change","The color used on a classroom worksheet"],"answer":2,"why":"The relevant distinction concerns the timing and kind of response to hardship.","skill":"Compare perspectives fairly","id":"L18-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which thesis engages more than one perspective?","choices":["Every opposing idea is foolish and needs no discussion.","This essay has three perspectives and several paragraphs.","There were programs during the Great Depression.","Work programs should lead, with immediate relief for urgent needs."],"answer":3,"why":"The thesis chooses a priority and defines a role for another perspective.","skill":"Compare perspectives fairly","id":"L18-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which example best supports an immediate-relief argument?","choices":["A park contains several kinds of trees.","A bank building uses stone walls.","A printed report has many numbered pages.","A family needs food before a job program can place a worker."],"answer":3,"why":"The example directly explains why timing matters for meeting an urgent need.","skill":"Compare perspectives fairly","id":"L18-questions-4"},{"stem":"What is a weakness in the claim One program will solve every economic problem?","choices":["Its scope exceeds the available support.","It uses a complete grammatical sentence.","It contains a recognizable subject.","It refers to a type of public policy."],"answer":0,"why":"Every economic problem is an absolute that would need far broader evidence.","skill":"Compare perspectives fairly","id":"L18-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which transition signals a qualified acknowledgment?","choices":["Although this approach offers immediate help, ...","Instead of considering any evidence, ...","For no identifiable reason, ...","As an unrelated topic, ..."],"answer":0,"why":"Although introduces an acknowledgment while preparing a limit or contrast.","skill":"Compare perspectives fairly","id":"L18-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new debate concerns museum admission fees. What does fair comparison require?","choices":["Describe each view’s reason accurately.","Ignore the shared goal of public access.","Invent an unreasonable motive for opponents.","Assume all museums face identical conditions."],"answer":0,"why":"Fair comparison begins with accurately representing the reasons each view offers.","skill":"Compare perspectives fairly","id":"L18-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H06","H24"],"visual":null,"writing":{"issue":"During a severe economic crisis, what should public institutions prioritize?","perspectives":["Meet urgent needs directly before pursuing other goals.","Create paid work that combines income with public improvements.","Repair institutions so the crisis is less likely to recur."],"minWords":90,"directions":"Develop your own position and explain its relationship to at least one other perspective. Use a specific example and explain how it supports your reasoning. These are original practice prompts.","rubric":["Ideas and analysis","Development and support","Organization","Language use and conventions"]},"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L19","number":19,"title":"Production and two unknowns","period":"1941–1945","section":"Mathematics","skill":"Solve systems of equations","era":3,"stage":"Apply","minutes":40,"story":["World War II required the United States to organize production, shipping, and labor on a large scale. A manager might need to consider two kinds of goods at once. Total quantity and total resource use could place two different limits on the same plan.","The practice scenarios in this lesson use invented workshop units. One kit requires two material units, and another requires five. A plan that names the total number of kits and the total material use produces two equations. Both equations must be true for the same pair of values.","Solving a system means finding that pair. A value that works in only one equation is insufficient. Label the variables and substitute the proposed solution into both relationships."],"steps":["Define both unknowns with their units.","Write one equation for each independent condition.","Use substitution or elimination to solve the pair.","Check the values in both original equations."],"worked":{"prompt":"A practice shop makes 10 kits: small kits use 2 units and large kits use 5. Total use is 32. Find each count.","steps":["Let s + l = 10 and 2s + 5l = 32.","Substitute s = 10 − l: 20 − 2l + 5l = 32.","3l = 12 gives l = 4, then s = 6. Check both totals."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which system represents 12 kits, with 2 units for each small kit s and 4 for each large kit l, using 36 units?","choices":["s − l = 12; 2s − 4l = 36","2s + l = 12; s + 4l = 36","s + l = 12; 2s + 4l = 36","s + l = 36; 2s + 4l = 12"],"answer":2,"why":"The first equation counts kits. The second adds the resource use from each type.","skill":"Solve systems of equations","id":"L19-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Solve x + y = 14 and x − y = 4. What is x?","choices":["7","9","5","18"],"answer":1,"why":"Add the equations: 2x = 18, so x = 9.","skill":"Solve systems of equations","id":"L19-questions-1"},{"stem":"Solve a + b = 10 and 3a + 5b = 38. What is b?","choices":["4","8","2","6"],"answer":0,"why":"Substitute a = 10 − b. Then 30 + 2b = 38, so b = 4.","skill":"Solve systems of equations","id":"L19-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which pair satisfies x + y = 11 and 2x + y = 17?","choices":["x = 6, y = 5","x = 8, y = 3","x = 5, y = 6","x = 4, y = 7"],"answer":0,"why":"6 + 5 = 11 and 2(6) + 5 = 17. The values satisfy both equations.","skill":"Solve systems of equations","id":"L19-questions-3"},{"stem":"A proposed solution satisfies only one equation. What should you conclude?","choices":["It solves every possible system.","The variables must have no units.","One equation must be erased.","It does not solve the system."],"answer":3,"why":"A system requires all its equations to hold at the same time.","skill":"Solve systems of equations","id":"L19-questions-4"},{"stem":"Subtract x + y = 8 from 3x + y = 18. What equation results?","choices":["2x + 2y = 10","3x = 18","2x = 10","4x = 26"],"answer":2,"why":"Subtract corresponding sides and terms: (3x + y) − (x + y) = 18 − 8.","skill":"Solve systems of equations","id":"L19-questions-5"},{"stem":"Two lines cross at (3, 4). What is the solution of the system they represent?","choices":["x = 0, y = 7","x = 3, y = 4","x = 7, y = 0","x = 4, y = 3"],"answer":1,"why":"The intersection point lies on both lines and therefore satisfies both equations.","skill":"Solve systems of equations","id":"L19-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"In a new system, m + n = 9 and m − n = 3. Find m.","choices":["9","12","6","3"],"answer":2,"why":"Adding gives 2m = 12, so m = 6.","skill":"Solve systems of equations","id":"L19-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H25"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L20","number":20,"title":"The home front in order","period":"1941–1945","section":"English","skill":"Choose transitions and logical sequence","era":3,"stage":"Apply","minutes":40,"story":["Rationing during World War II limited purchases of selected goods. Coupons and other rules helped manage scarce supplies while the war effort required resources. The details of different programs varied, and a historical explanation should avoid suggesting that every good was regulated in the same way.","An organized paragraph guides a reader through a relationship. If a sentence explains a cause, the next may describe a result. If two sentences show different experiences, a contrast transition may be appropriate. A word such as therefore should not be used merely because it sounds formal.","Revision also concerns sequence. A reader usually needs to know what a coupon system did before reading an example of its use. The best order provides necessary information at the moment it is needed."],"steps":["Name the relationship between the ideas: example, result, contrast, or addition.","Choose a transition that states that relationship accurately.","Place definitions before examples that depend on them.","Read the paragraph again to check its overall flow."],"worked":{"prompt":"Supplies were limited. ___, officials restricted some purchases. Which transition fits?","steps":["The second sentence describes a response to the first condition.","Therefore signals a result.","However would incorrectly signal contrast."]},"guided":{"stem":"Choose the transition: Supplies were limited. ___, some purchases were restricted.","choices":["On the contrary","However","For example, instead","Therefore"],"answer":3,"why":"The restriction is presented as a result of scarcity.","skill":"Choose transitions and logical sequence","id":"L20-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Choose the transition: Rationing affected many households. ___, experiences differed by location and resources.","choices":["For instance, therefore","Therefore","However","Similarly, because"],"answer":2,"why":"The second sentence qualifies a broad statement through contrast.","skill":"Choose transitions and logical sequence","id":"L20-questions-1"},{"stem":"A sentence defines a ration coupon. Where should it go?","choices":["Before an example that requires the definition","Inside an unrelated sentence about another war","After the conclusion that changes the topic","Only after every possible example has appeared"],"answer":0,"why":"A definition should precede the example that depends on it.","skill":"Choose transitions and logical sequence","id":"L20-questions-2"},{"stem":"Choose the transition: Families adapted in several ways. ___, some planned meals around available goods.","choices":["For example","Consequently, in contrast","On the contrary","Nevertheless"],"answer":0,"why":"The second sentence gives one instance of the adaptation described.","skill":"Choose transitions and logical sequence","id":"L20-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which order is clearest?","choices":["Conclude, introduce an unrelated person, omit the definition.","Explain scarcity, define rationing, describe an example.","Describe an unexplained coupon, change topic, define scarcity.","Give only dates without explaining any relationship."],"answer":1,"why":"The order supplies the reason, the system, and then an application.","skill":"Choose transitions and logical sequence","id":"L20-questions-4"},{"stem":"Choose the sentence that maintains the paragraph’s focus on rationing.","choices":["A modern museum opened a new parking lot.","Coupons shaped how families planned purchases.","A distant battlefield had a different climate.","A president’s childhood home had several rooms."],"answer":1,"why":"The sentence directly develops the paragraph’s stated topic.","skill":"Choose transitions and logical sequence","id":"L20-questions-5"},{"stem":"When is furthermore appropriate?","choices":["When reversing the previous claim","When introducing an unrelated topic","When adding a related supporting point","When identifying an exact quotation only"],"answer":2,"why":"Furthermore signals addition. It does not express contrast or make unrelated material relevant.","skill":"Choose transitions and logical sequence","id":"L20-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new paragraph states a problem and then a response caused by it. Which transition fits?","choices":["For an unrelated reason","In contrast","On the other hand","As a result"],"answer":3,"why":"As a result states the causal relationship the paragraph establishes.","skill":"Choose transitions and logical sequence","id":"L20-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H25"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L21","number":21,"title":"Weather in the Aleutians","period":"1942–1943","section":"Science","skill":"Interpret trends and interpolation","era":3,"stage":"Apply","minutes":40,"story":["The Aleutian campaign brought World War II to islands in the North Pacific. Japanese aircraft attacked Dutch Harbor in June 1942, and Japanese forces occupied Attu and Kiska. American forces retook Attu in 1943. Japanese forces had evacuated Kiska before American and Canadian troops landed there that August.","Weather, terrain, and supply problems shaped the campaign. This lesson uses a modern classroom model to practice reading a temperature trend. The table is invented and does not report weather from a particular military operation. It records the temperature of a model container at regular intervals.","Interpolation estimates a value between measured points. Extrapolation extends a pattern beyond the measured range and requires greater caution. Neither procedure can justify certainty when a pattern changes."],"steps":["Identify the independent axis and the measured quantity.","Describe whether the values rise, fall, or remain steady.","For interpolation, use the two measurements surrounding the requested value.","Treat an extension beyond the data as a prediction, not a recorded result."],"worked":{"prompt":"Estimate the model temperature at 15 minutes, between 10 and 20.","steps":["At 10 minutes the value is 8°C; at 20 it is 6°C.","15 is halfway between the times.","A straight-line estimate is 7°C."]},"guided":{"stem":"What is the measured temperature at 30 minutes?","choices":["6°C","10°C","2°C","4°C"],"answer":3,"why":"Read the row corresponding to 30 minutes.","skill":"Interpret trends and interpolation","id":"L21-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"What trend appears across the measured interval?","choices":["Temperature decreases at a steady rate.","Temperature rises at a steady rate.","Temperature remains unchanged.","Temperature first rises and then falls."],"answer":0,"why":"Each 10-minute step decreases the value by 2°C.","skill":"Interpret trends and interpolation","id":"L21-questions-1"},{"stem":"Assuming a linear trend, estimate the temperature at 25 minutes.","choices":["9°C","5°C","3°C","7°C"],"answer":1,"why":"25 minutes is halfway between 20 and 30; the midpoint of 6°C and 4°C is 5°C.","skill":"Interpret trends and interpolation","id":"L21-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which estimate is interpolation?","choices":["Estimating 15 minutes from 10 and 20","Assuming every container has the same temperature","Guessing a value before the first measurement","Predicting 60 minutes after the last measurement"],"answer":0,"why":"Interpolation uses values within the measured range.","skill":"Interpret trends and interpolation","id":"L21-questions-3"},{"stem":"What is the total temperature change from 0 to 40 minutes?","choices":["A decrease of 2°C","An increase of 40°C","An increase of 8°C","A decrease of 8°C"],"answer":3,"why":"The value changes from 10°C to 2°C, a difference of −8°C.","skill":"Interpret trends and interpolation","id":"L21-questions-4"},{"stem":"Why should a prediction at 90 minutes be treated cautiously?","choices":["A longer time always guarantees the same trend.","Every later temperature must be identical.","The table already records that exact time.","It extends far beyond the measurements."],"answer":3,"why":"The observed pattern may change outside the measured interval.","skill":"Interpret trends and interpolation","id":"L21-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which claim is supported by the table alone?","choices":["The model cooled across the recorded interval.","The table is a wartime military weather record.","Every Aleutian operation had these temperatures.","Weather alone determined the entire campaign."],"answer":0,"why":"The table is invented model data. It cannot establish historical measurements or a sole cause of the campaign’s outcome.","skill":"Interpret trends and interpolation","id":"L21-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new table gives 12°C at 10 minutes and 8°C at 20 minutes. Under a linear model, what is the value at 15?","choices":["10°C","11°C","6°C","9°C"],"answer":0,"why":"The midpoint of 12 and 8 is 10, matching the midpoint of the times.","skill":"Interpret trends and interpolation","id":"L21-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H08"],"visual":{"kind":"line","title":"Cooling classroom model","caption":"Invented practice measurements. These are not weather records from the Aleutian campaign.","headers":["Minutes","Temperature °C"],"rows":[[0,10],[10,8],[20,6],[30,4],[40,2]]},"writing":null,"es":"Interpolar es estimar entre dos mediciones. Extrapolar es extender el patrón más allá de los datos. Identifica las unidades y evita presentar una predicción como si fuera una observación."},{"id":"L22","number":22,"title":"Security and the author’s purpose","period":"1947–1954","section":"Reading","skill":"Analyze purpose and qualifications","era":3,"stage":"Apply","minutes":40,"story":["After World War II, American leaders connected international economic recovery with national security. The Marshall Plan offered aid to help rebuild European economies. Supporters argued that stability abroad could also strengthen trade and reduce political danger.","At home, fears of disloyalty led to investigations, loyalty programs, and accusations that could damage careers. The question was not simply whether a country should protect itself. It was also how public institutions should investigate danger while protecting rights and evaluating evidence fairly.","A government can identify a real concern and still choose a flawed response. Recognizing that distinction allows a reader to evaluate a policy without assuming that its stated goal proves its methods are justified. Evidence must support the accusation, and the method must be examined separately from the intention.","This passage therefore asks readers to separate a goal from the means used to pursue it. It does not argue that all security concerns are imaginary or that every official action is equally reasonable."],"steps":["Identify the question or distinction the author returns to.","Separate the author’s position from a position being described.","Notice qualifications such as can, some, or not every.","Choose the purpose that accounts for the whole passage."],"worked":{"prompt":"Does the passage say security never matters?","steps":["Paragraph 3 allows that a concern can be real.","It then asks whether the response is justified.","The purpose is to distinguish goal and method, not to dismiss all security concerns."]},"guided":{"stem":"The author’s main purpose is to encourage readers to:","choices":["Evaluate methods separately from stated goals","Study only trade and ignore public rights","Assume all government concerns are imaginary","Accept every accusation made by an official"],"answer":0,"why":"The final two paragraphs explicitly separate concerns, evidence, and methods.","skill":"Analyze purpose and qualifications","id":"L22-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"The Marshall Plan example mainly establishes:","choices":["An argument that aid ended every international conflict","A link between recovery and security goals","A claim that economic policy has no purpose","A proof that every domestic accusation was true"],"answer":1,"why":"Paragraph 1 describes how economic recovery was connected to security and trade.","skill":"Analyze purpose and qualifications","id":"L22-questions-1"},{"stem":"What distinction matters most in paragraph 3?","choices":["The beginning and ending of a military parade","A European capital and an American state","A newspaper’s title and its price","A real concern and a justified response"],"answer":3,"why":"The paragraph argues that a concern’s reality does not automatically justify a method.","skill":"Analyze purpose and qualifications","id":"L22-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which statement would the author most likely accept?","choices":["A good intention proves every action is appropriate.","A policy’s methods require their own evaluation.","An accusation should replace the need for evidence.","All investigations reach the same valid conclusion."],"answer":1,"why":"That statement preserves the passage’s central distinction.","skill":"Analyze purpose and qualifications","id":"L22-questions-3"},{"stem":"The phrase not every primarily serves to:","choices":["Introduce a new historical decade","Limit the scope of a claim","Replace evidence with a personal insult","Prove that no official ever acted"],"answer":1,"why":"A qualification prevents an overbroad claim about all actions.","skill":"Analyze purpose and qualifications","id":"L22-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which description best captures the tone?","choices":["Mocking and dismissive","Celebratory and uncritical","Entirely indifferent to evidence","Analytical and qualified"],"answer":3,"why":"The author distinguishes related ideas and rejects extreme conclusions.","skill":"Analyze purpose and qualifications","id":"L22-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which statement overstates the author’s position?","choices":["Goals do not settle every question about methods.","A real concern can produce a flawed response.","All efforts to protect national security are illegitimate.","Evidence should support an accusation."],"answer":2,"why":"The passage expressly rejects the idea that all security concerns are imaginary.","skill":"Analyze purpose and qualifications","id":"L22-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new passage praises a policy goal but questions its implementation. Its position is best described as:","choices":["Opposed to every possible public purpose","Certain that intention resolves every question","Unable to distinguish purpose from implementation","Supportive of the goal with reservations about methods"],"answer":3,"why":"A qualified position can support a goal while criticizing how it is pursued.","skill":"Analyze purpose and qualifications","id":"L22-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H09","H26"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L23","number":23,"title":"The space age and functions","period":"1957–1969","section":"Mathematics","skill":"Interpret functions and graphs","era":3,"stage":"Apply","minutes":40,"story":["The space program made scientific and engineering decisions visible to a broad public. Apollo 11 carried out a crewed lunar landing in 1969, with Armstrong and Aldrin on the surface while Collins remained in lunar orbit. The mission depended on systems whose changing quantities had to be represented and interpreted.","A function assigns one output to each input in its domain. In this lesson, invented training models represent a quantity as a function of time. A formula and a graph can describe the same relationship. Neither is an actual Apollo flight record.","Read the variables before using the formula. A value of f(3) means the output when the input is 3; it does not mean multiply the letter f by 3. A model's stated domain also matters: a formula intended for an interval should not be treated as a description of all possible times."],"steps":["Identify the input output and units.","Substitute the input into the formula carefully.","Connect a starting value with the graph’s vertical intercept.","Interpret the rate of change and respect the stated domain."],"worked":{"prompt":"In a practice model, d(t) = 4t + 12. Find d(3).","steps":["Substitute 3 for t.","4(3) + 12 = 24.","The output is 24 in the model’s stated distance units."]},"guided":{"stem":"For f(t) = 5t + 8, what is f(4)?","choices":["17","13","28","40"],"answer":2,"why":"Substitute 4: 5 × 4 + 8 = 28.","skill":"Interpret functions and graphs","id":"L23-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"In h(t) = 3t + 20, what does 20 represent?","choices":["The output when t is zero","The number of separate input variables","The output added each time t rises by one","The largest possible value of t"],"answer":0,"why":"At t = 0, h(0) = 20; it is the initial value or vertical intercept.","skill":"Interpret functions and graphs","id":"L23-questions-1"},{"stem":"What is the rate of change of y = 7x − 2?","choices":["5","−7","−2","7"],"answer":3,"why":"For a linear function y = mx + b, m is the rate of change.","skill":"Interpret functions and graphs","id":"L23-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which input gives an output of 30 in g(t) = 6t + 12?","choices":["7","2","3","5"],"answer":2,"why":"Solve 6t + 12 = 30. Then 6t = 18 and t = 3.","skill":"Interpret functions and graphs","id":"L23-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which condition must a table satisfy to represent y as a function of x?","choices":["One x is paired with two different y values.","Every y must be different from all other y values.","Each x is paired with exactly one y.","The x values are always excluded from the table."],"answer":2,"why":"A function requires one output per input. Different inputs may share an output.","skill":"Interpret functions and graphs","id":"L23-questions-4"},{"stem":"A model’s domain is 0 ≤ t ≤ 10. Which input lies outside it?","choices":["10","12","4","0"],"answer":1,"why":"The stated interval includes 0 through 10, including its endpoints.","skill":"Interpret functions and graphs","id":"L23-questions-5"},{"stem":"Two linear models have the same intercept but slopes 2 and 5. Which rises faster?","choices":["The model with slope 5","The intercept alone determines the rate","The model with slope 2","They must rise at the same rate"],"answer":0,"why":"Slope measures change per unit of input; 5 is larger than 2.","skill":"Interpret functions and graphs","id":"L23-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new model is p(t) = 2t + 9. Find p(6).","choices":["17","30","21","12"],"answer":2,"why":"Substitute the input: 2 × 6 + 9 = 21.","skill":"Interpret functions and graphs","id":"L23-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H11"],"visual":{"kind":"line","title":"Training model d(t) = 4t + 12","caption":"Invented linear training model; not an Apollo trajectory.","headers":["Time units","Distance units"],"rows":[[0,12],[1,16],[2,20],[3,24],[4,28]]},"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L24","number":24,"title":"Protest and a counterargument","period":"1954–1968","section":"Writing","skill":"Respond to a counterargument","era":3,"stage":"Apply","minutes":40,"story":["Civil rights organizers used litigation, local organizing, protest, and political pressure to challenge discrimination. The Civil Rights Act of 1964 addressed discrimination in public accommodations and employment. Its passage grew from sustained organizing and conflict rather than an automatic national agreement.","A counterargument is a serious objection to a writer's position. Addressing one can improve an argument by showing where a claim needs explanation or limits. It is different from inventing an obviously unreasonable opponent.","Suppose a writer favors public protest as a way to bring attention to injustice. A reasonable concern is that attention alone may not produce enforceable policy. The writer can acknowledge that concern and explain how protest can work with legal and legislative strategies. A qualified argument can be stronger than a claim that one tactic is always sufficient."],"steps":["Identify a reasonable objection to your actual claim.","State it fairly without exaggerating the opponent’s position.","Respond with a reason and a specific example or qualification.","Explain how the response refines rather than abandons your position."],"worked":{"prompt":"How might a protest-focused argument answer the concern that attention alone is insufficient?","steps":["Acknowledge that publicity does not automatically change law.","Explain that attention can build support for enforceable action.","Revise the claim to describe protest working alongside institutional strategies."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which is a serious counterargument to protest alone?","choices":["No person has ever noticed a public event.","Public attention may not produce enforceable policy.","A sign can never contain a meaningful sentence.","Every protester dislikes all forms of discussion."],"answer":1,"why":"The objection addresses the link between attention and policy, a real issue for the claim.","skill":"Respond to a counterargument","id":"L24-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Which response most effectively answers that objection?","choices":["The objection should be repeated without a response.","All tactics always work under all conditions.","Protest can build support for legal and legislative action.","Anyone questioning protest must oppose equality."],"answer":2,"why":"It explains a mechanism and connects protest with other strategies.","skill":"Respond to a counterargument","id":"L24-questions-1"},{"stem":"What makes a counterargument fair?","choices":["It removes every reason from the opposing view.","It addresses a plausible objection.","It assigns opponents a foolish motive.","It describes a claim unrelated to the essay."],"answer":1,"why":"A fair counterargument challenges the actual position with a defensible concern.","skill":"Respond to a counterargument","id":"L24-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which claim is best qualified?","choices":["Only one tactic can matter in every movement.","Protest has never contributed to any change.","Protest can help when linked to further action.","Protest always solves every institutional problem."],"answer":2,"why":"Can and linked to further action keep the argument within a defensible scope.","skill":"Respond to a counterargument","id":"L24-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which phrase signals a concession?","choices":["This concern is valid when ...","The opposing view should be ignored ...","No reasonable person could ask ...","Evidence is unnecessary because ..."],"answer":0,"why":"A concession acknowledges a condition or strength in the other view.","skill":"Respond to a counterargument","id":"L24-questions-4"},{"stem":"What should follow a concession?","choices":["An identical sentence copied several times","A claim that evidence is no longer needed","An unrelated anecdote with no connection","An explained response or refinement"],"answer":3,"why":"The writer must show how the acknowledged point affects the argument.","skill":"Respond to a counterargument","id":"L24-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which evidence would most strengthen a claim about combining tactics?","choices":["The color of a classroom poster","A list of streets without any explanation","An example linking organizing with a policy change","The word count of an unrelated speech"],"answer":2,"why":"Relevant evidence should connect the tactics to the result the writer claims.","skill":"Respond to a counterargument","id":"L24-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new essay favors a preservation project but acknowledges cost. What is the strongest next move?","choices":["Pretend the cost was never mentioned.","Explain how priorities or funding could address the cost.","Declare all buildings equally valuable.","Insult people who ask about the budget."],"answer":1,"why":"A response should engage the acknowledged concern with reasoning.","skill":"Respond to a counterargument","id":"L24-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H10"],"visual":null,"writing":{"issue":"When a community seeks institutional change, which strategy should lead?","perspectives":["Public protest should make the problem impossible to ignore.","Legal and legislative action should produce enforceable rules.","Local organizing should build sustained participation before other actions."],"minWords":100,"directions":"Develop your own position and explain its relationship to at least one other perspective. Use a specific example and explain how it supports your reasoning. These are original practice prompts.","rubric":["Ideas and analysis","Development and support","Organization","Language use and conventions"]},"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L25","number":25,"title":"Voting rights across two texts","period":"1965–1975","section":"Reading","skill":"Integrate paired texts and a graphic","era":4,"stage":"Advance","minutes":40,"story":["Passage A — Original historical analysis. A voting right can exist in constitutional language while discriminatory practices obstruct its exercise. The Voting Rights Act of 1965 addressed barriers that had persisted despite earlier legal protections. Federal action mattered because local rules and practices could keep a national promise from becoming effective.","Passage B — Original historical analysis. A law changes the rules, but people still have to use and defend the opportunity it creates. Organizing, information, access, and enforcement affect practical participation. Evaluating a law therefore requires looking both at its provisions and at what happens afterward.","These passages emphasize different parts of the same problem. The first highlights the role of a legal intervention. The second highlights the work required to make rights usable. They need not be read as opposing claims.","The accompanying graphic uses invented participation rates for a classroom district. It is not an actual election record. A rising rate in the graphic can establish a pattern within the practice data; by itself it cannot identify the cause of that pattern."],"steps":["Summarize each passage separately before comparing them.","Identify agreement as well as differences in emphasis.","Read the graphic’s labels and data note.","Combine sources only where they support the same specific conclusion."],"worked":{"prompt":"Do the passages disagree about whether law matters?","steps":["Passage A emphasizes the legal intervention.","Passage B says laws change rules but require action afterward.","They share concern about usable rights while emphasizing different mechanisms."]},"guided":{"stem":"Both passages are concerned with:","choices":["Making voting rights usable","Treating all local practices as identical","Measuring the exact size of every electorate","Replacing every law with public opinion"],"answer":0,"why":"Both distinguish a promise of rights from the ability to exercise them.","skill":"Integrate paired texts and a graphic","id":"L25-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Passage B adds which emphasis to Passage A?","choices":["A denial that barriers ever existed","An argument that participation needs no support","A claim that no national law can matter","Practical work following a legal change"],"answer":3,"why":"Passage B focuses on organizing, access, and enforcement after a law changes the rules.","skill":"Integrate paired texts and a graphic","id":"L25-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which claim is supported by the practice graphic?","choices":["Every eligible person participated in every year.","The district is an actual historical election district.","Participation rose across the listed years.","One specific policy caused the entire rise."],"answer":2,"why":"The values rise from 40 to 60 to 70 percent. The data note denies that this is an actual district record.","skill":"Integrate paired texts and a graphic","id":"L25-questions-2"},{"stem":"The increase from 40% to 60% is:","choices":["20 percent of the original rate","20 percentage points","40 percentage points","60 percentage points"],"answer":1,"why":"Subtract the percentages to find a percentage-point change: 60 − 40 = 20.","skill":"Integrate paired texts and a graphic","id":"L25-questions-3"},{"stem":"What evidence is missing for a strong causal claim about the practice trend?","choices":["The existence of different listed years","The value shown for the last year","Information that isolates a proposed cause","The names of the graph’s two axes"],"answer":2,"why":"A time trend alone does not isolate policy effects from other changing factors.","skill":"Integrate paired texts and a graphic","id":"L25-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which statement best synthesizes the passages?","choices":["Only one kind of action can ever matter.","Every law immediately guarantees equal participation.","Legal action and practical support can work together.","Passage B proves that Passage A is entirely false."],"answer":2,"why":"The synthesis preserves the legal emphasis of A and the practical emphasis of B.","skill":"Integrate paired texts and a graphic","id":"L25-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which task requires integrating text and graphic?","choices":["Reading the last number in the graph alone","Counting the letters in a passage heading","Explaining why a rise does not prove a cause","Finding the first date in the title alone"],"answer":2,"why":"The data show a pattern, while the text warns that evaluation requires additional evidence.","skill":"Integrate paired texts and a graphic","id":"L25-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"Two new texts both favor access but emphasize different methods. Which comparison is sound?","choices":["They cannot have any meaningful relationship.","They must contradict every sentence in each other.","Their methods must produce identical outcomes.","They share a goal but differ in emphasis."],"answer":3,"why":"Agreement on a goal can coexist with a different emphasis on how to pursue it.","skill":"Integrate paired texts and a graphic","id":"L25-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H27","H16"],"visual":{"kind":"line","title":"Practice participation pattern","caption":"Invented classroom district and rates. These are not historical election data.","headers":["Practice year","Participation %"],"rows":[[1,40],[2,60],[3,70]]},"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L26","number":26,"title":"The Great Society and a data sample","period":"1964–1970","section":"Mathematics","skill":"Use statistics and probability","era":4,"stage":"Advance","minutes":40,"story":["Great Society initiatives expanded the federal government's role in areas such as health coverage, education, and poverty. Medicare and Medicaid became law in 1965. Public debate about programs often depended on how needs, costs, and outcomes were described.","Statistics can summarize data, but each summary answers a particular question. The mean uses every value. The median describes the middle of an ordered set. The range describes the distance between the largest and smallest values. None tells the entire story of a population.","The practice sets in this lesson are invented. A sample drawn from a single classroom cannot automatically represent the whole nation. Before generalizing, ask how the data were collected and whether the sample fits the question."],"steps":["Identify whether the question asks for center, spread, or probability.","Order the values when finding a median or range.","Use a clearly defined total when calculating probability.","Consider sample selection before making a population claim."],"worked":{"prompt":"Practice attendance values are 8, 10, 12, 14, 16. Find the mean.","steps":["Add the values: 60.","Divide by the five observations: 60 ÷ 5 = 12.","The mean is 12; it does not describe every individual observation."]},"guided":{"stem":"Find the mean of 10, 12, 14, 16, 18.","choices":["12","16","70","14"],"answer":3,"why":"The sum is 70. Divide by the five values: 70/5 = 14.","skill":"Use statistics and probability","id":"L26-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Find the median of 5, 9, 12, 18, 30.","choices":["9","12","14.8","18"],"answer":1,"why":"The values are ordered and the third of five is the middle value.","skill":"Use statistics and probability","id":"L26-questions-1"},{"stem":"What is the range of 6, 11, 15, 20?","choices":["6","20","13","14"],"answer":3,"why":"Range is largest minus smallest: 20 − 6 = 14.","skill":"Use statistics and probability","id":"L26-questions-2"},{"stem":"A practice box has 3 blue and 7 white cards. What is the probability of drawing blue at random?","choices":["3/7","3/10","1/3","7/10"],"answer":1,"why":"Favorable outcomes are 3 blue cards out of 10 total cards.","skill":"Use statistics and probability","id":"L26-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which value most strongly increases the mean in 10, 11, 12, 13, 100?","choices":["100","12","11","10"],"answer":0,"why":"The large value pulls the mean upward relative to the other observations.","skill":"Use statistics and probability","id":"L26-questions-4"},{"stem":"Why is a single classroom a weak sample for a national claim?","choices":["A classroom sample cannot contain any numbers.","Its selection may not represent the population.","Every national population has exactly one classroom.","Only the largest value matters in every survey."],"answer":1,"why":"Representativeness concerns how the sample relates to the population being described.","skill":"Use statistics and probability","id":"L26-questions-5"},{"stem":"A practice survey has 24 supporters among 40 respondents. What fraction supports the proposal?","choices":["3/5","5/8","2/5","3/8"],"answer":0,"why":"24/40 reduces to 3/5 by dividing both values by 8.","skill":"Use statistics and probability","id":"L26-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new ordered set is 4, 7, 9, 11, 24. What is its median?","choices":["7","55","9","11"],"answer":2,"why":"The middle of the five ordered values is 9.","skill":"Use statistics and probability","id":"L26-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H28"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L27","number":27,"title":"Environmental evidence and conclusions","period":"1970–1980","section":"Science","skill":"Evaluate claims against measurements","era":4,"stage":"Advance","minutes":40,"story":["Growing environmental concerns led to changes in federal institutions and law. EPA began operating in 1970, bringing several responsibilities together. Monitoring, research, and enforcement became part of a larger public effort to address pollution.","In this original classroom model, students compare particles remaining after air passes through two filter materials. They test the same starting amounts and measure the result with the same instrument. The values are invented. The question is narrow: which material leaves fewer model particles under the tested conditions?","Scientific reasoning requires matching the strength of a conclusion to the strength of the evidence. A repeated difference can support a comparison in the tested model. It cannot by itself establish every possible environmental effect, explain a national trend, or prove that no other conditions matter."],"steps":["Locate the measurements relevant to the claim.","Compare like conditions rather than unrelated rows.","Check whether the claim is descriptive, causal, or universal.","Reject conclusions that exceed the tested outcome or conditions."],"worked":{"prompt":"At the same starting count, how can the filters be compared?","steps":["Compare the results in the same row.","A lower remaining count means more particles were removed in this model.","Do not compare one filter’s low starting count with the other’s high starting count."]},"guided":{"stem":"At a starting count of 200, which filter leaves fewer particles?","choices":["The table provides no result for 200","Both leave the same count","Filter B","Filter A"],"answer":2,"why":"At 200 starting particles, B leaves 30 and A leaves 50.","skill":"Evaluate claims against measurements","id":"L27-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"At a starting count of 100, how many more particles does A leave than B?","choices":["10","40","20","15"],"answer":0,"why":"A leaves 25 and B leaves 15. The difference is 10.","skill":"Evaluate claims against measurements","id":"L27-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which claim is supported across every listed starting count?","choices":["B leaves fewer model particles than A.","The starting count never affects the result.","B leaves zero particles in every trial.","A removes every possible pollutant."],"answer":0,"why":"B has a lower recorded result in all three rows. The other statements conflict with the data or exceed the model.","skill":"Evaluate claims against measurements","id":"L27-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which comparison is fairest?","choices":["A at 100 and B at 300","A’s first value and an unrelated city average","A’s material name and B’s trial date","A and B at the same starting count"],"answer":3,"why":"Like starting conditions make the filters’ outcomes comparable.","skill":"Evaluate claims against measurements","id":"L27-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which conclusion is too broad?","choices":["A leaves 75 particles when starting at 300.","B leaves 45 particles when starting at 300.","The table includes three starting counts.","B solves every air-quality problem."],"answer":3,"why":"The model tests one particle outcome under limited conditions, not every air-quality problem.","skill":"Evaluate claims against measurements","id":"L27-questions-4"},{"stem":"What would help assess the reliability of the comparison?","choices":["Replacing the measurements with a slogan","Changing the starting conditions without recording them","Only keeping results that favor one filter","Repeated trials with reported variation"],"answer":3,"why":"Repeated observations and variation help assess how stable the measured difference is.","skill":"Evaluate claims against measurements","id":"L27-questions-5"},{"stem":"Why can this table not establish EPA’s historical impact?","choices":["It contains invented model measurements.","It compares two labeled materials.","It contains numbers in more than one column.","Its rows have a consistent structure."],"answer":0,"why":"The table is an instructional model, not an evaluation dataset about EPA.","skill":"Evaluate claims against measurements","id":"L27-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new model records 12 particles for A and 8 for B under identical conditions. What is supported?","choices":["The model explains all national pollution trends.","B leaves four fewer particles in that test.","B eliminates every environmental hazard.","A must always fail under every condition."],"answer":1,"why":"The narrow comparison is 12 − 8 = 4; broader claims need additional evidence.","skill":"Evaluate claims against measurements","id":"L27-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H12"],"visual":{"kind":"line","title":"Air-filter classroom comparison","caption":"Invented model data. Identical starting conditions in each paired comparison.","headers":["Starting particles","A remaining","B remaining"],"rows":[[100,25,15],[200,50,30],[300,75,45]]},"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L28","number":28,"title":"Watergate and precise language","period":"1972–1974","section":"English","skill":"Revise for concision and accuracy","era":4,"stage":"Advance","minutes":40,"story":["The Watergate break-in and the investigations that followed raised questions about presidential power, accountability, and evidence. Journalists, courts, Congress, and other institutions played different roles in uncovering and evaluating information. Richard Nixon resigned the presidency in 1974.","An explanation of such a complex event benefits from precise language. In order to determine what happened can usually become To determine what happened without losing meaning. A revision should remove unnecessary words while preserving qualifications and relationships.","Shorter is not automatically better. If a sentence says several investigators reached a conclusion, changing several to all makes a stronger claim. If a sentence distinguishes an accusation from a finding, removing that distinction may distort the history. The best edit is the clearest accurate version."],"steps":["Identify the meaning the sentence must preserve.","Remove repetition and phrases that add no useful information.","Keep necessary distinctions, qualifications, and causal relationships.","Prefer the clearest accurate choice rather than the shortest choice at any cost."],"worked":{"prompt":"Revise: The committee held a meeting in order to review the evidence.","steps":["In order to can become to.","The committee met to review the evidence preserves the purpose.","Do not change evidence to proof of every accusation."]},"guided":{"stem":"Choose the most concise accurate revision: At this point in time, the committee began its review.","choices":["The committee, at this particular time period, began reviewing.","The committee then began its review.","The review which the committee began was begun then.","The committee proved every allegation."],"answer":1,"why":"Then preserves the timing while removing unnecessary words. Proved changes the claim.","skill":"Revise for concision and accuracy","id":"L28-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Replace in order to examine without changing meaning.","choices":["after examination ended","to examine","because examining","despite examining"],"answer":1,"why":"To expresses the same purpose more directly.","skill":"Revise for concision and accuracy","id":"L28-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which edit changes a qualified claim into an unsupported absolute?","choices":["Removing a repeated word","Using to instead of in order to","Changing some records to all records","Replacing at this point in time with then"],"answer":2,"why":"Some and all have different scopes. That change requires new evidence.","skill":"Revise for concision and accuracy","id":"L28-questions-2"},{"stem":"Choose the clearest revision: The reason for the delay was because the records were incomplete.","choices":["The review was delayed because the records were incomplete.","The delay’s reason was the reason because of records.","Incomplete records proved the review had no purpose.","The reason was because of the reason for delay."],"answer":0,"why":"The revision states the event and cause directly without redundant wording.","skill":"Revise for concision and accuracy","id":"L28-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which wording preserves the distinction between allegation and finding?","choices":["All suspicions were established facts.","Investigators examined the allegation.","Investigators confirmed every allegation.","The allegation proved every related claim."],"answer":1,"why":"Examination does not by itself mean confirmation. The original distinction must remain.","skill":"Revise for concision and accuracy","id":"L28-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which sentence avoids unnecessary repetition?","choices":["The report’s summary summarized the summary findings.","The findings were summarized by the summarizing report.","The report summarized the findings in a summary.","The report summarized the findings."],"answer":3,"why":"The first choice expresses the idea once.","skill":"Revise for concision and accuracy","id":"L28-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which revision best preserves reported uncertainty?","choices":["No connection could ever exist.","The records prove every possible connection.","The records may indicate a connection.","Every reader must reach the same conclusion."],"answer":2,"why":"May preserves uncertainty. The alternatives replace it with unsupported certainty.","skill":"Revise for concision and accuracy","id":"L28-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new sentence says Several witnesses described the event. Which edit preserves its scope?","choices":["Several witnesses gave accounts of the event.","No witness described any event.","Every witness described the event identically.","The accounts prove every possible conclusion."],"answer":0,"why":"Several remains several; the other revisions change quantity or certainty.","skill":"Revise for concision and accuracy","id":"L28-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H29"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L29","number":29,"title":"An empty mill and a changing town","period":"1980–1990","section":"Reading","skill":"Analyze voice and figurative language","era":4,"stage":"Advance","minutes":40,"story":["Original historical fiction for reading practice. The narrator and events are invented. This is not a documented worker's testimony.","The first Saturday after the mill closed, Daniel woke before the alarm. For twenty-three years the morning had carried a sequence: coffee, boots, the walk past the bakery, the gate. Now the gate remained, but it had stopped being a destination. He sat at the kitchen table and waited for a sound he could no longer hear from the house.","His daughter spread a college map across the table. She pointed to a city he had never visited and spoke about laboratories, internships, and a train line that reached the campus. He nodded at each point. When she asked whether the distance worried him, he folded a corner of the newspaper and said the trains probably ran well.","Later he walked to the mill. A painted sign announced a proposal for new workshops inside the old buildings. Behind the fence, weeds had reached the lower windows. He tried to picture new workers at the benches. The picture would not stay still: it kept turning into faces he already knew.","On the way home he bought a second cup of coffee for his daughter. The bakery owner asked whether he wanted the usual. Daniel looked at the map folded under his arm. \"Not quite,\" he said.","The scene explores one imagined response to industrial change: attachment to a familiar world alongside uncertainty about new possibilities."],"steps":["Distinguish the narrator’s words from the author’s overall message.","Use actions and contrasts to interpret an emotion.","Explain what a figurative phrase contributes in this scene.","Allow a mixed emotional response when details support it."],"worked":{"prompt":"Why does Daniel answer a question about distance by discussing trains?","steps":["His answer addresses logistics rather than the feeling his daughter asks about.","The scene presents his hesitation indirectly.","This suggests difficulty expressing his concern, not proof that he opposes her plans."]},"guided":{"stem":"Daniel’s response to his daughter most strongly suggests:","choices":["A detailed knowledge of the new city","Complete indifference to her plans","Concern he does not state directly","Certainty that she must stay home"],"answer":2,"why":"The indirect answer and folded newspaper suggest an unspoken concern. The stronger conclusions are not established.","skill":"Analyze voice and figurative language","id":"L29-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"The gate has stopped being a destination primarily emphasizes:","choices":["The daughter has already left the country.","The bakery has moved to a new city.","A familiar routine has lost its purpose.","The gate has physically disappeared."],"answer":2,"why":"The gate remains, but his relationship to it has changed.","skill":"Analyze voice and figurative language","id":"L29-questions-1"},{"stem":"The unstable picture of future workshops suggests:","choices":["Every former worker opposes any new use.","Memory makes change difficult to imagine.","The proposal has already been fully rejected.","Daniel knows the names of the future workers."],"answer":1,"why":"The imagined new workers become familiar faces, linking uncertainty to memory.","skill":"Analyze voice and figurative language","id":"L29-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which description best fits the scene’s tone?","choices":["Mocking toward everyone in the town","Certain that no new opportunity can exist","Triumphant and entirely carefree","Reflective with restrained uncertainty"],"answer":3,"why":"The passage holds attachment and possibility together without resolving every concern.","skill":"Analyze voice and figurative language","id":"L29-questions-3"},{"stem":"The final words Not quite most likely indicate:","choices":["A recognition that his routine is changing","A refusal ever to buy coffee again","A factual correction about the train schedule","An announcement that the mill has reopened"],"answer":0,"why":"The phrase echoes a familiar routine while acknowledging that the present is different.","skill":"Analyze voice and figurative language","id":"L29-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which detail offers a possibility rather than a settled result?","choices":["The daughter’s map on the table","The sign proposing new workshops","The fact that Daniel wakes early","The weeds at the mill’s windows"],"answer":1,"why":"A proposal signals a possible future; the scene does not say it has been implemented.","skill":"Analyze voice and figurative language","id":"L29-questions-5"},{"stem":"What conclusion should a historian avoid drawing from this scene alone?","choices":["The passage is labeled historical fiction.","The narrator uses familiar routines to show change.","Every worker experienced mill closure this way.","The story depicts an imagined individual response."],"answer":2,"why":"One invented scene cannot establish how every real person felt.","skill":"Analyze voice and figurative language","id":"L29-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new narrator says a silent station still sets the town’s clock. The phrase most likely suggests:","choices":["The town has no residents at all","An old institution still shapes local habits","The station contains the only working clock on Earth","All trains still operate on the old schedule"],"answer":1,"why":"The figurative meaning concerns lasting influence, not a universal literal claim.","skill":"Analyze voice and figurative language","id":"L29-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H30"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L30","number":30,"title":"Networks and exponential change","period":"1986–2000","section":"Mathematics","skill":"Model repeated growth","era":4,"stage":"Advance","minutes":40,"story":["Publicly supported research networks helped create the infrastructure for the modern internet. NSFNET began in 1986, connecting researchers and supercomputer centers. Commercial use later expanded the scale and purpose of network connections.","Growth can be additive or multiplicative. Adding the same number each period gives a linear pattern. Multiplying by the same factor gives an exponential pattern. A quantity that doubles repeatedly does not add two each time; it is multiplied by two.","The invented network models below are not historical counts of internet users. They provide a setting for comparing mathematical patterns. Always distinguish a model from the data needed to establish how a real network grew."],"steps":["Identify whether the same amount or the same factor repeats.","Write the initial value at time zero.","For repeated multiplication, use initial value × factor to the number of periods.","Check the first few terms and keep the model’s limits in view."],"worked":{"prompt":"A practice network starts with 5 nodes and doubles each period. Find its size after 3 periods.","steps":["The growth factor is 2.","N(t) = 5 × 2ᵗ.","N(3) = 5 × 8 = 40 nodes."]},"guided":{"stem":"A practice network starts with 4 nodes and doubles three times. How many nodes result?","choices":["32","12","24","10"],"answer":0,"why":"4 × 2³ = 4 × 8 = 32.","skill":"Model repeated growth","id":"L30-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Which sequence represents doubling?","choices":["3, 5, 7, 9","3, 4, 5, 6","3, 6, 9, 12","3, 6, 12, 24"],"answer":3,"why":"Each term is twice the previous term.","skill":"Model repeated growth","id":"L30-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which expression models 10 initial units growing by a factor of 3 each period t?","choices":["3 × 10t","10 × 3ᵗ","30t","10 + 3t"],"answer":1,"why":"The initial value is multiplied by the same factor once per period.","skill":"Model repeated growth","id":"L30-questions-2"},{"stem":"What is 2⁵?","choices":["10","32","25","64"],"answer":1,"why":"2⁵ = 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 32.","skill":"Model repeated growth","id":"L30-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which pattern is linear rather than exponential?","choices":["8, 13, 18, 23","2, 4, 8, 16","5, 10, 20, 40","3, 9, 27, 81"],"answer":0,"why":"The linear pattern adds 5 each time. The others multiply by a constant factor.","skill":"Model repeated growth","id":"L30-questions-4"},{"stem":"For N(t) = 6 × 2ᵗ, what is N(0)?","choices":["12","0","2","6"],"answer":3,"why":"Any nonzero number to the zero power equals 1, so N(0) = 6.","skill":"Model repeated growth","id":"L30-questions-5"},{"stem":"Why is a doubling model not proof of actual historical user counts?","choices":["Exponential expressions are never usable.","A model requires comparison with real data.","Every network must double indefinitely.","Historical counts cannot be measured."],"answer":1,"why":"A mathematical assumption needs empirical support before it describes an actual historical trend.","skill":"Model repeated growth","id":"L30-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new model starts at 7 and triples twice. What is the result?","choices":["42","21","63","49"],"answer":2,"why":"7 × 3² = 7 × 9 = 63.","skill":"Model repeated growth","id":"L30-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H13"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L31","number":31,"title":"Investigating a national turning point","period":"2001–2004","section":"English","skill":"Edit complex historical explanations","era":5,"stage":"Advance","minutes":40,"story":["After the September 11 attacks, public investigations gathered records and testimony to understand what happened and how institutions had acted. The 9/11 Commission's records are part of the evidence historians can examine. A finding, an allegation, a recommendation, and a recollection are different kinds of statements.","Complex explanations need clear grammar and logical relationships. Modifiers should attach to the right subject. Lists should use parallel forms. A sentence should not imply a cause when it has established only an order in time.","Editing for clarity matters especially when the subject is consequential. A concise sentence can still be misleading if it turns uncertainty into certainty or changes who performed an action. The editor's responsibility is to preserve meaning while making the structure easier to follow."],"steps":["Identify the subject of every action.","Keep items in a list in parallel grammatical forms.","Place modifiers close to the words they describe.","Preserve distinctions among evidence, findings, and recommendations."],"worked":{"prompt":"Repair: After reviewing the records, the recommendation became clearer to the researchers.","steps":["The opening modifier should describe the people who reviewed.","Use: After reviewing the records, the researchers clarified the recommendation.","The revised subject matches the opening action."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which sentence avoids a dangling modifier?","choices":["After examining the records, the conclusion was written on paper.","After examining the records, the account revised itself.","After examining the records, a revision was in the room.","After examining the records, the researchers revised the account."],"answer":3,"why":"The researchers are the people doing the examining, so the modifier attaches correctly.","skill":"Edit complex historical explanations","id":"L31-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Choose the parallel list.","choices":["The team collected records, witnesses interviewed, and comparison.","The team collected records, interviewing witnesses, and account comparison.","The team collected records, interviewed witnesses, and compared accounts.","The team collecting records, interviewed witnesses, and to compare accounts."],"answer":2,"why":"All three actions use matching past-tense verbs.","skill":"Edit complex historical explanations","id":"L31-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which revision preserves uncertainty?","choices":["The records prove every possible connection.","No other explanation could ever be considered.","The records suggest a possible connection.","The records remove the need for interpretation."],"answer":2,"why":"Suggest and possible preserve the original caution rather than claiming certainty.","skill":"Edit complex historical explanations","id":"L31-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which transition correctly expresses sequence without asserting causation?","choices":["Afterward","Therefore, necessarily","As the sole cause","For that reason alone"],"answer":0,"why":"Afterward gives an order in time. The other expressions assert a causal relationship.","skill":"Edit complex historical explanations","id":"L31-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which distinction should an accurate editor preserve?","choices":["A recommendation is not a finding about what occurred.","A recollection and an official finding are identical.","All testimony establishes the same uncontested fact.","Every recommendation is proof of a past event."],"answer":0,"why":"A recommendation proposes action; a finding concerns conclusions about evidence.","skill":"Edit complex historical explanations","id":"L31-questions-4"},{"stem":"Choose the clearest sentence.","choices":["Before a conclusion, the accounts of comparison were commission-related.","The commission compared the accounts before reaching a conclusion.","The accounts and conclusion of before were compared.","The reaching of a conclusion was compared before accounts."],"answer":1,"why":"The sentence gives a clear actor, action, and sequence.","skill":"Edit complex historical explanations","id":"L31-questions-5"},{"stem":"Choose the correct punctuation for a nonessential phrase: The report ___ a detailed public document ___ described the findings.","choices":["The report, a detailed public document described the findings.","The report; a detailed public document; described the findings.","The report a detailed public document, described the findings.","The report, a detailed public document, described the findings."],"answer":3,"why":"The nonessential appositive phrase is enclosed by a pair of commas.","skill":"Edit complex historical explanations","id":"L31-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new list says read the source, evaluate the claim, and ___ the reasoning. Choose the parallel completion.","choices":["explain","an explanation of","to have explained","explaining"],"answer":0,"why":"The list uses parallel base-form verbs: read, evaluate, explain.","skill":"Edit complex historical explanations","id":"L31-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H31"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L32","number":32,"title":"Liberty security and a nuanced position","period":"2001–2010","section":"Writing","skill":"Qualify and develop an argument","era":5,"stage":"Advance","minutes":40,"story":["National emergencies raise questions about public action, individual rights, and oversight. Historical inquiry can examine what institutions knew, what they claimed, and what procedures they used. A difficult setting does not remove the need to evaluate evidence.","A nuanced argument identifies conditions and tradeoffs without becoming vague. A writer might support emergency measures while requiring public limits and independent review. Another might place a higher priority on safeguards because exceptional powers can affect people who have not been shown to pose a threat.","The task is to argue, not merely to announce that the issue is complicated. State which principle should guide action, explain why, and identify how that principle responds to another perspective. A qualification should make the position more precise rather than erase it."],"steps":["State the principle that should guide the decision.","Name a condition or limit that matters.","Explain a specific consequence or example.","Engage another perspective and show why your position remains justified."],"worked":{"prompt":"How can a claim favoring emergency action become more precise?","steps":["Specify the kind of threat the action addresses.","Require a defined duration and a review process.","Explain why those limits preserve the capacity to act while allowing oversight."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which thesis is both clear and qualified?","choices":["Every emergency measure is always justified.","Emergency powers need defined limits and independent review.","The issue is complicated, so no position can be taken.","No institution should ever respond to any danger."],"answer":1,"why":"The thesis identifies a principle and practical conditions without claiming an absolute.","skill":"Qualify and develop an argument","id":"L32-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Which sentence develops a reason for review?","choices":["Independent review can test whether the evidence supports continued action.","The word review has six letters.","This paragraph mentions a national issue.","Every reviewer must always agree."],"answer":0,"why":"The sentence explains a mechanism connecting review with justified action.","skill":"Qualify and develop an argument","id":"L32-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which statement is a useful qualification?","choices":["The measure should last only while the stated conditions hold.","Any possible action is equally appropriate.","Something might be different in some way.","No evidence is ever necessary."],"answer":0,"why":"A useful qualification identifies a meaningful condition rather than adding vague uncertainty.","skill":"Qualify and develop an argument","id":"L32-questions-2"},{"stem":"How should a writer address the concern that review may slow action?","choices":["Assume every critic has an unreasonable motive.","Deny that time can matter to anyone.","Explain a review process compatible with timely response.","Repeat the thesis without engaging the concern."],"answer":2,"why":"The response should engage the actual tradeoff and propose a reasoned relationship.","skill":"Qualify and develop an argument","id":"L32-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which example best supports a claim about limits on authority?","choices":["A meeting room has many chairs.","A document is printed in black ink.","A report contains an attractive cover.","A temporary measure requires renewed justification after a set interval."],"answer":3,"why":"The example directly connects public authority with a limit and a need for evidence.","skill":"Qualify and develop an argument","id":"L32-questions-4"},{"stem":"What weakens the sentence All restrictions are identical?","choices":["It contains a plural noun.","It uses a complete sentence.","It ignores differences in scope and conditions.","It can be read aloud."],"answer":2,"why":"Restrictions can differ in who they affect, how long they last, and what evidence supports them.","skill":"Qualify and develop an argument","id":"L32-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which ending most effectively develops the argument?","choices":["Add an unsupported claim that every opponent agrees.","Introduce a new unrelated issue with no explanation.","Replace the argument with a list of dates.","Restate the principle while explaining its broader implication."],"answer":3,"why":"A conclusion can connect the argued principle with what follows from it.","skill":"Qualify and develop an argument","id":"L32-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new argument supports public funding with periodic evaluation. Its qualification concerns:","choices":["The claim that evidence never matters","The impossibility of taking any position","The identity of every future reader","The conditions under which support continues"],"answer":3,"why":"Periodic evaluation sets a condition on continued support.","skill":"Qualify and develop an argument","id":"L32-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H31","H26"],"visual":null,"writing":{"issue":"When public institutions respond to an emergency, what should guide their use of authority?","perspectives":["Rapid action should have priority when a serious threat is identified.","Protection of individual rights should guide even emergency decisions.","Temporary action should be paired with explicit limits and independent review."],"minWords":120,"directions":"Develop your own position and explain its relationship to at least one other perspective. Use a specific example and explain how it supports your reasoning. These are original practice prompts.","rubric":["Ideas and analysis","Development and support","Organization","Language use and conventions"]},"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L33","number":33,"title":"Economic change in more than one step","period":"2007–2009","section":"Mathematics","skill":"Combine percentages and algebra","era":5,"stage":"Advance","minutes":40,"story":["The Great Recession lasted from December 2007 through June 2009 under the standard dating of the business cycle. Employment and housing conditions worsened, and the consequences extended beyond the official recession dates. A period's beginning and ending do not mean that every community experienced recovery at the same time.","This lesson uses invented budgets and index values to practice multi-step mathematics. They are not actual national economic measurements or financial recommendations. The central task is to distinguish the starting value from later values and to decide which operation applies at each step.","A percentage decrease followed by the same percentage increase does not generally return a quantity to its starting value. Each percentage applies to the value at that stage. Careful labeling prevents an appealing shortcut from replacing the actual relationship."],"steps":["List the stages in the order they occur.","Identify the base value for each percentage.","Write a separate expression for each operation.","Check the final value against the original rather than assuming symmetry."],"worked":{"prompt":"A practice index falls from 200 by 10%, then rises by 10%. Where does it end?","steps":["First: 200 × 0.90 = 180.","Second: 180 × 1.10 = 198.","It ends below 200 because the second percentage used a smaller base."]},"guided":{"stem":"A practice budget of $1,000 falls by 20%. What remains?","choices":["$1,200","$200","$980","$800"],"answer":3,"why":"1,000 × 0.80 = 800.","skill":"Combine percentages and algebra","id":"L33-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"That $800 budget then rises by 20%. What is the new amount?","choices":["$820","$960","$640","$1,000"],"answer":1,"why":"The new base is 800. Multiply it by 1.20 to get 960.","skill":"Combine percentages and algebra","id":"L33-questions-1"},{"stem":"A practice unemployment rate changes from 6% to 9%. What is the change in percentage points?","choices":["9 percentage points","15 percentage points","3 percentage points","50 percentage points"],"answer":2,"why":"Subtract the rates: 9 − 6 = 3 percentage points. The relative increase is a different calculation.","skill":"Combine percentages and algebra","id":"L33-questions-2"},{"stem":"What is the relative increase from 6 to 9?","choices":["150%","33.3%","50%","3%"],"answer":2,"why":"The increase is 3 relative to a base of 6. 3/6 = 0.5 = 50%.","skill":"Combine percentages and algebra","id":"L33-questions-3"},{"stem":"An invented grant budget satisfies 250 + 15n = 700. How many units n can it fund?","choices":["28","30","63","45"],"answer":1,"why":"Subtract 250, then divide 450 by 15 to get 30.","skill":"Combine percentages and algebra","id":"L33-questions-4"},{"stem":"An invented index moves from 80 to 100. What percent increase is that?","choices":["80%","25%","20%","125%"],"answer":1,"why":"The increase is 20 divided by the original base 80: 20/80 = 25%.","skill":"Combine percentages and algebra","id":"L33-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which statement about a 10% fall followed by a 10% rise is correct?","choices":["The two changes always cancel exactly.","The final value always doubles.","The original value is irrelevant to any calculation.","The second percentage uses a different base."],"answer":3,"why":"Each percentage acts on the current value, so equal percentages in opposite directions do not generally cancel.","skill":"Combine percentages and algebra","id":"L33-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new practice index of 500 falls 10% and then rises 10%. Find the final index.","choices":["500","450","550","495"],"answer":3,"why":"500 × 0.9 = 450, and 450 × 1.1 = 495.","skill":"Combine percentages and algebra","id":"L33-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H14"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L34","number":34,"title":"Energy models and competing explanations","period":"2000–2020","section":"Science","skill":"Compare predictions with evidence","era":5,"stage":"Advance","minutes":40,"story":["Climate research brings together measurements from many sources and evaluates explanations against that evidence. NASA describes multiple lines of evidence for a warming planet. A scientific claim is strengthened by agreement among relevant observations, not by the confidence with which it is stated.","For practice, consider two invented engineering models for a public building's energy use. Model A predicts that each insulation step saves 5 energy units. Model B predicts that early steps save more, with smaller additional gains later. Students compare both predictions with invented observations made under controlled classroom conditions.","This is a building-energy exercise, not a climate dataset and not a comparison between established climate science and a scientifically unsupported view. The task is to identify which model better matches the given measurements and what further evidence would help."],"steps":["Write each model’s specific prediction.","Compare both predictions with the same observed values.","Measure the size and pattern of discrepancies.","Prefer the better-supported model for this dataset while preserving the limits of the test."],"worked":{"prompt":"If the observation is 12 and model predictions are 10 and 13, which is closer?","steps":["Find absolute differences from the observation.","Model A differs by 2; Model B differs by 1.","Model B is closer for that observation, though one point is limited evidence."]},"guided":{"stem":"At step 2, which model is closer to the observed saving?","choices":["Model A","Model B","Both are equally close","Neither model makes a prediction"],"answer":1,"why":"The observation is 12, compared with A = 10 and B = 12. B matches exactly.","skill":"Compare predictions with evidence","id":"L34-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"At step 3, what is Model A’s absolute error?","choices":["3 units","1 unit","15 units","14 units"],"answer":1,"why":"A predicts 15 and the observation is 14. The absolute difference is 1.","skill":"Compare predictions with evidence","id":"L34-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which pattern does Model B propose?","choices":["Smaller additional savings at later steps","Exactly five additional units at every step","Larger additional savings without limit","No savings at any tested step"],"answer":0,"why":"B goes from 7 to 12 to 15: added savings are 5 and then 3.","skill":"Compare predictions with evidence","id":"L34-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which model has the smaller total absolute error for the three observations?","choices":["Both have a total error of zero","Neither can be compared with data","Model B","Model A"],"answer":2,"why":"A errors are 2, 2, 1 for a total of 5. B errors are 0, 0, 1 for a total of 1.","skill":"Compare predictions with evidence","id":"L34-questions-3"},{"stem":"What would most strengthen a follow-up test?","choices":["Repeat the measurements under documented conditions","Treat a prediction as a measurement without testing it","Use different conditions without recording the changes","Discard every result that disagrees with the preferred model"],"answer":0,"why":"Replication and clear conditions help assess reliability and fairness.","skill":"Compare predictions with evidence","id":"L34-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which conclusion exceeds these practice data?","choices":["A predicts a constant increment in savings.","Model B describes every building in every climate.","The models differ in their predicted pattern.","B fits these three observations more closely."],"answer":1,"why":"The model comparison concerns a limited invented dataset, not all buildings.","skill":"Compare predictions with evidence","id":"L34-questions-5"},{"stem":"Why are the data labeled invented?","choices":["All numerical data about the environment are fictional.","Labels make all observations unnecessary.","They are a classroom exercise, not historical measurements.","The model has no possible educational purpose."],"answer":2,"why":"The label distinguishes an authored reasoning exercise from a claim about real measurements.","skill":"Compare predictions with evidence","id":"L34-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new observation is 20. Models predict 17 and 21. Which is closer?","choices":["The prediction of 21","Neither can be compared","Both are equally close","The prediction of 17"],"answer":0,"why":"The absolute errors are 1 and 3 respectively.","skill":"Compare predictions with evidence","id":"L34-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H32","H12"],"visual":{"kind":"line","title":"Classroom building-energy model","caption":"Invented observations and predictions; energy units are defined only for this exercise.","headers":["Insulation step","Observed saving","Model A","Model B"],"rows":[[1,7,5,7],[2,12,10,12],[3,14,15,15]]},"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L35","number":35,"title":"Remembering the past in public","period":"1861–2020","section":"Reading","skill":"Evaluate claims and assumptions","era":5,"stage":"Advance","minutes":40,"story":["Passage A — Original classroom argument. A public monument can make a historical figure visible in everyday life. Yet visibility is not the same as explanation. A name and a statue may leave out the conflicts, choices, and exclusions that gave an event its meaning. Communities should pair commemoration with enough evidence to make interpretation possible.","Passage B — Original classroom argument. The ability to reinterpret a public site is itself part of democratic life. A community can preserve an object while changing its setting, adding explanations, or placing it in conversation with other accounts. Preservation need not freeze interpretation at the moment an object was first installed.","Both passages treat public memory as an activity rather than a finished list of facts. The first emphasizes explanation; the second emphasizes the possibility of reinterpretation. Neither says that every object must remain in the same place or that removing an object automatically settles a historical debate.","The lesson draws on the long relationship between Civil War memory, public institutions, and later claims about freedom. It asks you to evaluate the reasoning of the two original arguments, not to assume that they are quotations from a historical controversy."],"steps":["Identify the claim and the reasons offered for it.","Notice an assumption connecting a reason to a conclusion.","Compare what the passages allow, require, or leave open.","Reject an interpretation based on an absolute neither passage makes."],"worked":{"prompt":"Do the passages treat preservation as the end of interpretation?","steps":["Passage A asks for explanation beyond visibility.","Passage B explicitly permits new interpretation.","Both leave room for continuing inquiry."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which idea is shared by both passages?","choices":["Historical debate ends when a statue is installed.","Every monument explains itself completely.","Public memory requires interpretation.","All communities must choose one identical policy."],"answer":2,"why":"Both connect public objects with the work of explaining or reinterpreting their meaning.","skill":"Evaluate claims and assumptions","id":"L35-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Passage A assumes that visibility alone may fail to provide:","choices":["Historical understanding","An exact geographic location","An opportunity to see a name","Any physical object"],"answer":0,"why":"The argument distinguishes being visible from being adequately explained.","skill":"Evaluate claims and assumptions","id":"L35-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which action would be compatible with Passage B?","choices":["Forbidding all discussion of a site’s meaning","Assuming preservation requires unchanged explanation","Adding new context to an existing site","Declaring interpretation permanently complete"],"answer":2,"why":"The passage specifically allows new settings, explanations, and relationships.","skill":"Evaluate claims and assumptions","id":"L35-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which claim is not made by either passage?","choices":["Every public object must stay in its original place.","Explanation can matter for understanding.","Commemoration does not settle every question.","A site can receive new interpretation."],"answer":0,"why":"The final paragraph says neither passage requires every object to remain in place.","skill":"Evaluate claims and assumptions","id":"L35-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which evidence would most directly support Passage A?","choices":["Visitors misinterpret a site when context is absent.","A nearby street has several intersections.","A statue is made from a durable material.","The site receives sunlight during the afternoon."],"answer":0,"why":"Evidence of misunderstanding without context bears on the claim that visibility alone is insufficient.","skill":"Evaluate claims and assumptions","id":"L35-questions-4"},{"stem":"How does Passage B extend the discussion?","choices":["It denies the need for any historical evidence.","It replaces the topic with a military timetable.","It considers how interpretation can change.","It proves every preservation decision is identical."],"answer":2,"why":"Its central emphasis is continued reinterpretation rather than the adequacy of an initial explanation.","skill":"Evaluate claims and assumptions","id":"L35-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which conclusion is appropriately limited?","choices":["A monument’s meaning can never change.","All choices automatically produce the same understanding.","One physical change ends every disagreement.","Different choices can still require careful historical reasoning."],"answer":3,"why":"The passages focus on interpretive work, not a single universally sufficient physical choice.","skill":"Evaluate claims and assumptions","id":"L35-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new text argues that an archive needs explanatory labels. Its assumption is most likely that:","choices":["Objects cannot survive without a label.","Every visitor already knows the same history.","Access to objects does not ensure understanding.","All labels must have exactly the same length."],"answer":2,"why":"The reason for explanation is that seeing a source may not be sufficient to interpret it.","skill":"Evaluate claims and assumptions","id":"L35-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H01","H10","H27"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L36","number":36,"title":"A historian’s readiness workshop","period":"1861–2020","section":"Mixed","skill":"Connect evidence across ACT skills","era":5,"stage":"Advance","minutes":40,"story":["A student team is creating an exhibit about the meaning of freedom from the Civil War through the twentieth century. Its first draft contains a proclamation, a Reconstruction amendment, a photograph of a civil rights march, and a later argument about public memory. The sources concern related questions, but they were produced at different times and for different purposes.","The team must make decisions in several areas. It needs a clear central argument, accurate labels, an accessible layout, and an honest explanation of the evidence. It also needs a budget and a plan for evaluating whether visitors understand the exhibit.","A strong project does not use every source as if it says the same thing. It identifies a connection while preserving differences in context. It treats a budget model as a model and a survey as a sample. It revises sentences so visitors can follow the reasoning.","This final workshop combines those habits. The numerical situations are invented for practice. Your goal is to select an appropriate method for each new question and explain a reasoned position about how the exhibit should teach its subject."],"steps":["Identify the skill each question actually requires.","Choose evidence or a method that matches that task.","Check the scope of a conclusion and the units of a calculation.","Explain your decisions rather than treating every source or number as interchangeable."],"worked":{"prompt":"What should the team do before writing one claim about every source?","steps":["Identify each source’s context and purpose.","Find a supported connection without erasing differences.","Keep the exhibit’s argument specific enough for the evidence."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which principle best guides the exhibit’s argument?","choices":["Connect the sources while preserving their differences.","Treat a practice survey as the whole nation.","Use a single date as proof of every claim.","Assume every source has the same purpose."],"answer":0,"why":"The passage calls for relationships that preserve context rather than flattening sources into one meaning.","skill":"Connect evidence across ACT skills","id":"L36-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Choose the correct sentence.","choices":["The collection of sources, support a qualified claim.","The collection supporting a qualified claim.","The collection of sources support a qualified claim.","The collection of sources supports a qualified claim."],"answer":3,"why":"Collection is the singular subject; supports agrees and completes the sentence.","skill":"Connect evidence across ACT skills","id":"L36-questions-1"},{"stem":"An invented exhibit budget is $600. Printing uses 25%. How much remains?","choices":["$750","$575","$150","$450"],"answer":3,"why":"Printing uses 600 × 0.25 = 150. Subtracting leaves 450.","skill":"Connect evidence across ACT skills","id":"L36-questions-2"},{"stem":"A display test changes text size and lighting together. What limits the conclusion?","choices":["The effects of the two factors are difficult to separate.","No visitor could possibly read any display.","A measurement always proves a single cause.","The factors must have identical effects."],"answer":0,"why":"Changing two factors at once makes it harder to isolate which caused a difference.","skill":"Connect evidence across ACT skills","id":"L36-questions-3"},{"stem":"A team surveys only its own members. Why should it limit claims about all visitors?","choices":["The sample contains no possible opinions.","The sample may not represent the intended audience.","All visitors necessarily agree with the team.","Only the most confident response can count."],"answer":1,"why":"Representativeness affects whether a sample can support a population claim.","skill":"Connect evidence across ACT skills","id":"L36-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which addition most strengthens an exhibit label’s argument?","choices":["Replace a qualification with an absolute.","Add a dramatic statement with no source.","Repeat the claim without adding support.","Explain how a specific source supports the claim."],"answer":3,"why":"The added reasoning makes the relationship between evidence and claim explicit.","skill":"Connect evidence across ACT skills","id":"L36-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which statement distinguishes course completion from readiness evidence?","choices":["Completion proves every skill is permanently mastered.","A local six-question set guarantees a 36 ACT score.","One familiar passage represents every ACT text.","Official practice and new work provide additional evidence."],"answer":3,"why":"The local course develops skills. Broader readiness judgments require additional evidence under appropriate conditions.","skill":"Connect evidence across ACT skills","id":"L36-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new exhibit has an invented $400 budget and spends 15% on labels. How much remains?","choices":["$385","$340","$460","$60"],"answer":1,"why":"15% of 400 is 60. Subtract to get 340.","skill":"Connect evidence across ACT skills","id":"L36-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H01","H02","H10"],"visual":null,"writing":{"issue":"What should guide a public exhibit about freedom and citizenship from the Civil War forward?","perspectives":["Present a clear chronological story that gives visitors an accessible foundation.","Center conflicting sources so visitors practice evaluating evidence.","Connect historical events to present questions while explaining differences in context."],"minWords":120,"directions":"Develop your own position and explain its relationship to at least one other perspective. Use a specific example and explain how it supports your reasoning. These are original practice prompts.","rubric":["Ideas and analysis","Development and support","Organization","Language use and conventions"]},"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L37","number":37,"title":"Quadratic models in an expanding nation","period":"1945–1970","section":"Mathematics","skill":"Factor and solve quadratics","era":6,"stage":"Skill lab","minutes":25,"story":["Postwar construction and public engineering required mathematical models of space, motion, and cost. A model could help a planner reason about a relationship, but it needed clear assumptions and units. This laboratory uses invented geometric and training situations rather than actual engineering plans.","A quadratic expression includes a squared variable. Factoring rewrites it as a product, making its structure easier to examine. When a product equals zero, at least one factor must equal zero. Solving a quadratic can therefore produce more than one mathematical answer.","The context decides which solutions are usable. A negative length may solve an equation but fail to describe the physical situation. Check algebra and interpretation separately."],"steps":["Write the equation with zero on one side.","Look for a common factor or a pair of binomial factors.","Set each factor equal to zero.","Check solutions in the original equation and in the context."],"worked":{"prompt":"Solve x² − 7x + 12 = 0.","steps":["The numbers −3 and −4 multiply to 12 and add to −7.","Factor: (x − 3)(x − 4) = 0.","The solutions are x = 3 and x = 4."]},"guided":{"stem":"Factor x² − 5x + 6.","choices":["(x − 2)(x + 3)","(x − 2)(x − 3)","(x − 1)(x − 6)","(x + 2)(x + 3)"],"answer":1,"why":"The factors must multiply to +6 and produce a middle coefficient of −5.","skill":"Factor and solve quadratics","id":"L37-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Solve x² − 9 = 0.","choices":["x = −3 or x = 3","x = 3 only","x = −9 or x = 9","x = 9 only"],"answer":0,"why":"x² = 9 has two real solutions: both 3 and −3 square to 9.","skill":"Factor and solve quadratics","id":"L37-questions-1"},{"stem":"A practice rectangle has width x and length x + 2. Its area is 48. What positive width works?","choices":["4","8","12","6"],"answer":3,"why":"x(x + 2) = 48 gives x² + 2x − 48 = (x + 8)(x − 6). The positive width is 6.","skill":"Factor and solve quadratics","id":"L37-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which roots solve x² − 6x + 8 = 0?","choices":["−1 and −8","2 and 4","1 and 8","−2 and −4"],"answer":1,"why":"The expression factors as (x − 2)(x − 4).","skill":"Factor and solve quadratics","id":"L37-questions-3"},{"stem":"What is the discriminant of x² − 4x + 4 = 0?","choices":["−16","8","16","0"],"answer":3,"why":"b² − 4ac = (−4)² − 4(1)(4) = 16 − 16 = 0.","skill":"Factor and solve quadratics","id":"L37-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which expression equals (x + 3)²?","choices":["x² + 3x + 9","x² + 6x + 9","x² + 6","x² + 9"],"answer":1,"why":"Multiply (x + 3)(x + 3) and combine the two 3x terms.","skill":"Factor and solve quadratics","id":"L37-questions-5"},{"stem":"A length equation gives x = 5 and x = −8. Which is physically usable as a positive length?","choices":["Neither solves any equation","5","−8","Both are positive lengths"],"answer":1,"why":"The context requires a positive length even though both values may be algebraic roots.","skill":"Factor and solve quadratics","id":"L37-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"Solve x² − 8x + 15 = 0.","choices":["−3 and −5","2 and 6","1 and 15","3 and 5"],"answer":3,"why":"Factor as (x − 3)(x − 5) = 0.","skill":"Factor and solve quadratics","id":"L37-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H11","H24"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L38","number":38,"title":"Higher functions and network change","period":"1986–2000","section":"Mathematics","skill":"Use function transformations and logarithms","era":6,"stage":"Skill lab","minutes":25,"story":["The development of research networks offers a setting for thinking about changing quantities. Real networks are complicated; the functions below are invented classroom models. Their purpose is to make mathematical structure visible, not to report historical usage.","Adding a constant to a function shifts all its outputs. Replacing an input with a modified input changes how the function is evaluated. Composition sends the output of one function into another. A logarithm asks for the exponent needed to produce a value.","Careful notation is essential. In f(g(x)), evaluate g first. In log₂(8), ask what power of 2 equals 8. Do not treat every operation as ordinary multiplication."],"steps":["Identify the operation expressed by the notation.","Evaluate inner functions before outer functions.","For a logarithm, rewrite the relationship as an exponential equation.","Check domain restrictions, especially division by zero."],"worked":{"prompt":"If f(x) = 2x + 1 and g(x) = x², find f(g(3)).","steps":["First g(3) = 9.","Then f(9) = 2(9) + 1 = 19.","The order matters: g(f(3)) is a different calculation."]},"guided":{"stem":"If f(x) = 3x − 2, what is f(5)?","choices":["9","15","13","17"],"answer":2,"why":"Substitute 5: 3(5) − 2 = 13.","skill":"Use function transformations and logarithms","id":"L38-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"If f(x) = 2x and g(x) = x + 4, what is f(g(3))?","choices":["6","7","14","10"],"answer":2,"why":"g(3) = 7, then f(7) = 14.","skill":"Use function transformations and logarithms","id":"L38-questions-1"},{"stem":"What is log₂(16)?","choices":["4","2","8","32"],"answer":0,"why":"2⁴ = 16, so the logarithm is 4.","skill":"Use function transformations and logarithms","id":"L38-questions-2"},{"stem":"Compared with y = x², the graph of y = x² + 5 shifts:","choices":["Right 5 units","Up 5 units","Left 5 units","Down 5 units"],"answer":1,"why":"Adding 5 to the output increases every y value by 5.","skill":"Use function transformations and logarithms","id":"L38-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which input is excluded from h(x) = 1/(x − 3)?","choices":["0","1","−3","3"],"answer":3,"why":"At x = 3 the denominator is zero, making the expression undefined.","skill":"Use function transformations and logarithms","id":"L38-questions-4"},{"stem":"For f(x) = x², what is f(−4)?","choices":["16","8","−16","−8"],"answer":0,"why":"Squaring −4 gives (−4)(−4) = 16.","skill":"Use function transformations and logarithms","id":"L38-questions-5"},{"stem":"If 3ᵗ = 27, what is t?","choices":["9","4","24","3"],"answer":3,"why":"3³ = 27.","skill":"Use function transformations and logarithms","id":"L38-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new pair is f(x) = x + 2 and g(x) = 3x. Find f(g(4)).","choices":["6","18","14","12"],"answer":2,"why":"g(4) = 12 and f(12) = 14.","skill":"Use function transformations and logarithms","id":"L38-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H13"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L39","number":39,"title":"Railroad surveys and right triangles","period":"1869–1900","section":"Mathematics","skill":"Apply similarity and trigonometry","era":6,"stage":"Skill lab","minutes":25,"story":["Railroads required routes, bridges, and careful measurements. Surveying connected observations in the landscape with mathematical relationships. This laboratory uses invented triangles to practice those relationships; it is not a set of historical survey measurements.","Similar triangles have matching angle measures and proportional corresponding sides. In a right triangle, sine, cosine, and tangent connect an angle with ratios of side lengths. Label opposite, adjacent, and hypotenuse relative to the angle named in the problem.","A diagram can make the relationship clear, but a sketch may not be drawn to scale. Use stated measurements rather than judging a length from appearance."],"steps":["Identify the angle and label the sides relative to it.","Use similarity for matching shapes or SOH CAH TOA for a right triangle.","Substitute into the correct ratio.","Check the result and the units."],"worked":{"prompt":"A right triangle has opposite side 3, adjacent side 4, and hypotenuse 5. Find sin θ.","steps":["Sine is opposite divided by hypotenuse.","sin θ = 3/5.","Using 3/4 would instead give tangent."]},"guided":{"stem":"In a right triangle with opposite 5 and hypotenuse 13, what is sin θ?","choices":["5/13","13/5","5/12","12/13"],"answer":0,"why":"Sine is opposite divided by hypotenuse.","skill":"Apply similarity and trigonometry","id":"L39-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"A right triangle has opposite 8 and adjacent 6. What is tan θ?","choices":["5/4","4/5","4/3","3/4"],"answer":2,"why":"Tangent = opposite/adjacent = 8/6 = 4/3.","skill":"Apply similarity and trigonometry","id":"L39-questions-1"},{"stem":"Similar triangles have a scale factor of 3. A side of length 7 corresponds to what length?","choices":["21","10","49","4"],"answer":0,"why":"Multiply the corresponding side by the scale factor: 7 × 3 = 21.","skill":"Apply similarity and trigonometry","id":"L39-questions-2"},{"stem":"A triangle has angles 35° and 65°. What is the third angle?","choices":["80°","100°","90°","70°"],"answer":0,"why":"Triangle angles total 180°: 180 − 35 − 65 = 80.","skill":"Apply similarity and trigonometry","id":"L39-questions-3"},{"stem":"A circle has radius 4 units. What is its area?","choices":["8π square units","16π square units","4π square units","32π square units"],"answer":1,"why":"Circle area is πr² = π(4²) = 16π.","skill":"Apply similarity and trigonometry","id":"L39-questions-4"},{"stem":"If cos θ = 4/5 and the hypotenuse is 20, what is the adjacent side?","choices":["25","16","12","4"],"answer":1,"why":"Adjacent = hypotenuse × cosine = 20 × 4/5 = 16.","skill":"Apply similarity and trigonometry","id":"L39-questions-5"},{"stem":"Why should you avoid measuring a sketch with a ruler when it says not to scale?","choices":["The angles must all be equal.","All stated numbers must be ignored.","Its appearance may not match the stated ratios.","Geometry cannot use any drawings."],"answer":2,"why":"The problem’s stated measurements control the reasoning; the sketch may be only illustrative.","skill":"Apply similarity and trigonometry","id":"L39-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new right triangle has opposite 9 and hypotenuse 15. Find sin θ.","choices":["3/5","4/5","5/3","3/4"],"answer":0,"why":"9/15 simplifies to 3/5.","skill":"Apply similarity and trigonometry","id":"L39-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H18"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L40","number":40,"title":"Convoys vectors and matrices","period":"1941–1945","section":"Mathematics","skill":"Reason with number and quantity","era":6,"stage":"Skill lab","minutes":25,"story":["Wartime logistics required keeping track of quantities and directions. A table could organize several types of supplies, while a vector could represent a movement with both size and direction. The invented examples here are mathematical models, not actual convoy plans.","A vector written (x, y) can describe movement along two perpendicular directions. Add vectors by adding corresponding components. A matrix organizes numbers into rows and columns; addition also operates on corresponding entries. Complex numbers use i, where i² = −1.","The notation may look unfamiliar, but the first step is always to identify what each position means. A row or component cannot be moved arbitrarily without changing the represented information."],"steps":["Identify the meaning of each component, row, or column.","Combine only corresponding positions.","Use i² = −1 when simplifying complex expressions.","Keep the representation consistent when interpreting the result."],"worked":{"prompt":"A practice route moves (3, 2) and then (4, −1). What is the total vector?","steps":["Add first components: 3 + 4 = 7.","Add second components: 2 + (−1) = 1.","The combined vector is (7, 1)."]},"guided":{"stem":"Add (2, 5) and (−1, 3).","choices":["(3, 2)","(1, 2)","(−2, 15)","(1, 8)"],"answer":3,"why":"Add corresponding components: 2 − 1 = 1 and 5 + 3 = 8.","skill":"Reason with number and quantity","id":"L40-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"What is the magnitude of vector (3, 4)?","choices":["25","7","5","1"],"answer":2,"why":"Use √(3² + 4²) = √25 = 5.","skill":"Reason with number and quantity","id":"L40-questions-1"},{"stem":"Add the first rows (2, 4) and (5, 1) of two same-size matrices.","choices":["(10, 4)","(3, −3)","(7, 5)","(5, 7)"],"answer":2,"why":"Matrix addition adds corresponding entries, giving 2 + 5 and 4 + 1.","skill":"Reason with number and quantity","id":"L40-questions-2"},{"stem":"Simplify (3 + 2i) + (4 − 5i).","choices":["7 − 3i","12 − 10i","7 + 7i","−1 + 7i"],"answer":0,"why":"Combine real parts and imaginary parts separately: 3 + 4 = 7 and 2i − 5i = −3i.","skill":"Reason with number and quantity","id":"L40-questions-3"},{"stem":"What does i² equal?","choices":["1","2i","0","−1"],"answer":3,"why":"The imaginary unit is defined by i² = −1.","skill":"Reason with number and quantity","id":"L40-questions-4"},{"stem":"What is 3(2, −4)?","choices":["(6, −4)","(5, −1)","(2, −12)","(6, −12)"],"answer":3,"why":"Scalar multiplication applies to each component.","skill":"Reason with number and quantity","id":"L40-questions-5"},{"stem":"Why must matrix dimensions match for addition?","choices":["Rows and columns are interchangeable in every operation.","Every matrix must contain only positive numbers.","Each entry needs a corresponding position.","Addition always changes the number of rows."],"answer":2,"why":"Entry-by-entry addition requires matching shapes.","skill":"Reason with number and quantity","id":"L40-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new pair of vectors is (5, 1) and (−2, 6). Find their sum.","choices":["(3, 5)","(3, 7)","(−10, 6)","(7, −5)"],"answer":1,"why":"Add corresponding components: 5 − 2 = 3 and 1 + 6 = 7.","skill":"Reason with number and quantity","id":"L40-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H25"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L41","number":41,"title":"Sampling a changing community","period":"1889–2020","section":"Mathematics","skill":"Evaluate samples and conditional probability","era":6,"stage":"Skill lab","minutes":25,"story":["Historians use population records and surveys to ask questions about changing communities. Such evidence is valuable because it describes particular people under particular collection rules. A well-organized table does not automatically make a sample representative.","This laboratory uses an invented survey at a community history event. The table groups 40 visitors by whether they attended a workshop and whether they visited an exhibit. Conditional probability restricts the denominator to the group named after given that.","An association in this table does not show that attending a workshop caused a person to visit the exhibit. Visitors may differ in interest, available time, or other characteristics. Mathematical accuracy and a careful historical conclusion are separate responsibilities."],"steps":["Identify the population and the sampled group.","For a conditional question, restrict the denominator to the given group.","Count outcomes satisfying the requested condition.","Avoid treating association as an isolated causal effect."],"worked":{"prompt":"Among the 20 workshop attendees, 15 visited the exhibit. What proportion visited?","steps":["The condition limits the denominator to workshop attendees.","Use 15/20, not 15/40.","15/20 = 3/4 = 75%."]},"guided":{"stem":"What fraction of all 40 surveyed visitors attended a workshop?","choices":["1/2","3/4","1/4","3/8"],"answer":0,"why":"The table has 20 workshop attendees out of 40 total visitors.","skill":"Evaluate samples and conditional probability","id":"L41-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Given workshop attendance, what is the probability of an exhibit visit?","choices":["1/2","3/8","1/4","3/4"],"answer":3,"why":"Among 20 workshop attendees, 15 visited: 15/20 = 3/4.","skill":"Evaluate samples and conditional probability","id":"L41-questions-1"},{"stem":"How many surveyed visitors visited the exhibit?","choices":["25","15","20","40"],"answer":2,"why":"Add exhibit visitors across groups: 15 + 5 = 20.","skill":"Evaluate samples and conditional probability","id":"L41-questions-2"},{"stem":"Given no workshop attendance, what fraction visited the exhibit?","choices":["1/8","1/2","1/4","3/4"],"answer":2,"why":"Among 20 nonattendees, 5 visited the exhibit: 5/20 = 1/4.","skill":"Evaluate samples and conditional probability","id":"L41-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which conclusion is supported?","choices":["The sample represents every resident of the United States.","Exhibit visits were more common among workshop attendees in this sample.","Workshops caused every exhibit visit.","No nonattendee visited the exhibit."],"answer":1,"why":"The conditional rates differ in the sample. Causation and national representation are not established.","skill":"Evaluate samples and conditional probability","id":"L41-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which sampling method would better represent an event’s visitors?","choices":["Include only people who praise the exhibit.","Select visitors across different times using a planned method.","Survey only the organizer’s closest friends.","Stop after one unusually enthusiastic visitor."],"answer":1,"why":"A planned selection across relevant groups and times can reduce selection bias.","skill":"Evaluate samples and conditional probability","id":"L41-questions-5"},{"stem":"What is the probability that a randomly chosen surveyed visitor both attended a workshop and visited the exhibit?","choices":["3/8","3/4","1/4","1/2"],"answer":0,"why":"There are 15 visitors satisfying both conditions out of 40 total: 15/40 = 3/8.","skill":"Evaluate samples and conditional probability","id":"L41-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new survey includes 12 exhibit visitors among 30 attendees. Given attendance, what fraction visited?","choices":["5/2","3/5","1/4","2/5"],"answer":3,"why":"12/30 simplifies to 2/5.","skill":"Evaluate samples and conditional probability","id":"L41-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H04"],"visual":{"kind":"table","title":"Community history event survey","caption":"Invented sample for probability practice. Forty surveyed visitors; not a historical population record.","headers":["Group","Exhibit yes","Exhibit no","Total"],"rows":[["Workshop",15,5,20],["No workshop",5,15,20],["Total",20,20,40]]},"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L42","number":42,"title":"A public argument that stays focused","period":"1933–1941","section":"English","skill":"Evaluate relevance and rhetorical purpose","era":6,"stage":"Skill lab","minutes":25,"story":["Original practice paragraph: A conservation crew restored an eroded path and built drainage channels. The work gave participants income while improving a public resource. [Sentence to add.] The project therefore combined an immediate employment goal with a longer-term public benefit.","An editor is considering this addition: The crew also learned to measure slopes and organize materials. The sentence is relevant if the paragraph's purpose includes the skills and public benefits associated with the work. A sentence about the color of a distant building would contribute little to that purpose.","Rhetorical questions ask whether a choice serves a stated goal. Several choices may be grammatically possible. The best answer still has to develop the particular argument the writer is making. Editing the paragraph requires understanding its purpose before judging individual sentences."],"steps":["State the paragraph’s specific purpose.","Identify what the proposed sentence adds.","Decide whether the addition develops, repeats, contradicts, or distracts.","Choose the revision that serves the stated goal while preserving accuracy."],"worked":{"prompt":"Should the writer add a sentence explaining useful skills learned by participants?","steps":["The paragraph discusses benefits of the project.","Skills learned would add another relevant benefit.","The sentence can support the purpose if it does not replace the required employment and public-resource connections."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which addition best develops the paragraph’s public-benefit focus?","choices":["A distant city had a different street layout.","The crew leader preferred one color of shirt.","The drainage reduced damage to the trail during heavy rain.","A modern report uses a different font."],"answer":2,"why":"The sentence describes a specific public benefit of the improvement.","skill":"Evaluate relevance and rhetorical purpose","id":"L42-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Which addition would mainly distract from the stated purpose?","choices":["A sentence about the drainage channels’ function","A detail about improved access to the path","A paragraph about the history of an unrelated building","An explanation of participants’ income"],"answer":2,"why":"The unrelated building does not develop the paragraph’s combined employment and public-benefit purpose.","skill":"Evaluate relevance and rhetorical purpose","id":"L42-questions-1"},{"stem":"What should an editor do before judging a sentence’s relevance?","choices":["Count only the punctuation marks","Choose the longest available sentence","Assume every fact belongs in every paragraph","Identify the paragraph’s goal"],"answer":3,"why":"A fact can be accurate but irrelevant to a particular purpose.","skill":"Evaluate relevance and rhetorical purpose","id":"L42-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which conclusion best preserves the paragraph’s argument?","choices":["The work had no connection with employment.","The project addressed both present need and future use.","The project solved every economic problem nationwide.","No participant benefited from any public project."],"answer":1,"why":"The conclusion synthesizes the two benefits without making an unsupported universal claim.","skill":"Evaluate relevance and rhetorical purpose","id":"L42-questions-3"},{"stem":"A sentence repeats a benefit already explained with no added detail. Its main weakness is:","choices":["Contradictory verb tense by definition","Lack of any possible subject","Redundancy","Proof that the paragraph has no topic"],"answer":2,"why":"Repetition without development can weaken concision and focus.","skill":"Evaluate relevance and rhetorical purpose","id":"L42-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which transition introduces a second related benefit?","choices":["Despite that contradiction","In addition","For an unrelated reason","On the contrary"],"answer":1,"why":"In addition signals another supporting point.","skill":"Evaluate relevance and rhetorical purpose","id":"L42-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which revision best limits an overstatement about one local project?","choices":["The project proved no other action was needed.","The project contributed to local improvements.","The project guaranteed identical benefits everywhere.","The project ended every national problem."],"answer":1,"why":"Contributed and local preserve a defensible scale.","skill":"Evaluate relevance and rhetorical purpose","id":"L42-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new paragraph argues that an archive improves public access. Which detail is relevant?","choices":["An unrelated bridge has six supports.","A visitor’s car was parked elsewhere.","It provides searchable descriptions of its collections.","Its director once owned a blue coat."],"answer":2,"why":"Searchable descriptions directly explain how access improves.","skill":"Evaluate relevance and rhetorical purpose","id":"L42-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H24"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L43","number":43,"title":"Citizenship across two arguments","period":"1868–1965","section":"Reading","skill":"Compare complex claims and assumptions","era":6,"stage":"Skill lab","minutes":25,"story":["Passage A — Original classroom argument. A constitutional guarantee changes the terms of a public dispute. Once equal protection is written into the Constitution, a claim about unfair treatment can appeal to a national commitment rather than only to a local preference. That change is important even when institutions fail to honor the commitment. A broken promise remains a promise against which conduct can be judged.","Passage B — Original classroom argument. A right that cannot be exercised is an incomplete achievement. Legal language matters, but its practical meaning depends on access to institutions, enforcement, and the ability to challenge exclusion. The history of voting rights after Reconstruction shows why announcing a principle and securing its operation are related but separate tasks.","The Fourteenth Amendment and the Voting Rights Act of 1965 arose in different historical settings. These passages do not claim that the two measures were identical or that one event explains every later change. They offer two ways of evaluating legal reform: as a public standard and as a practical arrangement.","Compare the reasoning before choosing a winner. A disagreement about emphasis is not necessarily a direct contradiction. Both authors may recognize the value of legal language while asking different questions about what makes a reform effective."],"steps":["State each author’s claim without making it broader.","Identify the assumption connecting the reason to the claim.","Test whether one passage denies the other’s actual position.","Distinguish a different emphasis from a direct contradiction."],"worked":{"prompt":"Do the authors necessarily disagree about whether legal language matters?","steps":["A treats a guarantee as a standard for judging conduct.","B explicitly says legal language matters, then asks about its operation.","Their emphases differ; neither says the other concern has no value."]},"guided":{"stem":"Passage A mainly treats a constitutional guarantee as:","choices":["A description of existing institutional behavior","A procedure for resolving an individual case","A measure of how often people exercise a right","A standard for evaluating public conduct"],"answer":3,"why":"The promise supplies a standard against which conduct can be judged. It is not a description of actual compliance, a case procedure, or a participation count.","skill":"Compare complex claims and assumptions","id":"L43-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Passage B adds which condition to an assessment of reform?","choices":["People’s practical ability to exercise the right","The breadth of agreement when a guarantee is adopted","The length of time a guarantee remains in a document","The distinction between a national and a local promise"],"answer":0,"why":"B emphasizes access, enforcement, and challenges to exclusion. The other conditions concern adoption, duration, or the distinction emphasized in A.","skill":"Compare complex claims and assumptions","id":"L43-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which assumption most directly supports Passage A?","choices":["The public value of a guarantee depends on immediate compliance.","The primary benefit of a guarantee is a rise in participation.","A national commitment describes the conduct of local institutions.","A stated commitment can be useful even when violated."],"answer":3,"why":"A uses the promise as a basis for judging conduct, so its value need not depend on perfect compliance. Participation and existing local conduct are different questions.","skill":"Compare complex claims and assumptions","id":"L43-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which observation would most directly concern Passage B?","choices":["Historians compare the wording of two legal documents.","Advocates cite a guarantee while making a public claim.","An amendment establishes a new national legal standard.","Eligible voters face barriers despite a formal guarantee."],"answer":3,"why":"The gap between a guarantee and actual exercise directly concerns B. The other observations concern standards, language, or interpretation without showing practical access.","skill":"Compare complex claims and assumptions","id":"L43-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which synthesis best preserves both arguments?","choices":["Practical access is the evidence that gives legal language its value.","A reform’s public meaning depends chiefly on the date of its adoption.","Legal standards and practical enforcement can matter together.","A public promise should be judged apart from its institutional effects."],"answer":2,"why":"The synthesis preserves A’s independent value for a standard and B’s concern with operation. The first alternative makes practical access a condition of the language’s value; the second omits B’s concern.","skill":"Compare complex claims and assumptions","id":"L43-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which statement would both authors likely reject?","choices":["Conduct can fall short of a stated principle.","Writing a guarantee settles every question about a right.","A guarantee can influence later public arguments.","Institutions affect how a right is experienced."],"answer":1,"why":"A acknowledges broken promises, while B distinguishes announcement from operation.","skill":"Compare complex claims and assumptions","id":"L43-questions-5"},{"stem":"Why does the context distinguish 1868 from 1965?","choices":["To prevent a comparison from erasing historical differences","To establish which author has the more reliable evidence","To show that the authors are describing one continuous legal procedure","To explain why the later measure replaced the earlier standard"],"answer":0,"why":"The context preserves distinct settings and purposes. It does not rank the authors, say one measure replaced the other, or identify a single legal procedure.","skill":"Compare complex claims and assumptions","id":"L43-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new author values a law as a public benchmark but questions its enforcement. That position is:","choices":["Compatible with both concerns in these passages","A denial that any standard has value","Proof that the passages are exact opposites","A claim that historical context never matters"],"answer":0,"why":"A benchmark reflects A’s concern, and questioning enforcement reflects B’s concern.","skill":"Compare complex claims and assumptions","id":"L43-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H02","H27"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L44","number":44,"title":"A better test of wartime materials","period":"1941–1945","section":"Science","skill":"Revise a study and evaluate uncertainty","era":6,"stage":"Skill lab","minutes":25,"story":["Wartime production raised practical questions about materials, storage, and transport. Engineers needed to distinguish a useful pattern from a difference produced by an unfair comparison. This laboratory uses an invented model of protective packing; its values are not measurements from a historical military study.","Three packing materials were tested using equal-size model containers and the same load. Each container was dropped from the same height. Researchers measured how far an internal marker moved. Smaller movement indicated greater protection in this particular model. Three trials were completed for each material.","The table shows variation within each material as well as differences between materials. A useful conclusion considers both. A follow-up study could test additional heights, use more trials, and document how measurements were taken. It should change one planned factor at a time if the goal is to isolate that factor’s effect.","Even a well-controlled model has boundaries. Results for these containers and loads do not establish performance for every shipment, weather condition, or real wartime use. Identify what the study measures before deciding what it proves."],"steps":["Identify the changed factor and the measured response.","Compare repeated observations, including their variability.","Check controls and possible confounding factors.","Propose a revision that answers a specific remaining question."],"worked":{"prompt":"Material A has movements of 7, 8, and 9 mm. What is its mean?","steps":["Add the three measurements: 24 mm.","Divide by the three trials: 24/3 = 8 mm.","Interpret the mean within this model, not as a universal protection rating."]},"guided":{"stem":"What factor differs deliberately across the three groups?","choices":["Drop height","Packing material","Load mass","Container size"],"answer":1,"why":"The materials differ, while the stated size, height, and load are held constant.","skill":"Revise a study and evaluate uncertainty","id":"L44-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Which material has the lowest mean marker movement?","choices":["Material B","All have the same mean","Material A","Material C"],"answer":0,"why":"The means are A = 8, B = 4, C = 6 mm. Smaller is better protection in this model.","skill":"Revise a study and evaluate uncertainty","id":"L44-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which material has the greatest range across trials?","choices":["Material C","Material B","All have the same range","Material A"],"answer":0,"why":"C ranges from 3 to 9 mm, a range of 6. A ranges by 2 and B by 2.","skill":"Revise a study and evaluate uncertainty","id":"L44-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which change would help isolate the effect of drop height?","choices":["Test several heights with material and load held constant","Change height, material, and load together","Use a different measuring method for each height","Remove measurements that conflict with a prediction"],"answer":0,"why":"Holding other relevant conditions constant helps separate the effect of height.","skill":"Revise a study and evaluate uncertainty","id":"L44-questions-3"},{"stem":"Which statement best describes Material C’s results?","choices":["It has the lowest mean and no variation.","Its results prove the measuring tool never varies.","Every trial exceeds every result for Material A.","Its mean is intermediate, with more variation than the others."],"answer":3,"why":"C’s mean is 6, between 4 and 8, while its range is largest.","skill":"Revise a study and evaluate uncertainty","id":"L44-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which conclusion stays within the study’s scope?","choices":["Material C’s largest range means it had the largest mean.","Material A would outperform B if the drop height increased.","Material B had the lowest average movement in this model.","Material B’s smaller mean shows it used a lighter load."],"answer":2,"why":"The limited conclusion describes the measured outcome. The load was controlled, range is not mean, and additional heights were not tested.","skill":"Revise a study and evaluate uncertainty","id":"L44-questions-5"},{"stem":"Why might researchers record instrument precision?","choices":["To correct for every difference among container designs","To determine which material had the greatest mass","To establish the historical origin of each tested material","To interpret how much a small measured difference means"],"answer":3,"why":"Precision concerns the resolution of measurements. It does not by itself measure mass, remove design differences, or establish provenance.","skill":"Revise a study and evaluate uncertainty","id":"L44-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new model varies load mass but keeps packing and height constant. The independent variable is:","choices":["Packing material","Load mass","Marker movement","The number of words in the report"],"answer":1,"why":"Load mass is deliberately changed; marker movement is the response.","skill":"Revise a study and evaluate uncertainty","id":"L44-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H25"],"visual":{"kind":"table","title":"Protective packing model","caption":"Invented classroom data. Marker movement in millimeters; three repeated trials per material.","headers":["Material","Trial 1 (mm)","Trial 2 (mm)","Trial 3 (mm)"],"rows":[["A",7,8,9],["B",3,4,5],["C",3,6,9]]},"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L45","number":45,"title":"Reading a changing industrial town","period":"1970–2000","section":"Reading","skill":"Integrate a passage and quantitative evidence","era":6,"stage":"Skill lab","minutes":25,"story":["Original practice passage. A town’s former mill once supplied wages, apprenticeships, and a shared sense of identity. When production declined, residents debated how the town should describe the change. Some wanted an exhibit centered on loss. Others argued that such an exhibit would miss new enterprises and the efforts of people who stayed.","A local student researcher proposed a more careful account: The decline changed the community, but no single measure can describe the entire experience. An employment index could show a pattern in one sector while missing unpaid care, migration, changes in job quality, or the distribution of new opportunities. A trend is evidence to interpret, not a complete explanation by itself.","The invented graph below models two employment categories in a fictional industrial town. Both indexes begin at 100 in 1970, so they show change relative to each category’s own starting level. They do not report equal numbers of workers. The historical setting reflects debates about industrial change; the town, figures, and statements are original classroom constructions.","Use the passage and graph together. The passage identifies the limits of a single measure. The graph supplies one bounded pattern. Neither establishes the entire cause of change or the experience of every resident."],"steps":["Read the caption and identify what an index represents.","Describe the visible pattern without inventing a cause.","Connect that pattern with the author’s qualified claim.","Check whether a conclusion needs information absent from both sources."],"worked":{"prompt":"If an index falls from 100 to 70, what does it show?","steps":["It is 30 points below its own baseline.","The change is a 30% decline relative to that baseline.","It does not supply an actual worker count without a starting quantity."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which pattern appears in the invented graph?","choices":["Manufacturing loses more actual workers than services gain.","The manufacturing index declines most sharply from 1990 to 2000.","Manufacturing falls while services rise relative to their baselines.","The two categories differ by 85 actual workers in 2000."],"answer":2,"why":"The graph gives relative indexes: 100 to 55 and 100 to 140. It gives no actual counts. The largest manufacturing decline is 20 index points from 1980 to 1990, compared with 15 points in the final decade.","skill":"Integrate a passage and quantitative evidence","id":"L45-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Why does an index of 100 in both categories not establish equal worker counts?","choices":["Each category is measured using a different unit of time.","The graph omits a separate row for the town’s population.","The later service growth changes the earlier worker counts.","Each category uses its own starting quantity as a baseline."],"answer":3,"why":"Equal normalized indexes can represent different actual starting quantities. Missing population is not the central issue; the same years are used, and later values do not alter the baseline.","skill":"Integrate a passage and quantitative evidence","id":"L45-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which claim is supported by both passage and graph?","choices":["One sector’s decline can coexist with growth in another.","New service positions compensated for the town’s lost wages.","Most former mill workers moved directly into service work.","The town’s total employment was greater by the year 2000."],"answer":0,"why":"The sources support coexisting trends. Wages, individual transitions, and actual totals would require additional evidence.","skill":"Integrate a passage and quantitative evidence","id":"L45-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which question requires evidence beyond the graph?","choices":["What manufacturing index is shown for 1990?","Did the service index rise after 1970?","Did residents have equal access to the new opportunities?","Which category’s index is lower in 2000?"],"answer":2,"why":"The graph provides category indexes, not the distribution of access among residents.","skill":"Integrate a passage and quantitative evidence","id":"L45-questions-3"},{"stem":"What is the function of the list beginning with unpaid care?","choices":["To propose a replacement formula for the employment index","To identify experiences an employment index can omit","To explain the numerical gap between the two plotted lines","To give the main causes of the manufacturing decline"],"answer":1,"why":"The list identifies dimensions that a measure may miss. It does not calculate a gap, supply a formula, or isolate the causes of decline.","skill":"Integrate a passage and quantitative evidence","id":"L45-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which title best reflects the researcher’s position?","choices":["Measuring Recovery with a Rising Service Index","Preserving the Town’s Industrial Identity Through Loss","Economic Change Through More Than One Measure","Tracing the Transfer of Mill Workers into Services"],"answer":2,"why":"The researcher favors a broader account. The first title implies worker-level transitions not documented; the others privilege a single narrative or measure.","skill":"Integrate a passage and quantitative evidence","id":"L45-questions-5"},{"stem":"Which inference would exceed the supplied evidence?","choices":["A single policy caused every change in both indexes.","The passage favors a qualified account of community change.","The service index is above its 1970 baseline in 2000.","The manufacturing index is below its baseline in 2000."],"answer":0,"why":"The sources do not isolate a policy’s causal effect.","skill":"Integrate a passage and quantitative evidence","id":"L45-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new graph normalizes two school clubs to 100 in September. What additional fact is needed to compare actual membership?","choices":["The number of grid lines in the graph","Each club’s September membership","Only the color used for each line","Only the title’s number of words"],"answer":1,"why":"Baseline counts are needed to convert each normalized index into actual membership.","skill":"Integrate a passage and quantitative evidence","id":"L45-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H30"],"visual":{"kind":"line","title":"Two employment indexes in a fictional town","caption":"Invented data, not a historical series. Each category’s own 1970 level = 100; no actual worker counts are supplied.","headers":["Year","Manufacturing index","Service index"],"rows":[[1970,100,100],[1980,90,110],[1990,70,125],[2000,55,140]]},"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L46","number":46,"title":"A sustained argument about public history","period":"1861–2020","section":"Writing","skill":"Develop and revise a complex argument","era":6,"stage":"Skill lab","minutes":25,"story":["A community is planning an exhibit about freedom and citizenship from the Civil War forward. Its archive includes legal documents, personal accounts, photographs, and later interpretations. The planning group agrees that the exhibit should be accessible but disagrees about how much uncertainty and conflict it should present.","One position favors a clear central narrative so visitors leave with a usable foundation. A second favors contrasting sources so visitors can see why historians reach different conclusions. A third argues that the exhibit should connect past choices to present questions while carefully explaining changes in context.","A strong argument can use elements of more than one position. It should still make a clear claim, develop reasons, explain an example, and engage a meaningful alternative. A counterargument is useful when it identifies a real concern and changes or clarifies the writer’s reasoning. Merely calling another view wrong does not supply analysis.","For this extension, plan before drafting and revise after reading the whole response. The course’s minimum length is only a completion requirement. A developed essay usually needs more space. For a separate full-length rehearsal, your teacher may provide the official ACT Writing directions and a 40-minute session."],"steps":["Plan a claim, two connected reasons, a specific example, and a meaningful alternative.","Explain how the example supports the claim; do not just name an event.","Evaluate the alternative fairly and refine your position if needed.","Revise the whole argument for development, organization, and precise language."],"worked":{"prompt":"How can a writer answer the concern that contrasting sources may confuse visitors?","steps":["Acknowledge the need for clear guidance rather than dismissing the concern.","Propose labels that explain each source’s context and the disagreement.","Show how the proposal preserves both accessibility and critical inquiry."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which thesis makes a clear and qualified recommendation?","choices":["The archive contains several types of sources about freedom and citizenship.","A useful exhibit can be evaluated by asking how well it presents history.","Visitors and curators may differ about the interpretation of public records.","Pair a clear narrative with contrasting sources and explanatory labels."],"answer":3,"why":"The thesis recommends a specific approach. The alternatives describe contents, give a circular evaluation standard, or identify a disagreement without taking a position.","skill":"Develop and revise a complex argument","id":"L46-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Which sentence develops evidence rather than merely naming it?","choices":["The archive includes legal guarantees, later enforcement records, photographs, and personal accounts.","One section could present an amendment, while the next section could display a later document.","Comparing a guarantee with a later enforcement debate reveals a gap between principle and practice.","The Fourteenth Amendment and a later enforcement debate are two examples that could appear in the exhibit."],"answer":2,"why":"The sentence explains what the comparison reveals. The alternatives identify materials or their placement without interpreting their relationship.","skill":"Develop and revise a complex argument","id":"L46-questions-1"},{"stem":"Which counterargument directly challenges the proposal to foreground contrasting sources?","choices":["Visitors may need a clear sequence before comparing sources.","Explanatory labels can clarify the context of individual documents.","Contrasting sources give visitors more than one perspective.","A short chronology can help visitors locate a document in time."],"answer":0,"why":"The need for a sequence raises a limitation of foregrounding comparison. The alternatives support comparison or propose a way to make it work.","skill":"Develop and revise a complex argument","id":"L46-questions-2"},{"stem":"Which response engages that counterargument?","choices":["Provide a short chronology before the contrasting-source displays.","Explain that the archive has acquired several additional collections.","Add more contrasting documents to the existing opening display.","Describe how the current exhibit differs from a neighboring museum."],"answer":0,"why":"A chronology addresses missing background. More documents may intensify the problem, while acquisition and comparison with another museum do not answer it.","skill":"Develop and revise a complex argument","id":"L46-questions-3"},{"stem":"A paragraph lists three events but never connects them to the thesis. What should revision add?","choices":["A sentence announcing that the examples are important","A transition naming the next example in the sequence","A more detailed date and location for each event","Reasoning that connects the examples to the claim"],"answer":3,"why":"Development requires an explicit relationship between evidence and the argument. Added details, transitions, or an announcement of importance do not supply that reasoning.","skill":"Develop and revise a complex argument","id":"L46-questions-4"},{"stem":"Which revision most precisely limits the claim to a possible benefit of the proposed design?","choices":["Visitors can evaluate sources because this design gives them context.","This design improves how visitors evaluate sources in public exhibits.","This design may help visitors evaluate the selected sources.","The selected sources establish the design’s value for museum visitors."],"answer":2,"why":"May names a possible benefit and selected sources limits its scope. The alternatives assert a demonstrated effect, a causal explanation, or an established value that the proposal has not yet tested.","skill":"Develop and revise a complex argument","id":"L46-questions-5"},{"stem":"What is the best purpose for a concluding paragraph?","choices":["Synthesize the reasoning and clarify the recommendation’s significance","List the historical examples again in the order they first appeared","Summarize the three perspectives without evaluating their relationships","Describe an additional exhibit problem that the argument has not addressed"],"answer":0,"why":"The conclusion should bring the developed reasoning together. A neutral list or new undeveloped problem does not complete the recommendation.","skill":"Develop and revise a complex argument","id":"L46-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new essay gives an example but no explanation. Which question best guides revision?","choices":["Can I assume readers already agree with every assertion?","How can I add words without adding meaning?","How does this example support my specific claim?","Can I remove every alternative perspective?"],"answer":2,"why":"The revision question focuses on reasoning connecting evidence to a claim.","skill":"Develop and revise a complex argument","id":"L46-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H02","H10","H27"],"visual":null,"writing":{"issue":"How should a public exhibit teach freedom and citizenship from the Civil War forward?","perspectives":["Lead with a clear central narrative so visitors gain an accessible foundation.","Organize contrasting sources so visitors evaluate evidence and competing interpretations.","Connect historical choices to present questions while explaining differences in context."],"minWords":160,"directions":"Develop your own position and explain its relationship to at least one other perspective. Use a specific example and explain how it supports your reasoning. These are original practice prompts.","rubric":["Ideas and analysis","Development and support","Organization","Language use and conventions"]},"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L47","number":47,"title":"Selecting a mathematical method","period":"1869–2000","section":"Mathematics","skill":"Solve mixed problems with multiple steps","era":6,"stage":"Skill lab","minutes":25,"story":["A community history center is preparing displays about railroads, public engineering, and changing technology. Its planning team must work with budgets, models, space, and visitor data. The situations in this laboratory are invented. Each asks you to select a mathematical method instead of following a lesson heading that names the operation.","Start by translating the question. What is known? What is requested? Which units and restrictions matter? A linear cost model, a circular floor plan, and a probability question require different relationships. Choosing a relevant method is part of the task, even before the calculation begins.","After solving, return to the situation. A fraction may need conversion to a percentage. A square root may have a restriction. A discounted price may still require a fixed fee. A numerically correct intermediate value is not necessarily the final answer."],"steps":["List the known quantities and the requested quantity.","Select and write a relationship that connects them.","Calculate carefully, preserving units and restrictions.","Check that the answer completes the entire question."],"worked":{"prompt":"An invented printing order costs $80 plus $3 per panel. A $200 budget covers how many panels?","steps":["Write 80 + 3p ≤ 200.","Subtract 80 and divide by 3: p ≤ 40.","At most 40 panels fit the budget; the fixed fee is included."]},"guided":{"stem":"A model budget is $500. A purchase costs $240 after a 20% discount. What was its original price?","choices":["$260","$300","$192","$288"],"answer":1,"why":"The sale price is 80% of the original: 240/0.80 = 300. The budget amount is not needed.","skill":"Solve mixed problems with multiple steps","id":"L47-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"A circular display has radius 3 m. What is its circumference?","choices":["9π m","6π m","18π m","3π m"],"answer":1,"why":"Circumference = 2πr = 6π meters. Area would have square units.","skill":"Solve mixed problems with multiple steps","id":"L47-questions-1"},{"stem":"For a model y = 4x + 12, which x produces y = 40?","choices":["28","13","10","7"],"answer":3,"why":"40 = 4x + 12, so 28 = 4x and x = 7.","skill":"Solve mixed problems with multiple steps","id":"L47-questions-2"},{"stem":"Solve √(x + 5) = 4.","choices":["9","21","−1","11"],"answer":3,"why":"Square both sides: x + 5 = 16. Thus x = 11; substitution verifies √16 = 4.","skill":"Solve mixed problems with multiple steps","id":"L47-questions-3"},{"stem":"Five model attendance counts are 8, 10, 10, 12, and 20. What is their median?","choices":["12","20","60","10"],"answer":3,"why":"The middle of the five ordered values is 10. The mean is 12, which is a different statistic.","skill":"Solve mixed problems with multiple steps","id":"L47-questions-4"},{"stem":"A practice box has 4 red and 6 blue tokens. Two are drawn without replacement. What is P(both red)?","choices":["4/25","2/15","1/5","2/5"],"answer":1,"why":"Multiply 4/10 by 3/9 = 12/90 = 2/15; the second denominator changes after the first draw.","skill":"Solve mixed problems with multiple steps","id":"L47-questions-5"},{"stem":"A function is defined by f(x) = 2x for x < 3 and f(x) = x + 5 for x ≥ 3. Find f(3).","choices":["6","11","8","3"],"answer":2,"why":"The boundary value 3 belongs to the second rule, so f(3) = 3 + 5 = 8.","skill":"Solve mixed problems with multiple steps","id":"L47-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new printing order costs $60 plus $4 per panel. With $180, how many panels are affordable?","choices":["30","15","45","60"],"answer":0,"why":"Subtract the fixed fee: 180 − 60 = 120. Divide by 4 to get 30 panels.","skill":"Solve mixed problems with multiple steps","id":"L47-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H18","H13"],"visual":null,"writing":null,"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."},{"id":"L48","number":48,"title":"The independent historian’s challenge","period":"1861–2020","section":"Mixed","skill":"Transfer skills and evaluate readiness","era":6,"stage":"Skill lab","minutes":25,"story":["The final extension asks you to review a proposed exhibit about citizenship and change. The student curators have assembled sources from the Civil War, Reconstruction, industrial growth, civil rights, and technological change. They must decide which claims the evidence supports and how to communicate those claims clearly.","Their project also includes invented practical models: a printing budget, a visitor survey, and a test of label readability. A historical document, a numerical model, and an experimental result each require a different kind of interpretation. Treating them as interchangeable would weaken the exhibit.","Readiness means selecting a method when the prompt does not announce it, explaining the choice, and noticing the limits of a conclusion. This small set provides one opportunity to practice that transfer. It cannot represent the full range, length, or difficulty of an official ACT administration.","After the course, use new official practice material with your teacher to identify remaining needs. Keep first attempts separate from repeated work. Continue targeted study and spaced review, and judge progress with more than one source of evidence."],"steps":["Identify the task before selecting a strategy.","Use the relevant text, equation, or study design.","Distinguish a supported conclusion from a tempting overstatement.","Review new official work to decide the next instructional step."],"worked":{"prompt":"A source names one excluded group. Can a label say every group was excluded?","steps":["The proposed label enlarges the source’s claim.","Return to the actual group and the stated context.","A defensible label keeps the claim within the evidence."]},"guided":{"stem":"Which practice best supports the exhibit’s historical accuracy?","choices":["Explain each source’s context before connecting it to others.","Assume every source was created for the same audience.","Use invented visitor data as an official historical census.","Treat a modern interpretation as a verbatim nineteenth-century document."],"answer":0,"why":"Context and clear source types support a connection without misrepresenting the evidence.","skill":"Transfer skills and evaluate readiness","id":"L48-guided"},"questions":[{"stem":"Choose the correct sentence.","choices":["The sources, each created for a different audience, requires context.","The sources, each created for a different audience, require context.","The sources, each created for a different audience require context.","The sources each created for a different audience requiring context."],"answer":1,"why":"The plural subject sources takes require. The intervening nonessential phrase is set off with paired commas.","skill":"Transfer skills and evaluate readiness","id":"L48-questions-1"},{"stem":"Printing costs $90 plus $2.50 per panel. What is the total for 24 panels?","choices":["$114","$150","$225","$60"],"answer":1,"why":"24 × 2.50 = 60, then add the fixed 90: total 150.","skill":"Transfer skills and evaluate readiness","id":"L48-questions-2"},{"stem":"A readability study assigns visitors randomly to two label versions under the same conditions. Why is this useful?","choices":["It proves every possible visitor will respond identically.","It ensures that the longer label must perform better.","It makes all measurements and descriptions unnecessary.","It helps reduce systematic differences between the comparison groups."],"answer":3,"why":"Random assignment can reduce systematic group differences; it does not guarantee identical individuals or outcomes.","skill":"Transfer skills and evaluate readiness","id":"L48-questions-3"},{"stem":"An argument recommends short labels but ignores a concern about missing context. What revision would strengthen it?","choices":["State that no reader can ever need further explanation.","Add an unrelated statistic with no source or relevance.","Address how concise labels can provide essential context.","Describe the opposing view as foolish without explanation."],"answer":2,"why":"A response to the relevant concern improves the argument’s analysis and practical recommendation.","skill":"Transfer skills and evaluate readiness","id":"L48-questions-4"},{"stem":"A survey of 20 volunteer club members favors one exhibit. Which conclusion is defensible?","choices":["The sample represents every visitor to every museum.","Most surveyed club members favored it.","The exhibit caused all participants to learn more.","Every student in the school favors it."],"answer":1,"why":"The limited sample supports a description of those respondents, not universal preference or causal learning effects.","skill":"Transfer skills and evaluate readiness","id":"L48-questions-5"},{"stem":"What provides the strongest next evidence of ACT readiness?","choices":["New official practice completed under appropriate conditions and reviewed by skill","Repeating one familiar question until its answer is memorized","Assuming one short local set samples every possible question","Counting a certificate as a guaranteed national test score"],"answer":0,"why":"New official practice broadens the evidence beyond familiar short sets. Skill analysis helps guide continued study.","skill":"Transfer skills and evaluate readiness","id":"L48-questions-6"}],"review":[{"stem":"A new model uses $50 plus $3 per panel. What is the cost for 18 panels?","choices":["$150","$68","$54","$104"],"answer":3,"why":"18 × 3 = 54. Add the fixed 50 to get 104.","skill":"Transfer skills and evaluate readiness","id":"L48-review-1"}],"sourceIds":["H01","H02","H10"],"visual":null,"writing":{"issue":"What should count as convincing evidence that a public history project is successful?","perspectives":["Clear, accurate explanations should be the primary measure.","Visitors’ ability to evaluate sources should be the primary measure.","Multiple measures should include understanding, access, and the limits of available evidence."],"minWords":160,"directions":"Develop your own position and explain its relationship to at least one other perspective. Use a specific example and explain how it supports your reasoning. These are original practice prompts.","rubric":["Ideas and analysis","Development and support","Organization","Language use and conventions"]},"es":"Lee la pregunta primero. Identifica la habilidad. Sigue el ejemplo y después intenta un problema nuevo sin ayuda. Explica qué evidencia o procedimiento apoya tu respuesta."}],"eras":[{"id":0,"title":"Civil War and Reconstruction","dates":"1861–1877","summary":"The Civil War began as a struggle over secession and the future of a republic shaped by slavery. Enslaved people pursued freedom while Union policy changed. The Emancipation Proclamation widened the war's purpose but did not abolish slavery nationwide. Constitutional amendments after the war established broader legal protections, while resistance and uneven enforcement made the meaning of freedom and citizenship an ongoing struggle."},{"id":1,"title":"Industry Immigration and Reform","dates":"1877–1917","summary":"Industrial growth changed work, transportation, and urban life. The Pullman conflict showed how a dispute between a company and its employees could become a national confrontation involving unions, railroad owners, and federal power. Settlement workers such as Jane Addams and Ellen Gates Starr responded to crowded urban conditions through neighborhood services and reform campaigns. Their work connected everyday needs with larger arguments over opportunity and responsibility."},{"id":2,"title":"World War I Migration and Depression","dates":"1917–1941","summary":"Wartime jobs helped draw African Americans toward industrial cities, while racial violence and unequal opportunity pushed families away from the South. Migration reshaped northern communities, culture, and politics. The Great Depression later exposed weaknesses in employment and banking. The Emergency Banking Act of 1933 sought to restore trust by examining banks and reopening those judged financially secure, demonstrating how public confidence could shape economic recovery."},{"id":3,"title":"World War II and Cold War","dates":"1941–1969","summary":"World War II required production, supply networks, scientific work, and difficult military operations. The Aleutian campaign showed how weather and geography shaped decisions in a distant theater. At home, the incarceration of Japanese Americans exposed the failure to protect civil liberties. After the war, the Marshall Plan connected European economic recovery with American security goals and the political tensions of the emerging Cold War."},{"id":4,"title":"Rights Space and Environmental Change","dates":"1964–2000","summary":"Civil rights organizing pressed the federal government to make equality enforceable. The Civil Rights Act of 1964 addressed discrimination in public accommodations and employment. At the same time, the space program made scientific and engineering decisions visible to a mass audience, culminating in Apollo 11. Environmental concerns also produced new federal institutions, including EPA in 1970. Across these changes, evidence, public pressure, and policy interacted."},{"id":5,"title":"A Changing Nation","dates":"2001–2020","summary":"New computer networks changed how people shared information and how institutions organized work. Government-supported research networks helped establish the infrastructure that commercial providers later expanded. Economic changes also created new risks. The Great Recession began in December 2007 and officially ended in June 2009, but its effects lasted beyond those dates. Understanding this period requires separating measured trends from sweeping claims about technology, prosperity, or recovery."}],"sources":[{"id":"H01","title":"Emancipation Proclamation 1863","url":"https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/emancipation-proclamation","era":1,"ref":"turn21view0","facts":["Lincoln issued the final proclamation on January 1, 1863.","It applied to designated areas in rebellion and left slavery untouched in loyal border states.","It accepted Black men into Union military service and linked emancipation to Union military success."]},{"id":"H02","title":"Fourteenth Amendment 1868","url":"https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/14th-amendment","era":1,"ref":"turn21view1","facts":["The Fourteenth Amendment was ratified in 1868.","It established national citizenship and protections involving due process and equal protection.","Its legal promises faced resistance and uneven enforcement after Reconstruction."]},{"id":"H03","title":"The Strike of 1894","url":"https://www.nps.gov/pull/learn/historyculture/the-strike-of-1894.htm","era":2,"ref":"turn21view2","facts":["The strike at the Pullman factories began on May 11, 1894.","The American Railway Union's boycott of Pullman cars disrupted railroad traffic nationally.","Federal injunctions and troops contributed to the union's defeat."]},{"id":"H04","title":"Jane Addams","url":"https://www.nps.gov/people/jane-addams.htm","era":2,"ref":"turn21view3","facts":["Jane Addams and Ellen Gates Starr founded Hull House in Chicago in 1889.","Hull House provided services and supported reforms involving labor, children, and urban conditions.","Addams became a suffrage and peace advocate and shared the 1931 Nobel Peace Prize."]},{"id":"H05","title":"The Great Migration","url":"https://www.loc.gov/classroom-materials/great-migration","era":3,"ref":"turn17search1","facts":["From the 1910s through 1970, millions of African Americans left the South for other regions.","Industrial employment during World War I contributed to migration.","The migration reshaped urban communities, civil rights organizations, politics, and culture."],"access_note":"First-party indexed text retrieved; direct open with trailing slash returned 403."},{"id":"H06","title":"Emergency Banking Act of 1933","url":"https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/emergency-banking-act-of-1933","era":3,"ref":"turn21view5","facts":["Roosevelt signed the Emergency Banking Act on March 9, 1933.","The law followed a national bank holiday.","Examination and reopening of financially secure banks were intended to rebuild confidence."]},{"id":"H07","title":"Executive Order 9066","url":"https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/executive-order-9066","era":4,"ref":"turn22view1","facts":["Roosevelt issued Executive Order 9066 on February 19, 1942.","The order authorized military exclusion; its implementation led to forced removal and incarceration of Japanese Americans.","Many people incarcerated were U.S. citizens and were not individually charged."]},{"id":"H08","title":"The Aleutian Campaign","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/wwii-nhl-aleu-campaign.htm","era":4,"ref":"turn22view2","facts":["Japanese aircraft attacked Dutch Harbor on June 3 and 4, 1942.","The campaign involved Attu and Kiska and difficult weather in 1942–1943.","Japanese forces evacuated Kiska before the August 1943 American and Canadian landing."]},{"id":"H09","title":"Marshall Plan 1948","url":"https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/marshall-plan","era":4,"ref":"turn22view3","facts":["Truman signed the act establishing the Marshall Plan on April 3, 1948.","The plan provided aid to help rebuild European economies.","Recovery goals were connected to trade, political stability, and American security concerns."]},{"id":"H10","title":"Civil Rights Act 1964","url":"https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/civil-rights-act","era":5,"ref":"turn22view4","facts":["Johnson signed the Civil Rights Act on July 2, 1964.","The law prohibited discrimination in public accommodations and employment.","Its passage followed civil rights activism and substantial congressional conflict."]},{"id":"H11","title":"Apollo 11 Mission Overview","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/missions/apollo/apollo-11/apollo-11-mission-overview/","era":5,"ref":"turn22view5","facts":["Apollo 11 launched on July 16, 1969.","Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin landed on the Moon while Michael Collins remained in lunar orbit.","The mission's goal included a crewed landing and return, scientific exploration, and sample collection."]},{"id":"H12","title":"The Origins of EPA","url":"https://www.epa.gov/history/origins-epa","era":5,"ref":"turn23view2","facts":["EPA treats December 2, 1970, as its institutional birthday.","A reorganization consolidated several federal environmental responsibilities.","Research, monitoring, and enforcement were included in the new agency's responsibilities."]},{"id":"H13","title":"Birth of the Commercial Internet","url":"https://www.nsf.gov/impacts/internet","era":6,"ref":"turn23view1","facts":["ARPANET began with four computer nodes in late 1969.","NSF launched NSFNET in 1986 to connect researchers and supercomputer centers.","These publicly supported networks helped establish infrastructure for later commercial internet growth."]},{"id":"H14","title":"The Great Recession","url":"https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/great-recession-of-200709","era":6,"ref":"turn23view0","facts":["The Great Recession lasted from December 2007 to June 2009.","It was the longest U.S. recession since World War II.","Employment and housing conditions worsened substantially, and policy responses included fiscal programs and Federal Reserve actions."]},{"id":"H15","title":"Nineteenth Amendment","url":"https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/19th-amendment","era":2,"ref":"turn26search1","facts":["Congress passed the amendment in 1919 and ratification occurred in August 1920.","The amendment barred denial of voting rights on account of sex.","Other legal and practical barriers to voting remained for many women."]},{"id":"H16","title":"Fifteenth Amendment","url":"https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/15th-amendment","era":1,"ref":"turn17search18","facts":["The Fifteenth Amendment was ratified in 1870.","It barred denying voting rights on account of race, color, or previous servitude.","It did not prevent later disenfranchisement through discriminatory practices and violence."]},{"id":"H17","title":"The Transcontinental Telegraph","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/the-transcontinental-telegraph.htm","era":1,"ref":"turn37view0","facts":["The first transcontinental telegraph was completed on October 24, 1861.","The line accelerated long-distance communication and carried news of Civil War events."]},{"id":"H18","title":"A Moment in Time Andrew J Russell","url":"https://www.nps.gov/gosp/learn/historyculture/a-moment-in-time.htm","era":2,"ref":"turn41view1","facts":["The Golden Spike Ceremony took place at Promontory Summit on May 10, 1869.","Andrew J. Russell photographed the meeting of Union Pacific and Central Pacific locomotives and railroad workers."]},{"id":"H19","title":"A History of Protecting Our Water Environment","url":"https://www.mwrd.org/what-we-do/history-protecting-our-water-environment","era":2,"ref":"turn37view2","facts":["The Sanitary District of Chicago was established in 1889.","Construction of the Chicago Sanitary and Ship Canal reversed the Chicago River in 1900, sending wastewater away from Lake Michigan."]},{"id":"H20","title":"The 1906 Food and Drugs Act and Its Enforcement","url":"https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/changes-science-law-and-regulatory-authorities/part-i-1906-food-and-drugs-act-and-its-enforcement","era":2,"ref":"turn37view3","facts":["Muckraking journalism and Upton Sinclair’s The Jungle helped build support for food regulation.","Roosevelt signed the Food and Drugs Act on June 30, 1906; its provisions addressed adulteration and misleading labels in interstate commerce."]},{"id":"H21","title":"I want you for U S Army","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/96507165/","era":3,"ref":"turn40search10","facts":["The First World War recruiting poster depicts Uncle Sam pointing toward the viewer.","James Montgomery Flagg used the image to encourage enlistment in the U.S. Army."],"access_note":"First-party indexed source text verified; direct page access returned an error during retrieval."},{"id":"H22","title":"History of 1918 Flu Pandemic","url":"https://archive.cdc.gov/www_cdc_gov/flu/pandemic-resources/1918-commemoration/1918-pandemic-history.htm","era":3,"ref":"turn37view5","facts":["The 1918 influenza pandemic spread worldwide in 1918 and 1919.","Public responses included isolation, quarantine, hygiene measures, and restrictions on gatherings; their application varied."]},{"id":"H23","title":"A New African American Identity The Harlem Renaissance","url":"https://nmaahc.si.edu/explore/stories/new-african-american-identity-harlem-renaissance","era":3,"ref":"turn33search4","facts":["The Harlem Renaissance included literature, visual arts, music, theater, and dance.","Alain Locke was an influential writer and critic associated with the movement’s assertion of Black cultural pride."],"access_note":"First-party indexed source text verified; direct page access returned an error during retrieval."},{"id":"H24","title":"Civilian Conservation Corps","url":"https://www.nps.gov/thro/learn/historyculture/civilian-conservation-corps.htm","era":3,"ref":"turn38view1","facts":["Congress established the Civilian Conservation Corps on March 31, 1933.","The program employed young men in conservation, flood control, forest management, and park development projects during the Great Depression."]},{"id":"H25","title":"Sacrificing for the Common Good Rationing in World War II","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/rationing-in-wwii.htm","era":4,"ref":"turn38view2","facts":["The United States used rationing to limit civilian purchases of selected scarce goods during World War II.","War demands and disruptions to international trade contributed to shortages; goods rationed included gasoline, sugar, and rubber products."]},{"id":"H26","title":"Prelude to McCarthyism The Making of a Blacklist","url":"https://www.archives.gov/publications/prologue/2006/fall/agloso.html","era":4,"ref":"turn38view3","facts":["Truman established a federal employee loyalty screening program in 1947.","The public list of supposedly subversive organizations was used beyond federal employment and could damage people through alleged associations."]},{"id":"H27","title":"Voting Rights Act 1965","url":"https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/voting-rights-act","era":5,"ref":"turn38view4","facts":["Johnson signed the Voting Rights Act on August 6, 1965.","The act addressed discriminatory voting barriers, including literacy tests, and authorized federal examiners in covered jurisdictions."]},{"id":"H28","title":"Medicare and Medicaid Act 1965","url":"https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/medicare-and-medicaid-act","era":5,"ref":"turn38view5","facts":["Johnson signed the Social Security Amendments of 1965 on July 30, 1965.","The legislation established Medicare for older Americans and Medicaid for people with limited income."]},{"id":"H29","title":"Watergate Break in Fiftieth Anniversary","url":"https://www.nixonlibrary.gov/news/watergate-break-50th-anniversary","era":5,"ref":"turn39view0","facts":["The June 17, 1972 burglary targeted Democratic National Committee headquarters at the Watergate complex.","Security guard Frank Wills reported suspicious door-latch tape; the investigation reached the highest levels of Nixon’s administration."]},{"id":"H30","title":"Competition and the Decline of the Rust Belt","url":"https://www.minneapolisfed.org/article/2014/competition-and-the-decline-of-the-rust-belt","era":6,"ref":"turn39view1","facts":["The Rust Belt’s share of U.S. employment and manufacturing jobs declined over decades beginning before the late 1970s.","The authors analyze competition and innovation as an explanation for the region’s manufacturing decline."],"interpretation_note":"The competition mechanism is the cited authors’ research interpretation, not a settled single-cause explanation."},{"id":"H31","title":"9 11 Commission Records","url":"https://www.archives.gov/research/9-11","era":6,"ref":"turn39view2","facts":["Congress created the independent bipartisan 9/11 Commission to investigate the September 11, 2001 attacks and recommend prevention measures.","During 2003 and 2004 the commission held hearings, interviewed witnesses, and produced a report; its records are preserved by the National Archives."]},{"id":"H32","title":"Evidence NASA Climate Change","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/climate-change/evidence/","era":6,"ref":"turn39view3","facts":["Evidence for climate change comes from natural records such as ice cores and tree rings as well as modern instruments and satellites.","NASA identifies human greenhouse gas emissions since the mid-nineteenth century as the principal cause of current warming."]},{"id":"H33","title":"Illinois and the 19th Amendment","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/illinois-and-the-19th-amendment.htm","facts":["Illinois granted women limited voting rights in 1913; the Nineteenth Amendment followed in 1920."],"era":1}],"hero":{"title":"Civil rights March on Washington DC","year":1963,"date":"1963-08-28","creator":"Warren K. Leffler","image_url":"https://cdn.loc.gov/service/pnp/ppmsca/04200/04296v.jpg","item_url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2003688163/","resource_url":"https://www.loc.gov/resource/ppmsca.04296/","local_path":"/workspace/scratch/fbe908a8bce2/act-history-site/dist/assets/march-on-washington.jpg","attribution":"Warren K. Leffler, Civil rights March on Washington, D.C., August 28, 1963. Library of Congress, Prints and Photographs Division, U.S. News & World Report Magazine Photograph Collection. LC-DIG-ppmsca-04296.","rights":"No known restrictions on publication.","source_ref":"turn28search1","metadata_ref":"turn28search0","dimensions":[835,1024],"note":"Actual JPEG downloaded successfully and visually inspected. Retain original creator and LOC rights advisory. No assertion of government authorship; this was a private magazine photograph."},"secondImage":null};
